    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/3605-de-la-Montagne/Communications-Officer_JR0000080015-1": {
      "title": "Communications Officer",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-03\nPosted: Posted 11 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Position Summary: Reporting to the Director of Communications, Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences, and working in close collaboration with the Director, Administration and Operations, Medical Education, the Communications Officer develops communications materials to inform and engage a variety of audiences, with a primary focus on students, faculty and staff within the School of Medicine at the Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences. The Officer provides expert advice and support to internal clients, in particular those in the undergraduate medical education and postgraduate medical education programs in the School of Medicine. Primary Responsibilities: Develop in-depth knowledge of internal clients’ goals, structures and operational challenges; proactively advise on communications, campaigns and channels to meet client needs and to advance University and Faculty goals. Collaborate in the development of communications strategies consistent with University and Faculty goals and priorities; create and implement communications rollouts. Translate complex information, including policies, into engaging and accurate content for students and faculty, through a variety of internal communications channels. Create content across multiple channels, working with Faculty communications staff and clients as needed, to strengthen community and belonging within the School of Medicine, including at Campus Outaouais and across the McGill clinical teaching network. Support key events and collaborate with the School of Medicine and Faculty’s Events officers on large-scale events. Ensure clients’ web presence reflects best practices and latest McGill functionalities; establish and implement standard operating procedures to update key websites, particularly within the School of Medicine, ensuring that content is current and optimized. Liaise with School of Medicine leadership, internal clients, and student leadership, as well as with partners in the Faculty’s network of teaching hospitals and institutes, to monitor audience needs and issues, solicit feedback and collaborate on joint communication initiatives where appropriate. Develop and implement best practices and standard processes to support clients’ communication needs, such as style guides and templates; where appropriate, coach non-communicators developing content to ensure consistency and quality control. Remain current with best practices in communications, particularly internal communications, e-newsletters and digital content; share best practices with other Faculty communicators.    Monitor the effectiveness of communications with key performance indicators; adapt strategies and materials as needed according to best practices. Support special Faculty projects, as needed. Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities Undergraduate degree in communications or related field. Three (3) years' related experience, or more. Thrives in a complex organizational environment with multiple clients. Ability to anticipate possible difficulties, troubleshoot and implement solutions. Outstanding writing, editing and proofreading skills. Fully versed in planning and coordinating communications projects across channels. Strong organizational, priority-setting, problem-solving and listening. Ability to build relationships with clients / colleagues and to work autonomously and as part of a team in a fast-paced, changing, deadline-oriented environment. Demonstrated professionalism and discretion. Proven ability to work with computer applications and tools related to the field of communications (Microsoft Office, web, social media, etc.), as well as databases and presentation software. English and French, spoken and written is required. Experience working in communications in a medical school and/or healthcare environment a strong asset. As one of Montreal's Top Employers, here is what we offer: Competitive benefits package (Health, Dental, Life Insurance) (if eligible) Defined contribution pension plan (with employer contribution up to 10%) (if eligible) Group Registered Retirement Savings Plan (RRSP) and Tax Free Savings Account (TFSA) Competitive vacation policy Two (2) personal days Two (2) floating holidays Nine (9) \"Summer Fridays\" - paid days off between the St-Jean Baptiste holiday and Labour Day Paid time off over the December holiday period Tuition waiver for regular employees and their dependents Up to two (2) days of remote work per week where the position permits Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level # 4 on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: Bachelor's Degree 3 Years Related Experience / Annual Salary: (MPEX Grade 04) $66,190.00 - $82,740.00 - $99,290.00 Job Profile: MPEX-COM2A - Internal External Relations - Professional 2 Hours per Week: 33.75 (Full time) Supervisor: Director Communications Position End Date (If applicable): Deadline to Apply: 2026-09-15 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/James-Admin-Bldg/Safety-Abroad-Officer_JR0000080063": {
      "title": "Safety Abroad Officer",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-03\nPosted: Posted 11 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Position Summary: Reporting to the Associate Director, International Education, the Safety Abroad Officer is responsible for the development, implementation and monitoring of international safety education, training, protocols and communications. They will manage activities related to international travel with a focus on risk management for McGill student travel for University-sanctioned activities. The incumbent will manage the McGill Student Travel registry app, the Safety Abroad webpage and McGill’s Learning Management Course platform for training. The incumbent will be responsible for developing and maintaining standard operating processes and procedures, implementing quality management practices and continuous improvements to ensure efficient and effective use of the systems. They will also be responsible for compiling and reviewing Faculty safety plans for field courses travel. This role leads data analysis and prepares data requests. The incumbent will establish travel safety preparedness information and orientation sessions (i.e. Regional Information and Networking sessions) and serve as an advisor to students, faculty, staff and administration on matters related to student travel risk and security. Primary Responsibilities: Training and Education Develops, delivers, and oversees international travel safety education and training for students studying abroad. Acts as a University-wide resource related to safety abroad and provides expert advice on global safety and security to University leadership, committees, faculty, staff and students. Provides expertise and support to ensure programs, activities and actions are conducted according to the University’s travel safety requirements. Remains informed on current standards and best practices through participation in external communities of practice. Identifies opportunities to inform Faculties and other units about safety abroad requirements and procedures. Develops, delivers and monitors safety abroad information, training and outreach. Manages some traveler tracking and travel safety compliance. Technical systems support and management Acts as the key internal resource and subject matter expert for system workflows and troubleshooting systems. Oversees accuracy of international travel data in app. Maintains effective communication with external service providers. Protocols and Process Provides support in the design, implementation, and evaluation of risk management and safety abroad protocols, procedures, and programs. Promotes the development and consistent implementation of processes to support safety abroad for students. Utilizes a continuous improvement approach to ensure best and promising practices are implemented in incident management, documentation, and systems approaches. Emergency Response Reviews incident alerts and initiates timely communications with students, staff, faculty, partners, and other stakeholders regarding events or incidents that may pose safety concerns, in accordance with established Standard Operating Procedures. Oversees the administrative procedure for reviewing travel requests to areas of elevated risk (travel exemptions). Manages emergency response and crisis management support activities. Makes recommendations on active cases and presents related documentation/case analysis. Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities Successful completion of a post-secondary degree in a related field and a minimum of 4 years of experience in a related safety/study abroad role in a post-secondary environment. Experience with intercultural and international issues required. Knowledge and ability to design and facilitate information sessions and risk assessments to diverse audiences. Experience working effectively with diverse communities of students and promoting inclusion; strong interpersonal skills and intercultural sensitivity. Ability to handle emergency situations and effectively prioritize and execute tasks. Knowledge of current best practices pertaining to international activities. Experience working with university policy and regulations governing study abroad; risk management. Exceptional verbal and written communication skills (English and French). Critical attention to detail. Strong time management and organizational skills Analytical, research and problem-solving skills Demonstrated experience working in a decentralized environment with diverse stakeholders including senior management. Ability to handle sensitive and confidential matters with discretion, tact, sound judgment, integrity and diplomacy. As one of Montreal's Top Employers, here is what we offer: Competitive benefits package (Health, Dental, Life Insurance) (if eligible) Defined contribution pension plan (with employer contribution up to 10%) (if eligible) Group Registered Retirement Savings Plan (RRSP) and Tax Free Savings Account (TFSA) Competitive vacation policy Two (2) personal days Two (2) floating holidays Nine (9) \"Summer Fridays\" - paid days off between the St-Jean Baptiste holiday and Labour Day Paid time off over the December holiday period Tuition waiver for regular employees and their dependents Up to two (2) days of remote work per week where the position permits Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level # 4 on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: Bachelor's Degree 3 Years Related Experience / Annual Salary: (MPEX Grade 04) $66,190.00 - $82,740.00 - $99,290.00 Job Profile: MPEX-ADM2A - General Administration - Professional 2 Hours per Week: 33.75 (Full time) Supervisor: Associate Director, International Education Position End Date (If applicable): Deadline to Apply: 2026-09-14 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Education-Building/Senior-Administrative-Coordinator_JR0000080067": {
      "title": "Senior Administrative Coordinator",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-03\nPosted: Posted 11 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Position Summary: Under the direction of the immediate supervisor, the incumbent oversees the day-to-day operations of the buildings and implements changes to improve efficiencies.  May organize, distribute and verify work. Provides comprehensive support to the organization of events and initiatives. Liaises with internal and external stakeholders. Major Duties and Responsibilities: Act as frontline resource, provide front-desk coverage and serve as the point of contact for building-related matters. Monitor the Faculty-wide service inbox, ensuring each inquiry is acknowledged, actioned or escalated, and followed through with the appropriate services or contractors, including on-site coordination, until the work and correspondence is followed through to resolution. Oversee daily operations of the buildings, set requests and space-related priorities and schedules, implement and improve procedures, and follow up on decisions. Serve as a resource on policies, resolve complex issues, and create process documentation. Coordinate high volume correspondence and follow ups on time sensitive requests involving multiple stakeholders. Identifies and analyses discrepancies and errors. Corrects errors and follows up on problems. Serve as the primary liaison with FMAS for cleaning, maintenance, repairs, shutdowns, and renovations. Communicate building policies, maintain occupancy records, manage key and card access (including after-hours access), and support emergency preparedness, fire drills, inspections, and safety communications. Work with Space Management Coordinators to track space usage, conduct space verifications, update space inventory data, and report changes. Coordinate temporary space assignments for events in alignment with building policies. Respond to inquiries from callers and visitors, provide information, and direct them to appropriate contacts. The list of duties and responsibilities outlined above is representative and not a complete and detailed list of tasks which may be performed by an employee. Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities: Demonstrated experience with space and building coordination. Proven ability to respond calmly and constructively to complaints, dissatisfaction, and challenging interactions while maintaining perspective and professionalism. Ability to triage and respond to requests promptly, including identifying when guidance is required and proactively escalating matters rather than allowing them to remain unanswered. Proven ability to work autonomously and as a member of a team, coordinate multiple and or overlapping tasks, shifting priorities, and address urgent requests effectively. Proven organizational, prioritization, skills, with the ability to stay focused, aligned with team members, and adaptable to rapid changes. Demonstrated capacity to provide responsive customer service and be solution-oriented in a high-pressure environment.  Maintains professionalism when navigating complex and sensitive situations. Demonstrated accuracy and attention to detail. Proven ability to coordinate arrangements for events and meetings. Demonstrated communication skills and a proven ability to transmit and receive information accurately both in writing and verbally. Demonstrated discretion in dealing with confidential information. Proficient in a PC environment using specialized databases, word processing, spreadsheets, presentation software, email and the Internet. Knowledge of French and English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level #4 on a scale of 0-4. Additional Information: The incumbent will have to work onsite 5 days a week. Minimum Education and Experience: DEP - Office Systems 4 Years Related Experience / DEP - Secretarial Studies Hourly Salary: (MUNACA Level G) $31.19 - $38.65 Hours per Week: 33.75 (Full time) Supervisor: Administrative Supervisor Position End Date (If applicable): Deadline to Apply: 2026-09-14 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
