    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Burnside-Hall/Invigilator---MATH-263--F26-_JR0000080643": {
      "title": "Invigilator – MATH 263 (F26)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-17\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit : Department of Mathematics and Statistics Exam Period : • MATH 263 - October 15th 2026 (7 :05pm-8:25pm plus setup and takedown) Position Summary : • Responsible for setting up the examination room according to specific requirements as well as ensuring that equipment and facilities are functioning properly. • Administers the delivery of the exams and documentation. • Handles students' problems. • Ensures that students comply with regulations with respect to exams. • Alerts professor when students have a question. • At the end of the examination period, collects all exam materials and ensures nothing is left behind in the examination room(s). • Responsible for reporting back to the professor and ensure delivery of the exams. Qualifications : • Must be client-focused and service oriented. • Punctual. • Demonstrated excellent communication and organizational skills, and an ability to transmit and receive information accurately. • Demonstrates ability to listen and efficiently assist clients with problems. • Ability to follow instructions and to work independently and as part of a team. • Ability to act in an acceptable manner so as not to disrupt students writing the exams. • English, spoken and read. • French an asset. Experience and education : • High-School Diploma is the minimum requirement • Priority will be given to candidates who are already in McGill's priority pool Location: This position is located in Montreal, Quebec, and the work for this position is expected to be performed in Quebec. Salary : • $18.00/hour 11.02 – “An Employee that holds more than one position at the University must inform their Supervisor in writing, on the application form or during the course of the Employee’s appointment, of any other assignments presently held at the University.” Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Hourly Salary: (AGSEM Invigilator) $18.00 Deadline to Apply: 2026-09-25 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/McLennan-Library/Invigilator_JR0000080737": {
      "title": "Invigilator",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-17\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Position Summary: Under the guidance and supervision of the Chief Invigilator and the Exam Deputy, the primary responsibility of invigilators is to supervise exams, enforce the University Exam Regulations, and provide an environment which will allow each student to perform to the best of their abilities on their exam. Invigilators are expected to ensure that there are no distractions or disruptions in the area where students are writing their exams. Invigilators must pay constant attention to students under their surveillance, to ensure that cheating does not take place. Invigilators are responsible and accountable for all exams and exam materials in their possession. Please note that this application is a two-step process. You need to complete the following form to complete your application:   https://www.mcgill.ca/exams/invigilator-application-webform Hiring Unit: Enrolment Services. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Hourly Salary: (AGSEM Invigilator) $18.00 Deadline to Apply: 2026-09-25 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/MUHC-GLEN/Assistant-Professor--Clinical---Department-of-Medicine--Division-of-Physical-Medicine-and-Rehabilitation--Palliative-Care--C2-260911-_JR0000080615": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor (Clinical), Department of Medicine, Division of Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation, Palliative Care (C2-260911)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-17\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\n​ Please refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Position Description The McGill University Department of Medicine in the Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences invites applications for a full-time position as a member of the Contract Academic Staff as a Clinician-Teacher or Clinician-Educator at the rank of Assistant Professor (Clinical) based at the McGill University Health Centre (MUHC). This is a Contract Academic Staff position and does not confer eligibility for tenure. This position is conditional on the Institution successfully obtaining a PEM (i.e. physician permit) for this position. The Department of Medicine is a leader in academic medicine. Our educational and research missions support excellent and innovative clinical programs across our teaching hospitals. The McGill University Health Centre is one of McGill’s major teaching hospitals affiliated with the Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences at Canada’s internationally renowned McGill University, located in the vibrant and multicultural city of Montreal, in the province of Quebec. The Division of Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation is highly committed to the pursuit of excellence in clinical care, research, and medical education. As a relatively small division, candidates will be expected to demonstrate an appreciation for the breadth of physiatry and have a vision for how to grow it while demonstrating an ability to work as part of a team.   Job Duties Please see the Department of Medicine (DOM) website for McGill DOM Career path definitions . Our career path definitions specify typical ranges of time allotted to clinical work, teaching (clinical and didactic), research, and administration. As a Clinician-Teacher, the successful candidate’s major non-clinical focus will be on teaching the next generation of physicians. Teaching activity will include both didactic and clinical teaching. As a Clinician-Educator, the successful candidate will focus on education research of sufficient caliber to publish in peer-reviewed journals or education administration with a focus on leading/developing world-class training programs. This position is focused on Palliative Care, an evolving area of practice within Physiatry. Clinical responsibilities include providing inpatient and ambulatory palliative care consultations at the adult sites of the McGill University Health Centre (MUHC), including the Royal Victoria Hospital and the Montreal General Hospital. Participation in on-call coverage within this area of practice is expected. The successful candidate will also contribute to the weekly Cancer Pain Clinic. Additional opportunities may be available to participate in consultations through the Alan Edwards Pain Management Unit and in general physiatry services at the Montreal General Hospital. The incumbent will be expected to be actively involved in relevant aspects of McGill’s academic mission (e.g., research, teaching, supervision of students and residents, and involvement in academic and administrative committees). McGill faculty members are expected to contribute to service activities within their units, the University, and the wider scholarly community. A demonstrated commitment to equity, diversity, and inclusion is also expected. Qualifications and Education Requirements Candidates must: Hold an MD degree; Have completed specialty training in Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation; Be certified by the Royal College of Physicians and Surgeons of Canada (or equivalent) in Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation; Be eligible for licensure in Quebec and certification by the Collège des médecins du Québec ; Have at least one additional year of clinical training in Palliative Care or Pain Management ; Bilingual language skills (French and English) are preferred, or a commitment to acquire the necessary language skills to practice medicine in Quebec. Candidates should be able to interact in English and in French with patients, families and health care professionals. Faculty/Department/Unit: Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences, Department of Medicine, Division of Physical Medicine & Rehabilitation Employee Type: Contract Academic Staff ('Academic Contractual') Rank: Assistant Professor Salary: Remuneration will be according to Quebec health care fee schedule Job Status: Full-time Posting Period: Please submit your application within 30 days of date of posting The following supporting documents are required: A cover letter & curriculum vitae; A statement indicating your academic plans if you are successful in obtaining this position. Indicate your planned career path as a Clinician-Teacher or Clinician-Educator .   Describe your clinical time allocation and detail your teaching plans and plans for service to the academic community; The names and contact information of three referees Use a personal email address when creating an account in Workday to submit your application. Do not use @mail.mcgill.ca or @mcgill.ca email accounts to apply. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. L’Université McGill est une institution de langue anglaise dans laquelle la majorité des activités d’enseignement et de recherche s’effectuent en langue anglaise, rendant essentielle l’utilisation de l’anglais pour la communication orale et écrite. McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Education-Building/Course-Lecturer-EDEC-277-760-OFNIE-F26_JR0000080682": {
      "title": "Course Lecturer EDEC 277 760 OFNIE F26",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-17\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Office of First Nations and Inuit Education (OFNIE) Course Number-Section and Title: EDEC 277 760 Mohawk Language 1 Course Description: Students will learn the Mohawk phonological system (including glottal stop, length mark, up and down stress). Syntactically and morphologically, they will focus on the pronoun system (tense included). Word generation conventions will be analyzed and Mohawk labels developed to describe how the language functions. Posting Dates : September 17 - 30, 2026 Application Deadline: September 30, 2026; 11:59 p.m. CL Credits: 3 (This course is not team-taught) Number of Positions Available: 1 Term: Fall 2026 Course Dates : November 23 - December 18, 2026 Contract dates : November 9, 2026 - January 1, 2027 Schedule: Monday, Tuesday, & Thursday: 6:00 p.m. - 9:15 p.m. Location: Kahnawà:ke Teaching Qualification Requirements (TQRs): Education A Master’s degree (in exceptional circumstances applicants without a Master’s degree but with significant research or professional experience related to the course content may be considered). Experience Expert knowledge of course content as outlined in the course description. Knowledge of the Quebec Education program (QEP) and the curriculum of the partnering school board with whom this course is being giving in collaboration. Familiarity with the 13 professional competencies specified in the Ministère de l’Éducation Knowledge of the impact of colonialism on Indigenous communities and proven record integrating pedagogical approaches anchored to equity, diversity and inclusion. Experience as a teacher and/or consultant in the partnering school board for which this course is being delivered. Experience teaching in the Quebec elementary school system. Previous successful experience teaching similar course(s) to pre-service teachers. Previous successful experience teaching similar courses in Indigenous communities. Experience developing curriculum and/or instructional approaches grounded in Indigenous knowledge and pedagogies. Other Adherence to evolving program requirements concerning course content and program-developed competencies; course content and references must reﬂect the most current advances in the ﬁeld.  The course instructor is expected to demonstrate capacity and willingness to modify the course to the educational, social, and cultural context of the community in which it is being delivered.  Availability to participate in course coordination and planning activities prior to the course start date (approximately two weeks in advance; online format). Demonstrated knowledge and/or experience with Indigenous issues in First Nations and Inuit contexts, for example through prior teaching, community engagement, or relevant academic or professional work.   Information supplémentaires/Additional Information:   Effective dates of the appointment are November 9, 2026 - January 1, 2027. Course dates are November 23 - December 18, 2026. This course will be offered in person in Kahnawà:ke following the schedule above. You are covered by the working conditions specified in the McGill Course Lecturers and Instructors Union collective agreement; https://www.mcgill.ca/hr/files/hr/mcliu_collective_agreement_-_final_version_for_printing_0.pdf . McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English Language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing.  Please contact ofnie.education@mcgill.ca  with any questions you may have. Hiring Unit: Bureau de l’éducation des Premières Nations et des Inuit (OFNIE) Course Title: Langue mohawk 1 Subject Code: EDEC 277 760 Location: Kahnawà꞉ke Schedule: Lundi, mardi et jeudi, de 18 h à 21 h 15 Deadline to Apply: 2026-09-30 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Bronfman-Building/Research-Assistant---SGI--Sustainable-Africa-_JR0000080726-1": {
      "title": "Research Assistant - SGI (Sustainable Africa)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-17\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Position Summary: This project is a Research-to-Action that seeks to explore linkages between nutrition and agri-food businesses in local and global agri-food/public health supply networks, with a focus on linkages and gaps in target geographies: selected districts/regions in Ghana, with linkages to other African countries, starting with Ivory Coast, Senegal, and with linkages to Canada.  The project is situated within the broader goals of the Sustainable Growth Initiative (SGI) Office of Sustainable Africa and the Nkabom Collaborative, a multi-stakeholder partnership with the Mastercard Foundation that brings together McGill University, six Ghanaian educational institutions, and an industry partner to transform Ghana’s nutrition and agri-food ecosystem by creating opportunities for youth-led impact through experiential learning, innovation, and entrepreneurship. The project will generate evidence-based insights and actionable recommendations to inform SGI programming, institutional partnerships, policy engagement, and future project development focused on sustainable nutrition and agri-food systems.  Project Purpose  The purpose of this research is to understand how entrepreneurship and public health supply networks interact to support or constrain the sustainability of nutrition and agri-food organizations and the project will:  1. Map relevant public health and entrepreneurship initiatives in the target countries.   2. Identify connections and gaps between these initiatives and nutrition and agri-food businesses.   3. Examine opportunities for stronger collaboration among academia, industry, government, and development organizations.   4. Develop recommendations to support SGI programming, partnerships, policy engagement, and future implementation initiatives.  Role Description   The Research Assistant will support the project by:  1. Conducting literature reviews on entrepreneurship, nutrition and agri-food businesses, public health supply networks, and sustainable enterprise development.   2. Mapping relevant initiatives, businesses, institutions, and ecosystem stakeholders.   3. Supporting stakeholder analysis and identifying existing partnerships and gaps.  4. Assisting with research and data collection.   5. Analyzing findings to identify barriers, opportunities, and potential solutions.   6. Developing a business model framework for strengthening the sustainability and growth of nutrition and agri-food organizations.   7. Preparing reports, presentations, briefing notes, and other knowledge translation materials.   8. Participating in fortnightly project meetings and supporting project coordination.    Qualifications: The successful candidate should:  1. Be enrolled as a student at McGill University during the period of employment (Undergraduate, Master's, or PhD).   2. Be available to work from September 2026 through December 2027.   3. Demonstrate an interest in entrepreneurship, agriculture, nutrition, public health, sustainable development, supply chains, or related fields.   4. Demonstrate strong research, communication, analytical, and organizational skills.   5. Be able to conduct literature reviews and stakeholder mapping.   6. Have prior coursework or experience in entrepreneurship, policy, innovation, agriculture, nutrition, food systems, or related fields (preferred).   7. Be able to work independently while contributing effectively to an interdisciplinary team.  Supervision & Support  This Research Assistant will work under the supervision of Prof. Julie Ricard and John Gradek and will receive:  1. Academic oversight and mentorship.   2. Structured guidance through fortnightly progress meetings.   3. Access to SGI networks and relevant stakeholders.   4. Support in translating research findings into practical recommendations and implementation opportunities.  Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Hourly Salary: $25.00 Hours per Week: 10 (Part time) Location: Bronfman Building Supervisor: Associate Professor (Professional) Position Start Date: 2026-09-28 Position End Date: 2027-12-31 Deadline to Apply: This position is covered by the Association of McGill University Research Employees (AMURE) collective agreement. McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Bronfman-Building/Tenure-Track-Position-in-Strategy---Organization--Desautels-Faculty-of-Management--McGill-University_JR0000080691": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track Position in Strategy & Organization, Desautels Faculty of Management, McGill University",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-17\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\n​ Please refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Tenure-Track Position in Strategy & Organization Desautels Faculty of Management McGill University Position Summary McGill’s Desautels Faculty of Management invites applications for one Assistant Professor tenure-track position in the Strategy & Organization (S&O) Area. The start date for the position is August 1, 2027 . The position is open to candidates with a Ph.D. in any area of specialization relevant to strategy and organization, as well as to applicants demonstrating outstanding potential who are doctoral students and who plan to have completed the requirements for their Ph.D. by December 2027. Preference will be given to candidates whose research aligns with Faculty priority areas of Analytics, AI & Advanced Digital Technologies; Innovation & Entrepreneurship; Sustainability; or Equity, Diversity & Inclusion. How organizations define, pursue, govern, and sustain forms of value that cannot be reduced to short-term economic performance may be the most pressing challenges of our time. Social impact is no longer a peripheral concern, but increasingly central to how the next generation of managers will be educated. Successful candidates should have the ability to teach courses focusing on the social impact of strategic management and organizations ; and will demonstrate the ability to advance a cutting-edge, compelling and impactful research agenda. Social impact in strategic management is core to the intellectual identity of the Strategy and Organization area, where colleagues engage with programs focused on how organizations formulate direction, coordinate complex activity, respond to institutional pressures, and create value across multiple stakeholder groups. The ideal candidate will have evidence of effective teaching, an experience-appropriate record of research publications in high quality, peer-reviewed journals, and clear promise of conducting innovative and important research publishable in the top journals in strategy and organization studies such as those listed on the Desautels 22 . Successful candidates are also expected to work actively with doctoral students, to teach in our undergraduate and graduate programs, and contribute to the administration of the Area and Faculty. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing; but knowledge of French or a willingness to learn French is desirable. McGill University McGill is a world-renowned institution of higher learning with a strong emphasis on scholarly activities and groundbreaking research across various disciplines and is consistently ranked as one of the top universities, both nationally and internationally. Located in downtown Montreal, McGill’s Desautels Faculty of Management brings together a diverse community of scholars whose expertise contributes to a dynamic and collaborative academic environment. The Faculty offers a personalized and experiential approach to education, emphasizing responsible management by applying interdisciplinary, integrated and globally minded perspectives to teaching, research and partnerships. The Strategy and Organization Area at Desautels brings together scholars specializing in strategic management, organizational theory, and the study of globalization, industry emergence, and organizational change. Professors have diverse disciplinary backgrounds and theoretical and methodological approaches and enjoy a high level of independence in their research and teaching endeavors. McGill's emphasis is on research that combines theoretical and empirical rigour with social relevance, often addressing \"big questions\" through longitudinal, process-focused study. The Faculty offers a Major in Strategic Management at the undergraduate level, along with concentrations in Social Business & Enterprise and Global Strategy, as well as an MBA concentration in Global Strategy and Leadership and a PhD specialization in Strategy and Organization. Members of the Area are affiliated with the Centre for Strategy Studies in Organizations (CSSO), which promotes research on the formation of strategic direction, innovation in organizational forms and practices, and the relationship between organizations and society. Montreal is a vibrant, multilingual city that blends rich history with modern innovation, making it an ideal place to live and work. With its safe neighbourhoods, affordable housing, excellent public transit and year-round activities, it’s a welcoming environment for both individuals and families. From world-class culture and cuisine to thriving industries and diverse communities, Montreal offers a dynamic lifestyle that’s both enriching and accessible. Discover Montreal       Best cities to study in the world       Desautels Faculty of Management Compensation levels, teaching load, research funding and travel support are competitive and commensurate with experience. Application process and deadline These materials must be submitted through Workday ( all in the same section as you submit your CV ): Cover letter Curriculum vitae Statement of current and prospective research When applicable, evidence of teaching effectiveness Sample research papers We are not requesting reference letters at this point; reference check is a secondary step which may follow.      All application materials should be submitted electronically by October 20, 2026 and only complete electronic submissions are accepted. The hiring process including the review of application materials, interviews and campus visits with short-listed candidates will be ongoing until the positions are filled or the search is terminated. Short-listed candidates invited to meet with members of the Faculty should expect to interview, if health and safety standards allow it, in-person in Montreal.   Any questions about the position or receipt of materials should be addressed to saku.mantere@mcgill.ca The cover letter in the application may be addressed to: Prof. Saku Mantere Desautels Faculty of Management McGill University 1001 Sherbrooke Street West Montreal, Quebec, Canada H3A 1G5 Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based. McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Education-Building/Course-Lecturer-EDEC-275-760-OFNIE-F26_JR0000080657": {
      "title": "Course Lecturer EDEC 275 760 OFNIE F26",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-17\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Office of First Nations and Inuit Education (OFNIE) Course Number-Section and Title: EDEC 275 760 Mohawk Heritage Language 1 Course Description: Students will develop a basic knowledge of the Mohawk phonological system and have some understanding of the morphological and syntactic rules, the stress and intonation patterns which control the language, and how Mohawk culture is reflected in the language. Posting Dates : September 17 - 30, 2026 Application Deadline: September 30, 2026; 11:59 p.m. CL Credits: 3 (This course is not team-taught) Number of Positions Available: 1 Term: Fall 2026 Course Dates : November 23 - December 18, 2026 Contract dates : November 9, 2026 - January 1, 2027 Schedule: Monday, Tuesday, & Thursday: 6:00 p.m. - 9:15 p.m. Location: Kahnawà:ke Teaching Qualification Requirements (TQRs): Education A Master’s degree (in exceptional circumstances applicants without a Master’s degree but with significant research or professional experience related to the course content may be considered). Experience Expert knowledge of course content as outlined in the course description. Knowledge of the Quebec Education program (QEP) and the curriculum of the partnering school board with whom this course is being giving in collaboration. Familiarity with the 13 professional competencies specified in the Ministère de l’Éducation Knowledge of the impact of colonialism on Indigenous communities and proven record integrating pedagogical approaches anchored to equity, diversity and inclusion. Experience as a teacher and/or consultant in the partnering school board for which this course is being delivered. Experience teaching in the Quebec elementary school system. Previous successful experience teaching similar course(s) to pre-service teachers. Previous successful experience teaching similar courses in Indigenous communities. Experience developing curriculum and/or instructional approaches grounded in Indigenous knowledge and pedagogies. Other Adherence to evolving program requirements concerning course content and program-developed competencies; course content and references must reﬂect the most current advances in the ﬁeld.  The course instructor is expected to demonstrate capacity and willingness to modify the course to the educational, social, and cultural context of the community in which it is being delivered.  Availability to participate in course coordination and planning activities prior to the course start date (approximately two weeks in advance; online format). Demonstrated knowledge and/or experience with Indigenous issues in First Nations and Inuit contexts, for example through prior teaching, community engagement, or relevant academic or professional work.   Information supplémentaires/Additional Information:   Effective dates of the appointment are November 9, 2026 - January 1, 2027. Course dates are November 23 - December 18, 2026. This course will be offered in person in Kahnawà:ke following the schedule above. You are covered by the working conditions specified in the McGill Course Lecturers and Instructors Union collective agreement; https://www.mcgill.ca/hr/files/hr/mcliu_collective_agreement_-_final_version_for_printing_0.pdf . McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English Language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing.  Please contact ofnie.education@mcgill.ca  with any questions you may have. Hiring Unit: Bureau de l’éducation des Premières Nations et des Inuit (OFNIE) Course Title: Langue patrimoniale mohawk 1 Subject Code: EDEC 275 760 Location: Kahnawà:ke Schedule: Lundi, mardi et jeudi, de 18 h à 21 h 15 Deadline to Apply: 2026-09-30 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Bronfman-Building/Innovation-for-Impact-Outreach-and-Events-Assistant_JR0000080724": {
      "title": "Innovation for Impact Outreach and Events Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-17\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Desautels Faculty of Management   Position Summary: This position will support the planning and delivery of the Innovation for Impact Competition, scheduled for March 2027. Working closely with the Program Manager, the selected candidate will contribute to student outreach, marketing, and event logistics to help ensure the success of the competition. Key Responsibilities: - Assist in expanding and maintaining a database of student clubs, associations, and other relevant student organizations, with a particular focus on the Desautels Faculty of Management, to support targeted promotion of the competition. - Develop promotional and marketing materials for both digital and print distribution. - Support recruitment and outreach efforts through activities such as information tabling and classroom visits during October and November 2026. - Design and prepare winner recognition materials, including posters and graphics for publication on LinkedIn, the SGI website, and other communication channels. - Provide on-site event support and logistical assistance during the competition on March 19, 2027, at the Dobson Centre for Entrepreneurship. - Perform other event-related and administrative tasks as required to support competition planning and execution. Workload : The workload for this position will vary throughout the appointment period, with busier periods expected during promotional campaigns and in the weeks leading up to the competition. Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities - Registered McGill student in good academic standing at the undergraduate or graduate level. - Demonstrated experience creating professional promotional materials and graphics for LinkedIn, social media platforms, websites, or other marketing channels. - Experience supporting event planning, coordination, and logistics, with strong organizational and time-management skills. - Familiarity with student engagement and outreach, including collaborating with student clubs, associations, and campus organizations. - Strong written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to effectively promote initiatives and engage diverse student audiences. - Proficiency with graphic design tools (e.g., Canva, Adobe Creative Suite, or equivalent) is considered an asset. - Ability to work independently, manage multiple priorities, and meet deadlines in a dynamic work environment. - Interest in sustainability, innovation, entrepreneurship, or student leadership programming is considered an asset. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level # (qualifier) on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: High School Diploma 2 Years Related Experience Hourly Salary: (AMUSE C) $19.02 Estimated Number of Work Hours per Week: 5 (Part time) Position Start Date: 2026-10-05 Position End Date: 2027-03-26 Deadline to Apply: This position is covered by the AMUSE collective agreement. McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/McGill-College-2001/Office-Assistant-C_JR0000079637": {
      "title": "Office Assistant C",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-17\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Department of Psychology Position Summary: Acts as a liaison between the supervisor and internal and external stakeholders, including students, study participants, and graduate students, providing administrative information and support related to laboratory operations and research studies. Helps organize individual and group events, such as lab meetings, workshops, and training sessions. Provides support with office software and administrative systems. Acts as a resource person to help resolve issues that may arise during planning, scheduling, and event coordination. Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities Knowledge of Microsoft 365, Qualtrics, Cloud Research and eRAP. Ability to conduct statistical analyses (e.g., JASP). Experience and Education: Background in Psychology Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level 3 (qualifier) on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: High School Diploma 2 Years Related Experience Hourly Salary: (AMUSE C) $18.50 Estimated Number of Work Hours per Week: 10 (Part time) Position Start Date: 2026-08-20 Position End Date: 2027-02-21 Deadline to Apply: 2026-09-23 This position is covered by the AMUSE collective agreement. McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
