    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Macdonald-Eng-Bldg/Invigilation--FACC-300-Engineering-Economy_JR0000060725": {
      "title": "Invigilation: FACC 300 Engineering Economy",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-18\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Fall 2026 posting: The Faculty of Engineering is searching for eligible candidates as invigilators for the Winter 2026 term FACC 300 midterm exam. For details job description, please visit: https://www.mcgill.ca/engineering/about-us/employment-opportunities How to apply: Evergreen Requisition: JR0000060725 Invigilation: FACC 300 Engineering Economy can be accessed using the 'Search for Jobs'  in Workday. Internal Applicants: Internal applicants are McGill employees who currently hold an ACTIVE contract at McGill University. Current McGill employees must apply via internal postings. External Applicants: External applicants are any applicant who do not currently hold an active contract at McGill University. This applies for employees who may have had contracts in the past, but do not currently hold an active contract. They cannot apply internally. The website will direct you to create a Workday account. McGill Careers Website:  https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers Please note, applicants must use a personal email address when creating this account. Applicants must use their legal names when creating their Workday candidate home accounts. McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Macdonald-Eng-Bldg/Grader_JR0000080339": {
      "title": "Grader",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-18\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Grader: FACC 150 Foundations of Engineering Practice and Communication Job Description Summary The grader is responsible for grading assessments, posting grades, and returning assessments to students. Assessments may include, but are not limited to, quizzes, homework, exams, and presentations. Job Description Grade assignments and exams for FACC 150 Foundations of Engineering Practice and Communication according to guidelines established by the instructor. Provide constructive, detailed, and timely feedback to students on written work and other assessments. Maintain accuracy, fairness, and consistency in grading. Typical Functions (i.) Marks assignments and exams following set guidelines. (ii.) Completes grading keys and provides detailed, constructive feedback. (iii.) Completes grading and returns feedback by specified deadlines. (iv.) Maintains records of grades by entering them into MyCourses and/or Excel files. (v.) Provides timely feedback to support student learning and improvement. (vi.) The list of functions outlined above is representative and not a complete and detailed list of tasks which may be performed by an incumbent. Estimated total number of hours 40 Qualifications Familiarity with course content, such as having previously completed FACC 100, FACC 250, FACC 400 , or equivalent engineering communication coursework. Bachelor’s degree or Master’s degree in progress Strong written communication skills and demonstrated ability to provide constructive feedback on written work in English. Attention to detail and ability to apply grading rubrics consistently. Strong organizational and time-management skills, with the ability to meet grading deadlines. Experience using MyCourses, Excel, or other learning management systems is considered an asset. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Hourly Salary: $20.00 Position Start Date: 2026-09-26 Position End Date: 2026-12-22 Deadline to Apply: 2026-09-25 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Macdonald-Eng-Bldg/Student-Research-Assistant_JR0000080437": {
      "title": "Student Research Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-18\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). The Faculty of Engineering , Dean’s Office is seeking a Student Research Assistant to assist the Integrating AI to Reshape Engineering Pedagogy project (funded through Entente Canada-Quebec).     Primary Responsibilities    Conduct a comprehensive literature review and environmental scans on best practices for integrating AI content into undergraduate engineering programs .   With the Research project team, review McGill engineering programs and identify where AI literacy and applied AI knowledge and skills should be embedded.   Work with instructors to create AI modules and projects for engineering courses.   With the project team, support the development of learning outcomes, a course plan, and draft instructional content for a Fundamental AI in Engineering course.   Contribute to the development of conference presentations and academic articles.    Participate in an AI in engineering education working group.   Attend weekly 30-minute team meetings .     Job Requirements/Minimum Education/ Experience   Possesses subject matter expertise acquired through undergraduate studies in various Engineering programs at McGill.     Active McGill student in various Engineering programs at McGill .     Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities   Students applying should indicate knowledge/experience in any of the following areas:   Engineering education   Applied artificial intelligence or generative AI   Organization and time management skills   Effective communication skills   Ability to interact with instructors   Familiarity with Microsoft 365 tools   Familiarity with web conferencing technology (e.g., Microsoft Teams, Zoom)   Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Hourly Salary: $22.00 Hours per Week: 10 (Part time) Location: Macdonald Eng. Bldg. Supervisor: Academic Associate Position Start Date: 2026-10-03 Position End Date: 2027-05-31 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-02 This position is covered by the Association of McGill University Research Employees (AMURE) collective agreement. McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Various/Invigilator---SPOT-Midterm-Exams_JR0000080813": {
      "title": "Invigilator - SPOT Midterm Exams",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-18\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: School of Physical and Occupational Therapy Exam Dates/Times (including additional time): PHTH 301/Written 14 September 13h30-14h30 (arrival 13h00) PHTH 623/Written  29-Sep   9h30-12h30  (arrival 9h00) POTH 612/Written  30-Sep 12h30-14h30  (arrival 12h00) PHTH 482/Written  2-Oct-26 16h00-17h30 (arrival 15h30) POTH 440/Written  15-Oct-26  11h30-13h00 (arrival 11h00) PHTH 551/Written  16-Oct-26  13h30-15h30 (arrival 13h00) POTH 204/Written  19-Oct-26  14h00-16h00 (arrival 13h30) PHTH 550/Written  19-Oct-26  14h30-17h30 (arrival 14h00) PHTH 623/OSCE     20-Oct-26  8h00-13h00 (arrival 8h00) PHTH 550/OSCE     20-Oct-26  12h30-17h30 (arrival 12h15) POTH 455/Written  20-Oct-26   11h45-13h45 (arrival 11h15) PHTH 623/OSCE     22-Oct-26   8h00-13h00 (arrival 8h00) PHTH 550/OSCE     22-Oct-26   12h30-17h30 (arrival 12h15) PHTH 623/Written  10-Nov-26    9h30-12h30  (arrival 9h00) PHTH 482/Written  13-Nov-26    16h00-17h30 (15h30) PHTH 551/Written  26-Nov-26    9h00-11h00 (arrival 8h30)   Number of Positions: 26 Locations: Various Instructors: TBD Position Summary: Responsible for setting up the examination room according to specific requirements as well as ensuring that equipment and facilities are functioning properly. Administers the delivery of the electronic and paper exams and documentation accordingly. Handles students’ issues and directs them to IT support, the Exam Administrator or Professor as needed. Ensures that students comply with regulations with respect to the exam. Responsible to report any behavior by students or others that does not comply with University regulations. Ensures that all mobile and web‐accessible electronic devices are collected from the students prior to the start of the examination and returned upon their departure. Ensures that students are accompanied to the washroom. Alerts the Professor or Exam Administrator when students have a question. At the end of the examination period, collects all exam materials and ensures nothing is left behind in the examination room(s). Responsible for reporting back to the Professor or Exam Administrator and ensuring delivery of the exams. Qualifications: High School Leaving Certificate/Diploma (minimum requirement). Hourly Salary: (AGSEM Invigilator) $18.00 Deadline to Apply: 2026-09-24 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Macdonald-Eng-Bldg/Graders--Mechanical-Engineering----Fall-2026_JR0000080704": {
      "title": "Graders (Mechanical Engineering) - Fall 2026",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-18\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Mechanical Engineering Grader positions for the Fall 2026 semester A grader provides support with marking assignments and exams following guidelines set by instructor. Background knowledge of the subject matter is an asset.  These positions are open to McGill University students only. This position requires in-person work and is located in the city of Montreal. You are expected to have an address in the province of Quebec. Students must be present and available to work during the course dates. Hiring Unit: Mechanical Engineering Effective Dates of Appointment: August 31, 2026, to December 18, 2026 Hourly Salary: $19.50 Application Deadline: October 2, 2026, at 11:59 PM EST Positions available and total hours of work: MECH 265 - Numerical Linear Algebra (1 position, 100 hours) MECH 309 - Numerical Methods in Mechanical Engineering (1 position, 100 hours) MECH 315 - Intermediate Dynamics (1 position, 100 hours) MECH 500 - Selected Topics in Mechanical Engineering (1 position, 75 hours) MECH 501/ MECH 600 - Special Topics: Mechanical Engineering/ Advanced Topics in Mechanical Engineering 1 (1 position, 75 hours) MECH 530 - Mechanics of Composite Materials (1 position, 75 hours) MECH 533/ AERO 633 - Subsonic Aerodynamics/ Aerodynamics (1 position, 50 hours) MECH 535 - Turbomachinery and Propulsion (1 position, 100 hours) MECH 536 - Aerospace Structures (1 position, 75 hours) MECH 548 - Cellular Materials in Natural and Engineering Structures (1 position, 50 hours) MECH 559 - Engineering Systems Optimization (1 position, 75 hours) MECH 561 - Biomechanics of Musculoskeletal System (1 position, 50 hours) MECH 605 - Applied Mathematics 1 (1 position, 50 hours) MECH 632- Advanced Mechanics of Materials (1 position, 50 hours) Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Les-Cours-Mont-Royal/Engagement-Analyst_JR0000080490": {
      "title": "Engagement Analyst",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-17\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). University Advancement (UA) promotes McGill to its global community and to the public, engaging alumni, volunteers and friends of the University, and building on McGill’s record of fundraising success in support of University priorities. By joining UA, you can be part of a dynamic and exciting team of professionals that is making an extraordinary impact on the world by advancing the philanthropic and engagement ambitions of McGill University. Position Summary: Under the general direction of the Senior Director, Engagement Analytics & Annual Giving, the Engagement Analyst is responsible for analyzing large data sets from a variety of platforms and databases with the goal of creating strategic recommendations for mass solicitation, engagement and communication efforts. Work with various units in University Advancement including Information Management, Annual Giving, Alumni Relations, Communications and others with the goal of prioritizing engagement and solicitation efforts through analysis, statistical modeling, data visualization and key performance indicators (KPI) development. The Engagement Analyst would also be responsible for handling data requests, assisting with data integrity guidelines and partaking in University Advancement’s Analytics Centre of Excellence. Primary Responsibilities: Analytics Program Work with the Engagement Analytics team to implement and oversee a comprehensive engagement analytics program, to support the strategic information needs of University Advancement and its various Units through the optimization of mass giving, engagement and communication programs Participate in the creation of a variety of data models and data visualizations that would assist various departments across University Advancement in lead generation, segmentation, interest identification, persona development, broadcast and other mass communication analyses and prioritization/identification of prospects or workflow. Participate in statistical modeling, analysis and reporting to determine and optimize alignment of resources with the strategic plan, including, data mining, predictive modeling, forecasting and statistical analysis to identify trends and patterns in giving, volunteerism, event participation, engagement with content, alumni and donor behaviour, etc.; Participate in the performance reviews of mass program engagement strategies and analysis of constituent surveys. Participate in the development of dashboards, data visualizations and performance reports to measure digital campaigns, social media and website performance Participate in the development of standards, as led by various Units, in regards to data collection both from internal and external sources Ensure data integrity by investigating anomalies in reported numbers from a variety of sources throughout Advancement Work with Advancement Information Management to ensure reporting standards are adhered to across different departments and units where information is shared. Write/present project reports and executive summaries on a range of philanthropic issues, with particular focus on analytics, KPIs and emerging trends within Engagement. Play an active role in the development and monitoring of KPIs for Engagement, as led by the Unit – including developing reporting and data visualization tools, reviewing existing data structures and researching emerging trends. Participate in University Advancement’s Analytics Centre of Excellence with the aim of creating synergies and best-practices in analytics across University Advancement. Work with Senior Director, Engagement Analytics & Annual Giving to report and present high level trend analyses, financial and engagement projections and data-insights in support of strategic planning for the Senior Management Team. Use analytical techniques to process large volume of data from multiple sources into actionable opportunities. Conduct sophisticated ad hoc query data retrievals; provide analysis of data extracts as required. Assist in the development of data visualization methods and reporting capabilities to enable more effective strategic, tactical, and operational insights and decision-making. Other Responsibilities Utilize best-in-class data visualization tools to assist other departments in understanding and representing key information. Keep abreast of technology trends and new tools, as well as researching, testing and presenting solutions for integration into existing processes. Actively seek and collect industry trends and benchmarking data that support the business processes/outcomes of McGill University’s fundraising efforts. Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities Previous business intelligence or analytics experience in a non-profit fundraising, marketing analytics or customer insights role. Ability to understand business needs formulated by senior management and other internal clients and translate them into actionable analysis and information. Proven experience translating business requirements into data requirements. Ability to write and design reports and produce and deliver informative, well organized presentations. Proven experience in communications and marketing analytics including experience with Salesforce marketing cloud and Google Analytics. Knowledge of Google Analytics 4, SEM campaign strategies and/or SEO practices is an asset. Proven experience with Salesforce CRM workflows, reporting and analytics. Must be able to communicate effectively and confidently with internal clients at all levels within the organization. Knowledge in data extraction and manipulation Technical experience with analysis and visualization tools (e.g. Excel, Power BI, Tableau, R, …) Proven experience with the usage of Python, SQL or DAX for more advanced data analysis, statistical modeling, and machine learning applications. Creative, well organized and self-motivated, with strong analytical, planning and problem solving skills. Demonstrated ability to manage multiple projects and meet strict deadlines. Knowledge of cutting-edge/emerging technologies as they relate to business needs is an asset. Knowledge of and experience in building various statistical and machine learning models is an asset. Knowledge of French and English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level 3 on a scale of 0-4. Additional Information: A competency assessment may be required, including an open-ended analytical exercise with a presentation. This is a term appointment until January 2028. As one of Montreal's Top Employers, here is what we offer: Competitive benefits package (Health, Dental, Life Insurance) (if eligible) Defined contribution pension plan (with employer contribution up to 10%) (if eligible) Group Registered Retirement Savings Plan (RRSP) and Tax Free Savings Account (TFSA) Competitive vacation policy Two (2) personal days Two (2) floating holidays Nine (9) \"Summer Fridays\" - paid days off between the St-Jean Baptiste holiday and Labour Day Paid time off over the December holiday period Tuition waiver for regular employees and their dependents Up to two (2) days of remote work per week where the position permits Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level 3 on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: Bachelor's Degree 3 Years Related Experience / Annual Salary: (MPEX Grade 05) $73,470.00 - $91,840.00 - $110,210.00 Job Profile: MPEX-COM2C - Development & Alumni Relations - Professional 2 Hours per Week: 33.75 (Full time) Supervisor: Senior Director, Engagement Analytics and Annual Giving Position End Date (If applicable): 2028-01-28 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-02 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/James-Admin-Bldg/Regulatory-Officer_JR0000080423": {
      "title": "Regulatory Officer",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-17\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Position Summary: Reporting to the Secretary-General, in the office of the Secretariat, the Regulatory Officer manages the University’s access-to-documents function in line with the Act respecting Access to documents held by public bodies and the Protection of personal information. The incumbent supports the Secretary-General, as the University’s person in charge of access to documents, by receiving and analyzing access requests, arranging document searches and consultations, and preparing recommendations and draft decisions for the Secretary-General’s review and signature. The role also includes working closely with Legal Services in preparing and administering files that proceed before the Commission d’accès à l’information , raising awareness within the University community on access-related responsibilities, advising the University Secretariat on records management matters, and supporting other regulatory functions and special initiatives, as directed by the Secretary-General or Associate Director. Primary Responsibilities: Administer the University’s access-to-documents workflow, from receipt of a request through preparation of the response and closure of the file. Support the Secretary-General, as the University’s person in charge of access to documents, through the preparation of analyses, recommendations and draft decisions. All statutory decisions remain with and are signed by the Secretary-General. Arrange document searches and consultations with academic and administrative units across the University, identifying units likely to hold responsive documents and ensuring searches are conducted in a timely and complete manner. Collaborate with the Associate Director in assessing and managing access requests, including reviewing complex or sensitive matters and proposed approaches before escalation to the Secretary-General. Collaborate with the Manager, Privacy and Protection of Personal Information on requests involving personal information or privacy considerations, including where responsive documents contain personal information or engage privacy requirements. Prepare and administer files in support of Legal Services where an access decision is before the Commission d’accès à l’informatio n. Develop guidance, education and awareness activities concerning the access-to-documents process and the responsibilities of members of the University community. Advise the University Secretariat on records management matters and support for other regulatory functions and special initiatives, as required. Receive, acknowledge and review requests for access to documents submitted to the University. Communicate with requestors on administrative and procedural matters, including clarifying the scope of requests where necessary. Review documents received to assess their responsiveness and determine whether additional searches or consultations are required. Identify information that may be disclosed or withheld and matters requiring consultation with the Secretary-General, Associate Director, and Manager, Privacy and Protection of Personal Information. Prepare documents for disclosure, including completing or arranging required redactions and verifying disclosure packages for accuracy and completeness. Prepare analysis, recommendations and draft decisions, for the Secretary-General’s consideration and signature. Monitor requests to ensure compliance with statutory requirements and timelines and advise on approaching deadlines, processing challenges and matters requiring attention. Maintain accurate and complete records of the receipt, processing, analysis and disposition of access requests. Maintain tracking tools and prepare regular status reports on request volumes, deadlines, outcomes and files requiring follow-up. Develop and deliver information sessions and awareness activities concerning the access-to-documents process and the responsibilities of University units and employees. Prepare and maintain procedures, templates, guidance materials and other resources supporting the effective and consistent processing of access requests. Identify opportunities to improve access-related workflows, tracking systems, guidance and administrative practices, and propose recommendations. Advise the University Secretariat on the Office’s records management practices, in consultation with the University’s records management function as appropriate. Provide research, analysis and operational support in relation to other regulatory responsibilities and special initiatives of the University Secretariat, as assigned by the Secretary-General or Associate Director. Provide backup support to Secretariat colleagues during absences, significant institutional exercises or periods of exceptional workload, as required. Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities Strong interest and knowledge in institutional governance and regulatory issues, specifically in learning institutions Appreciation and ability to uphold the Unit’s mandate as an impartial office in the delivery of a wide range of regulatory activities Strong work ethic, demonstrated accountability for workflows and outcomes Ability to anticipate issues and consequences of actions and propose recommendations, in line with the Unit’s mandate and University policies and regulations Analytical skills, especially as related to interpreting legislation or university policy Superior writing and communication skills in English, ability to communicate in French (spoken and written) Demonstrated ability to work effectively in a team environment as well as independently Organizational and delegation skills, effectively leading the team in reporting status and identifying and resolving issues Attentive to detail without losing sight of the overall goal or objective, strategic thinking Ability to deliver work in a high-pressure environment with poise, diplomacy, and professionalism Ability to inspire trust and respect in the role Undergraduate Degree (Law, Arts, Management, or related field) Three (3) years related experience As one of Montreal's Top Employers, here is what we offer: Competitive benefits package (Health, Dental, Life Insurance) (if eligible) Defined contribution pension plan (with employer contribution up to 10%) (if eligible) Group Registered Retirement Savings Plan (RRSP) and Tax Free Savings Account (TFSA) Competitive vacation policy Two (2) personal days Two (2) floating holidays Nine (9) \"Summer Fridays\" - paid days off between the St-Jean Baptiste holiday and Labour Day Paid time off over the December holiday period Tuition waiver for regular employees and their dependents Up to two (2) days of remote work per week where the position permits Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level # 4 on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: Bachelor's Degree 3 Years Related Experience / Annual Salary: (MPEX Grade 04) $66,190.00 - $82,740.00 - $99,290.00 Job Profile: MPEX-ADM2F - Secretariat Administration - Professional 2 Hours per Week: 33.75 (Full time) Supervisor: Secretary-General Position End Date (If applicable): Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-01 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Strathcona-Music/Program-Assistant--McGill-Camerata-_JR0000080793": {
      "title": "Program Assistant (McGill Camerata)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-17\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Ensembles and Conducting Area (Jean-Sébastien Vallée) Position Summary: Under the supervision of the Program Director, the Program Assistant will support the overall administration and coordination of the McGill Camerata. Key Responsibilities: Assisting with the planning and execution of the McGill Camerata, a community engagement project part of the Choral Academy. Coordinating logistics for program components, including rehearsals and performances, for the McGill Camerata. Supporting communication efforts such as managing social media accounts, designing posters, drafting publicity materials, and updating web content for the McGill Camerata. Supporting on-site logistics and providing hands-on assistance during choral events related to the McGill Camerata. Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities Strong organizational and project management skills, with ability to handle multiple tasks and deadlines. Excellent written and verbal communication skills. Proficiency in social media management and digital communication tools. Attention to detail in preparing, proofreading, and compiling program and event materials. Strong interpersonal skills for coordinating with guest artists, faculty, students, and community members. Ability to work collaboratively as part of a team while also taking initiative independently. Flexibility and problem-solving skills to support on-site logistics and respond to event-day needs. Interest in or knowledge of choral music, performing arts, or arts administration  Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level # 4 (qualifier) on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: High School Diploma 2 Years Related Experience Hourly Salary: (AMUSE C) $20.00 Estimated Number of Work Hours per Week: 10 (Part time) Position Start Date: 2026-09-25 Position End Date: 2027-04-30 Deadline to Apply: 2026-09-24 This position is covered by the AMUSE collective agreement. McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
