    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/McLennan-Library/Exam-Office-Assistant_JR0000081043": {
      "title": "Exam Office Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-23\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Position Summary: Reporting to the Chief/Deputy Invigilators, the Exam Office Assistant supports the Crowdmark exam process by handling administrative tasks related to digital exam preparation and management. Duties and responsibilities: Organizing and preparing exam materials for digital processing and distribution. Ensuring accurate record-keeping of exam submissions and materials. Assisting with the distribution and tracking of exams through platforms. Providing basic support for troubleshooting and resolving issues with the exam process. Communicating with professors and invigilators to assist with exam-related queries. Performing general office duties and supporting the smooth execution of the exam session. Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities: Strong attention to detail and organizational skills. Ability to manage tasks efficiently in a fast-paced environment. Familiarity with basic digital tools and platforms. Clear communication skills and ability to work well within a team. Punctuality and availability for the full duration of the employment period. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level # (qualifier) on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: High School Diploma Minimal or No Related Experience Hourly Salary: (AMUSE A) $18.25 Estimated Number of Work Hours per Week: 20 (Part time) Position Start Date: 2026-10-05 Position End Date: 2026-12-23 Deadline to Apply: 2026-09-30 This position is covered by the AMUSE collective agreement. McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Raymond-Building/Assistant-Professor-in-Environment--Climate-Resilience--and-the-Arctic---Emerging-Leader-for-Impact--Chair_JR0000081013": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Environment, Climate Resilience, and the Arctic - Emerging Leader for Impact+ Chair",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-23\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\n​ Please refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Canada Impact+ Emerging Leaders (McGill University) Tenure-Track Faculty Position  Open to rank of Assistant Professor in Regulated Plant Pests Program Description The Canada Impact+ Emerging Leaders program is a one-time initiative designed to support institutions in recruiting internationally based Early Career Researchers to Canada. Emerging Leaders will receive research funding and institutional support to advance transformative research in Canada’s strategic priority areas , build partnerships across sectors and borders, and translate their research into applications and impacts that benefit Canadians and the world. The Faculty of Agricultural and Environmental Sciences at McGill University invites applications for a full-time tenure-track Assistant Professor position to advance transformational research programs that support the growth of Canada’s global leadership in the following strategic priority area: Environment, climate resilience, and the Arctic Emerging Leaders must have an independent program of research that demonstrates strategic alignment with this priority area. The award provides $100,000 per year for six years to McGill University to support the salary costs associated with the successful Emerging Leader appointment as well as direct research expenses, with the possibility of extension for a further six years. Successful candidates will be expected to begin a full-time tenure-track appointment at the rank of Assistant Professor no later than June 28, 2027 , in the Department of Plant Science. Job Duties The successful candidate specializes in the study of insects, pathogens, nematodes, or invasive weeds that are legally restricted by governments to protect agriculture and ecosystems. The successful candidate will develop an internationally recognized, externally funded research program focused on the biology, diagnostics, epidemiology, management or responses to environmental and climate change of regulated pests, invasive species, or quarantine organisms that affect agricultural crops in Canada. The successful candidate is expected to teach core courses [such as an introductory course related to plant pests (e.g., Insect Biology), Pesticides in Agriculture] in the Faculty’s undergraduate programs and a graduate course related to Integrated Pest Management. In addition to teaching, the candidate will be expected to conduct innovative research in regulated plant pests, making full use of the extensive research infrastructure at the Macdonald Campus of McGill University, more specifically the Plant Pest Containment (PPC) Laboratory, which is part of the Eastern Canadian Plant Phenotyping Platform (ECP3). The ideal candidate will bridge the gap between academic research and regulatory science, collaborating closely with government agencies, industry stakeholders, and international plant health organizations. The candidate should also actively pursue collaborative research with other departments and schools within the Faculty of Agricultural and Environmental Sciences to foster interdisciplinary approaches. Participation in Department, Faculty, and University committees is expected, as are interactions with provincial and federal organizations and professional societies. Qualifications and Education Requirement s Only candidates who were internationally based, both working and residing outside Canada, on June 28, 2026, are eligible. Candidates must be within five years following the start date of their first research-related appointment, minus the length of any eligible delays in research. Recent graduates and postdoctoral fellows may also be eligible.   Ph.D. in Plant Health, Plant Biology, Integrated Pest Management or related field; track record and demonstrated excellence in working in high-containment facilities (Biosafety Level 2 or 3) handling quarantine or regulated pests; knowledge of Canadian plant protection legislation, the Plant Protection Act, and international phytosanitary standards (ISPMs). McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. The ability to teach and communicate in French and prior experience with the Canadian agricultural and agri-food industry are considered an asset. Research-related appointments are defined as those where the individual has the autonomy to conduct research independently. Eligible leaves, including maternity, parental, medical, and bereavement leave, are credited as twice the amount of time taken. Professional leaves, including training, sabbatical, and administrative leave, are not credited. A PhD is required by McGill University for appointment to a tenure-track position; however, it is not a requirement of the Impact+ Emerging Leaders Program itself. Candidates should review the eligibility and selection criteria for the Impact+ Program prior to applying.     Canadians living abroad who wish to relocate to Canada are encouraged to apply. Faculty/Department/Unit: Faculty of Agricultural and Environmental Sciences / Department of Plant Science Job Type: Tenure-track Rank: Assistant Professor Salary: Salary will be commensurate with qualifications and experience. Posting Period: Please submit your application by October 30, 2026. Required Documents: The application package should include the documents listed below and must be merged into one single PDF file not exceeding 5 MB. Cover letter Curriculum vitae  Statement of research describing current and future research, alignment with the strategic priority area, and potential for knowledge translation, mobilization, and application of research results (maximum 2 pages) Research infrastructure needs: laboratory space, equipment (max. 1 page) Teaching statement (max. 1 page) and dossier Contact information for three references  Application Process Applications must be submitted through Workday, McGill’s HR management system. Posting Period   Review of applications will start on November 1, 2026. Inquiries from potential candidates are welcome and will be treated in strict confidence. Correspondence can be directed to Prof. Martin Filion ( martin.filion@mcgill.ca ), chair of the search committee. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Mtl-General-Hosp/Course-Lecturer---EXSU-605-Biomedical-Research-Innovation_JR0000080922": {
      "title": "Course Lecturer - EXSU 605 Biomedical Research Innovation",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-23\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). The Department of Surgery, Division of Surgical and Interventional Sciences is seeking a Course Lecturer to teach the 2027 Winter semester:  Biomedical Research Innovation. Course Title and Course Number: EXSU 605 - Biomedical Research Innovation Teaching Qualification Requirements: PhD Chemical and Biomedical Engineering   Experience: Experience conducting and publishing research in biosensor, diagnostic, biomaterials, in vitro and in vivo hands on, but also expertise in regenerative medicine with application to Medical/Clinical problem. History of consistently publishing manuscripts relevant to the field in high quality peer reviewed journals. An asset would be experience in undertaking research collaborations with medical specialists such as surgeons, clinicians, pathologists, etc.​ Number of credits: 3 ​Compensation: $ 11,824 Job Start Dat e: January 4th, 2027 Job End Date: April 14th, 2027 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for 