    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Bronfman-Building/Faculty-Position-in-Operations-Management---Finance---Ranked-Contract-Academic-Staff--CAS-_JR0000079970": {
      "title": "Faculty Position in Operations Management & Finance | Ranked Contract Academic Staff (CAS)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-22\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nFull time\n\n​ Please refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Faculty Position in Operations Management & Finance Ranked Contract Academic Staff (CAS) Desautels Faculty of Management McGill University Position Summary McGill’s Desautels Faculty of Management invites applications for one Ranked Contractual Academic Staff (CAS) position jointly in the Operations Management (OM) and Finance Areas. Note that this is not a tenure-track position. The start date for the appointment will be January 1 2027. With the exception of Ranked CAS who are appointed at the rank of Professor, a Ranked CAS is appointed to an initial term of one, two or three years; the contract is renewable. The position is open to candidates with a PhD in Operations Research, Operations Management, Statistics, Economics, Finance or a related field, with at least three years of teaching experience. We have a particular interest in candidates whose teaching connects to the Faculty priority area of Analytics, AI & Advanced Digital Technologies. Successful candidates should have the ability to teach courses in the areas of business statistics, operations management, and finance, principally to an undergraduate audience, and contribute to the administration of the Areas and Faculty. The largest share of the teaching load will be in business statistics, with the remaining sections allocated across Operations Management and Finance courses depending on teaching needs in a given academic year. This position requires teaching up to 8 course sections per academic year. We have a preference for candidates with a teaching portfolio and an established record in teaching business statistics related courses, and/or investments and corporate finance related courses, principally at the undergraduate level. McGill University is an English-language university where teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing; but knowledge of French or a willingness to learn French is desirable. McGill University McGill is a world-renowned institution of higher learning with a strong emphasis on scholarly activities and groundbreaking research across various disciplines and is consistently ranked as one of the top universities, both nationally and internationally. Located in downtown Montreal, McGill’s Desautels Faculty of Management brings together a diverse community of scholars whose expertise contributes to a dynamic and collaborative academic environment. The Faculty offers a personalized and experiential approach to education, emphasizing responsible management by applying interdisciplinary, integrated and globally minded perspectives to teaching, research and partnerships. Faculty members across  Areas  conduct research using a wide range of theoretical and methodological approaches and enjoy a high degree of independence in their research and teaching. The Faculty places strong emphasis on research quality, innovation, and impact, with opportunities to engage in various  research centres and institutes .  Montreal is a vibrant, multilingual city that blends rich history with modern innovation, making it an ideal place to live and work. With its safe neighborhoods, affordable housing, excellent public transit and year-round activities, it’s a welcoming environment for individuals and families. From world-class culture and cuisine to thriving industries and diverse communities, Montreal offers a dynamic lifestyle that’s both enriching and accessible. Discover Montreal       Best cities to study in the world                                     Desautels Faculty of Management Compensation levels are competitive and commensurate with experience. Application process and deadline These materials must be submitted through Workday (all in the same section as you submit your CV) : Cover letter Curriculum vitae Evidence of teaching effectiveness We are not requesting reference letters at this point; reference check is a secondary step which may follow. Any questions about the position or receipt of materials should be addressed to finance.mgmt@mcgill.ca All application materials should be submitted by Sunday, November 1, 2026; applications after the deadline may be considered. The hiring process including the review of application materials, interviews and campus visits with short-listed candidates will be ongoing until the position is filled or the search is terminated. Short-listed candidates invited to meet with members of the Faculty should expect to interview, if health and safety standards allow it, in-person in Montreal. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based. The cover letter in the application may be addressed to: Professors Juan Serpa & David Schumacher Desautels Faculty of Management McGill University 1001 Sherbrooke Street West Montreal, Quebec, Canada H3A 1G5 McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Various/Student-Research-Assistant_JR0000080860-1": {
      "title": "Student Research Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-22\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: School of Communications Sciences and Disorders Position Summary: This job will be paid only for activities related to data entry, organization, and cleaning; transcription and translation; and other data-related tasks.   Duties include:   Read and receive training on measures being used as part of this study; Reviewing and entering data into the appropriate folders owned by the Child Phonology Lab. Organize and clean raw data files; Transcribe audio and video files in Spanish and French into soft word documents and other digitized formats; Translate Spanish and French transcriptions into either language and occasionally into English. Review audio and video data files that will facilitate coding, analysis, and inter-rater reliability; Assist with tasks related to coding, analysis, and inter-rater reliability of study data; Aid with inputting, updating, and organizing data accurately inside spreadsheets and other structures approved by the study’s IRB; Receive training on structures being used for analysis as needed; Meet periodically with doctoral student and/or PI as needed. Qualifications:   Knowledge of Spanish, French, and English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level 4 (qualifier) on a scale of 0-4. Interest in child and language development. Interest in communication sciences and disorders. Fully trilingual in Spanish, English, and French; with and interest in learning about other non-official languages.   TCPS 2: CORE-2022 (Course on Research Ethics) certificate. Awareness or willingness to learn about Latin-American culture and/or populations; including but not limited to language, cultural, immigration, and social issues that impact these communities in Canada. Awareness or willingness to learn about culturally responsive speech-language pathology practice and what it means to enact cultural humility when working with vulnerable populations.   Awareness or willingness to learn about social determinants of health. Awareness or willingness to learn about intersectionality in health practice. Openness to feedback and constructive coaching Implement operational changes and strategic pivots swiftly based on instructions from managers and peers. Objectively recognize personal strengths and skill gaps. Take full ownership of mistakes, identifying them as learning opportunities rather than deflecting blame. Maintain intellectual curiosity; actively invest time in developing new skills to meet changing demands of home visits. Strong belief that abilities and skills can be developed through continuous learning. Flexibility and willingness to step out of comfortable routines to experiment with new processes and adapt systems and processes. Ability to ask clarifying questions and reframe challenging feedback into tangible goals. Ability or willingness to talk and engage with preschoolers who are developing their French Bonuses but not required: Preference will be given to psychology, linguistics, or other related major to Speech-Language Pathology (e.g., education, pre-med, etc.) Current or previous coursework in language development, phonetics, and/or other pre-SLP coursework. Knowledge of French and English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level #4 (qualifier) on a scale of 0-4. Hourly Salary: $19.02 Hours per Week: 11 (Part time) Location: Various Supervisor: Professor Position Start Date: 2026-09-21 Position End Date: 2027-03-31 Deadline to Apply: 2026-09-21 This position is covered by the Association of McGill University Research Employees (AMURE) collective agreement. McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Birks-Building/Grader---RELG-358---Fall-2026_JR0000080921-1": {
      "title": "Grader - RELG 358 - Fall 2026",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-22\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). The School of Religious Studies is recruiting one Grader for RELG 358: Religion and Cinema in India, for the fall 2026 semester. Course Description: This class offers a survey of major 20th century films from India, ranging from early devotionals and Bollywood mythologicals to art house and parallel cinema masterpieces to explore key themes in the history and religion of India with a focus on 20th century developments. Thematic topics covered include identity, gender, visual culture, and representation in the study of modern South Asian religions through a focus on India in the twentieth century through the lens of film. Discussion focuses on a range of themes such as nationalism, devotion, and secularism. This class has no pre-requisites and assumes no prior knowledge. Job Description: Weekly grading of in-class writing assignments (53 submissions) and entering grades Weekly entry of student contribution to oral discussion in MyCourses register End of term grading of final projects and enter grades (submission on Dec 1, grades due on Dec 8) Assist with assigning and entering grades for Engagement 1 (mid-term time) and Engagement 2 (end of term) Assist with assigning and entering grades for In-Class Writing 1 (mid-term time) and In-class Writing 2 (end of term) Qualifications: MA or Ph.D. student, ideally has taken RELG 358 in the past or prior coursework or familiarity with South Asian Studies.  Hiring Unit: School of Religious Studies Course Title: RELG 358 - Religion and Cinema in India Estimated Number of Positions: 1 Estimated number of hours per term: 30 hours Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Hourly Salary: $25.00 Position Start Date: 2026-11-15 Position End Date: 2026-12-08 Deadline to Apply: 2026-09-24 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Various/Casual-Research-Assistant-1_JR0000080889": {
      "title": "Casual Research Assistant 1",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-22\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: School of Physical & Occupational Therapy Position Summary: Timothy Wideman, Associate Professor at McGill University, is seeking a highly motivated Research Assistant to work on a CIHR-funded, research project that aims to improve entry-level pain education across Canadian physiotherapy (PT) programs. Under the direction of the Lab Manager and Principal Investigator, the incumbent will assist with the planning, coordination and administration of a national pilot randomized controlled trial and other projects/studies as needed. The incumbent will also attend lab meetings, project meetings and participate in training sessions. This is a part-time casual position (10-20 hours/week), with duties including, but not limited to assisting the lab manager with :   The planning and administration of educational modules and a national competency assessment in PT pain management Scheduling different parties involved in the educational sessions and assessments (e.g., simulated patient actors, facilitators, students) Scheduling rehearsal and training sessions Developing and maintaining detailed schedules for each assessment session Preparing and updating study documents Ensuring all parties receive the required documentation and information for each assessment session Data collection; including facilitating the virtual simulation-based assessment Coordinating the work of assessment evaluators Compensating participants for their time Organizing the data and datasets Quality control – ensure those facilitating the assessment have submitted all required files, identify missing data and taking the necessary steps to recover files  Organizing video/audio recordings from the assessment sessions Maintaining participant confidentiality by properly securing all study documents and keeping participant names or other identifiable information separate from study ID numbers at all times   Creating and administering surveys using LimeSurvey and compiling results Ethics renewals/amendments Assisting as needed on other projects/studies Clear and timely communication across stakeholders Qualifications: The ideal candidate will be able to demonstrate the following qualities and skills: Bilingual (Fluency in French and English) – Strong written and verbal communication skills are essential Knowledge of French and English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing.The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level # 4 on a scale of 0-4 Excellent organizational and time management skills Excellent interpersonal and communication skills Demonstrated ability to work autonomously and in a team High level of flexibility, adaptability, and attention to details Meticulous Strong ability to prioritize multiple tasks and meet time sensitive deadlines The ideal candidates will have the following experience/credentials: Applicants must be pursuing or hold a minimum undergraduate degree relevant to the position Previous work experience in a similar role is an asset Experience working in clinical health research with human subjects preferred Superior computer skills including a good knowledge of Microsoft’s Office suite (Word, Excel, PowerPoint, MS Teams, including OneDrive) Experience using database management software (e.g., SPSS, Redcap, Limesurvey) Experience using online video conferencing platforms such as Zoom Home office requirements: e.g. access to your own personal computer with microphone and camera, reliable and fast internet connection, ability to download Zoom application and recordings. Knowledge of French and English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level #4 on a scale of 0-4. Hourly Salary: $30.54 Hours per Week: 20 (Part time) Location: Various Supervisor: Associate Professor Position Start Date: 2026-09-21 Position End Date: 2027-11-01 Deadline to Apply: 2026-09-30 This position is covered by the Association of McGill University Research Employees (AMURE) collective agreement. McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Various/Research-Assistant-2_JR0000080918": {
      "title": "Research Assistant 2",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-22\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit : Institute of Health Sciences Education Position Summary: Working as a member of Dr. Monica Molinaro’s research team, the research assistant will work on research projects, where duties will include qualitative data collection and management, data analyses, analytics, reporting, and knowledge translation activities for health professions and health sciences education, health care systems, and health care policy research. In particular, Dr. Molinaro’s research aims to understand experiences of moral distress in health care providers. Dr. Molinaro is seeking a motivated and energetic team player with excellent research skills. Description of duties: The research assistant will provide support of a routine nature related to the collection and review of data for research projects. Duties will also include tasks to support the conduct of research projects, such as data collection, data management, and qualitative analyses, as well as additional duties related to conducting qualitative research as required.   Accountabilities to Dr. Molinaro may include: Participate in qualitative coding and analysis under direction of supervisor, prepare summaries. Assist with obtaining data and information from databases for others to use in the development of presentations, scientific papers and reports. Seek guidance and direction from Dr. Molinaro.   Qualifications: Education: Bachelor’s degree or equivalent in a health professions/health sciences education related field.     Experience: C ompletion of in a research methods course that addresses qualitative research methodology, and previous qualitative research experience is required.   Additional Assets: -       Experience with qualitative research skills (transcription, conduct interviews, focus groups, clean and code qualitative data) -       Experience with knowledge translation (synthesize research knowledge and develop plain language research summaries for research projects) Ability to balance multiple tasks, projects and competing priorities Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Knowledge of French and English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level #4(qualifier) on a scale of 0-4. Hourly Salary: $32.15 Hours per Week: 10 (Part time) Location: Various Supervisor: Assistant Professor Position Start Date: 2026-09-22 Position End Date: 2027-09-25 Deadline to Apply: 2026-09-29 This position is covered by the Association of McGill University Research Employees (AMURE) collective agreement. McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Education-Building/Office-Assistant-C_JR0000081077-1": {
      "title": "Office Assistant C",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-24\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Dean's Office Position Summary: Visit and trip coordination: Assist with the planning and coordination of faculty and leadership visits to China, including itineraries, meetings, institutional visits, schedules, and related logistics. Preparation of materials: Prepare and organize briefing notes, background information, meeting materials, presentations, agendas, and other documents for meetings and visits. Partnership support: Conduct background research on partner institutions and assist with the coordination and documentation of existing and prospective partnerships. MoU and agreement support: Assist with the preparation, review, formatting, and English-Chinese translation of Memoranda of Understanding (MoUs), agreements, and related correspondence and materials. Communication and correspondence: Assist with English- and Chinese-language communications with partner institutions, including drafting or translating emails and other correspondence. Program and event support: Provide organizational support for China-related academic programs, delegation visits, workshops, meetings, and other events. Undertake other related tasks in support of China-related programs and global engagement initiatives as needed. Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities Strong proficiency in both Chinese and English, including excellent written communication skills; Strong research skills to conduct literature review, need assessment, program evaluation, exploratory applied research; Strong organizational skills and attention to detail; Ability to communicate professionally with faculty members, administrators, students, and external partners; Ability to work independently, manage multiple tasks, and meet deadlines; Familiarity with the education context in China; Experience with translation, international academic partnerships, event coordination, or administrative support is an asset. Education/experience requirements: Master’s degree in education related programs   Additional qualifying skills and abilities: Teaching experience in mainland China and/or Hong Kong Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level # (qualifier) on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: High School Diploma 2 Years Related Experience Hourly Salary: (AMUSE C) $30.00 Estimated Number of Work Hours per Week: 15 (Part time) Position Start Date: 2026-10-05 Position End Date: 2027-07-31 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-01 This position is covered by the AMUSE collective agreement. McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Music-527-Sherbrooke/Audiovisual-Assistant-B--CIRMMT-_JR0000080658": {
      "title": "Audiovisual Assistant B (CIRMMT)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-24\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: CIRMMT Position Summary: Under the direction of the immediate supervisor, the incumbent may perform some or all of the listed typical functions in the context of the installation and operation of equipment and infrastructure necessary for CIRMMT productions and events (concerts, lectures, seminars, workshops, special events). Primary Duties: Greets users and answers specific inquiries with a positive attitude. This includes detailed information regarding use of equipment. Sets up props and/or A/V equipment and records events such as lectures, concerts, workshops, and meetings. Schedule audio/video equipment and rooms to record events. Maintains work area and spaces. The list of functions outlined above is representative and not a complete and detailed list of tasks which may be performed by an incumbent. For example, incumbents may be asked to perform some elements of Audio/Video Assistant level “A” tasks in addition to the above listed functions. Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities High School Diploma and / or equivalent professional experience. Availability for necessary events. Bilingual in French and English Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level # (qualifier) on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: High School Diploma 1 Year Related Experience Hourly Salary: (AMUSE B) $21.00 Estimated Number of Work Hours per Week: 6 (Part time) Position Start Date: 2026-10-05 Position End Date: 2027-09-30 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-07 This position is covered by the AMUSE collective agreement. McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
