    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/McIntyre--Medical/Teaching-Assistant---Drugs-and-Disease---PHAR-301---Winter-2027_JR0000081148": {
      "title": "Teaching Assistant - Drugs and Disease - PHAR 301 - Winter 2027",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-25\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Department of Pharmacology & Therapeutics Course Title: Drugs and Disease Course Code: PHAR 301 Estimated Number of Positions: 2 Total Hours of Work per Term: 70 Position Summary: Help with posting files to myCourses website for the course. Providing individualized assistance to students having difficulty with the course material, including small group tutorial sessions. Setting up and running exam review sessions, as required. Answering student e-mail inquiries. Monitoring the myCourse forum and managing the questions from students. Grade short answer-question exams. Qualifications: Graduate student who has successfully completed PHAR 301 with a very good grade, or has a very good background knowledge in Pharmacology. Must have sufficient command of English to be able to help international students. Very good communication skills and attention to detail.   Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Hourly Salary: $38.46 Position Start Date: 2027-01-05 Position End Date: 2027-04-30 Deadline to Apply: 2026-11-01 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Duff-Medical-Bldg/Course-Lecturer---BMDE-656---Medical-Device-Development-Process_JR0000081083": {
      "title": "Course Lecturer - BMDE 656 - Medical Device Development Process",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-25\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). COURSE DESCRIPTION The Department of Biomedical Engineering of the Faculty of Medicine, McGill University, is seeking applications for a Course Lecturer for the 2026-2027 academic year to teach BMDE 656 Medical Device Development Process (3 credits) . Course overview: Today’s development of medical devices must be performed following existing standards such as ISO 13485:2016 for Europe, 21CFR 820 Quality System Regulation of the FDA (U.S. Food & Drug Administration), and MDSAP (international Medical Device Single Audit Program). These regulations and standards ensure that medical devices are developed in a controlled manner to ensure efficacy and safety for the patient and user. The development projects are milestone-driven and follow a structured development path. Overview of all the development steps of a medical device, from a “back-of-the-napkin” idea or concept to subsequent use in humans. Discussion of the development of an existing medical device, review of all steps of the design process, including production of the required documentation in order to be able to complete a submission to a regulatory agency. RESPONSIBILITIES Preparing the students with the required knowledge, facilitating classroom discussions, assessing and evaluating student performance and preparing students to be successful leaders in their profession. OTHER MAIN RESPONSIBILITIES INCLUDE: •              Preparation of course lectures and teaching materials •              Preparation of assignments, tests and grading rubrics •              Participation in curricular discussions •              Hold regular office hours Education/ Éducation Bachelor of Science, Engineering, or Arts Experience/ Expérience More than 5 years in medical device reimbursement Other/ Autre Fluency in English and French (written and verbal) preferred This posting will expire at 00:00 am the day of the deadline to apply. You have until midnight the day before the deadline to apply to submit your application. Disclaimer Please note that, government directives permitting, it is anticipated that most courses will be taught in person. Should government restrictions prohibit in-person teaching, courses will revert to remote teaching. In the event of such a change, Course Lecturers will be advised without delay. Course Lecturers may be required to prepare and deliver teaching material suited to a remote/online teaching environment using a virtual learning platform (e.g., Zoom, WebEx) and the McGill myCourses learning management system. Training and assistance on remote teaching is available through Teaching and Learning Services (TLS) . Course Lecturers are encouraged to record their lectures. Hiring Unit: Biomedical Engineering Course Title: Medical Device Development Process Subject Code: BMDE 656 1 Location: Duff Medical Building, Rm 321 Schedule: Wednesdays 16:05-18:55 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-14 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Duff-Medical-Bldg/Course-Lecturer--BMDE-653---Patents-in-Biomedical-Engineering_JR0000081131": {
      "title": "Course Lecturer: BMDE 653 - Patents in Biomedical Engineering",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-25\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). The Department of Biomedical Engineering of the Faculty of Medicine, McGill University, is seeking applications for a Course Lecturer for the 2026-2027 academic year to teach Patents in Biomedical Engineering under the Graduate Certificate Program in Translational Biomedical Engineering. This course provides an overview of intellectual property, patents and the patents process. The course also provides insights into the strategies relating to commercialization and exploiting of patents, as well as enforcing patents. Responsibilities Preparing the students with the required knowledge, facilitating classroom discussions, assessing and evaluating student performance and preparing students to be successful leaders in their profession. Other main responsibilities include: Preparation of course lectures and teaching materials Preparation of assignments, tests and grading rubrics Participation in curricular discussions Hold regular office hours Teaching Qualification Requirements Experience in drafting and prosecuting patents, as well as freedom to operate, in different jurisdiction Education/Éducation Bachelor in Engineering Experience/Expérience Minimum 5 years Other/Autre Fluency in English and French (written and verbal) preferred. This posting will expire at 00:00 am the day of the deadline to apply. You have until midnight the day before the deadline to apply to submit your application.   Disclaimer Please note that given the resumption of in-person teaching activities, most courses will be taught in person. Exams may however need to be administered remotely depending, mainly on the size of the class. Course Lecturers will be required to plan accordingly. Should government restrictions prohibit the resumption of in person teaching, courses will revert to remote teaching. In the event of such a change, Course Lecturers will be advised without delay. Course Lecturers may be required to prepare and deliver teaching material suited to a remote/online teaching environment using a virtual learning platform [e.g., Zoom, WebEx] and the McGill myCourses learning management system. Training and assistance on remote teaching is available through Teaching and Learning Services (TLS). Course Lecturers are encouraged to record their lectures. Hiring Unit: Biomedical Engineering Course Title: Patents in Biomedical Engineering Subject Code: BMDE 1 Location: Duff Medical Building Schedule: Tuesdays and Thursdays 16:06-17:25 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-14 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/McIntyre--Medical/Teaching-Assistant---Introduction-to-Pharmacology-2---PHAR-201---Winter-2027_JR0000081094-1": {
      "title": "Teaching Assistant - Introduction to Pharmacology 2 - PHAR 201 - Winter 2027",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-25\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Department of Pharmacology & Therapeutics Course Title: Introduction to Pharmacology 2 Course Code: PHAR 201 Estimated Number of Positions: 2 Total Hours of Work per Term: 45 Position Summary: Attendance at classes. Running tutorials. Setting up and invigilating exams. Marking of midterm and final exams. Review of exams with students. Help with posting files to myCourses website for the course.       .      Qualifications: Graduate student who has successfully passed PHAR 562 and PHAR 563 or the equivalent.  Must have sufficient command of English to be able to help international students.  Very good communication skills and attention to detail.  Must be comfortable in marking the midterm and final short answer questions.   Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Hourly Salary: $38.46 Position Start Date: 2027-01-05 Position End Date: 2027-04-30 Deadline to Apply: 2026-11-01 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Raymond-Building/Assistant-Professor-in-Regulated-Plant-Pests----Canada-Impact--Emerging-Leaders_JR0000081085": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Regulated Plant Pests -  Canada Impact+ Emerging Leaders",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-25\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\n​ Please refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Canada Impact+ Emerging Leaders (McGill University) Tenure-Track Faculty Position Open to rank of Assistant Professor in Regulated Plant Pests   Program Description   The Canada Impact+ Emerging Leaders program is a one-time initiative designed to support institutions in recruiting internationally based Early Career Researchers to Canada. Emerging Leaders will receive research funding and institutional support to advance transformative research in Canada’s strategic priority areas , build partnerships across sectors and borders, and translate their research into applications and impacts that benefit Canadians and the world.   The Faculty of Agricultural and Environmental Sciences at McGill University invites applications for a full-time tenure-track Assistant Professor position to advance transformational research programs that support the growth of Canada’s global leadership in the following strategic priority area:   Environment, climate resilience, and the Arctic   Emerging Leaders must have an independent program of research that demonstrates strategic alignment with this priority area. The award provides $100,000 per year for six years to McGill University to support the salary costs associated with the successful Emerging Leader appointment as well as direct research expenses, with the possibility of extension for a further six years.   Successful candidates will be expected to begin a full-time tenure-track appointment at the rank of Assistant Professor no later than June 28, 2027 , in the Department of Plant Science.   Job Duties   The successful candidate specializes in the study of insects, pathogens, nematodes, or invasive weeds that are legally restricted by governments to protect agriculture and ecosystems. The successful candidate will develop an internationally recognized, externally funded research program focused on the biology, diagnostics, epidemiology, management or responses to environmental and climate change of regulated pests, invasive species, or quarantine organisms that affect agricultural crops in Canada.   The successful candidate is expected to teach core courses [such as an introductory course related to plant pests (e.g., Insect Biology), Pesticides in Agriculture] in the Faculty’s undergraduate programs and a graduate course related to Integrated Pest Management. In addition to teaching, the candidate will be expected to conduct innovative research in regulated plant pests, making full use of the extensive research infrastructure at the Macdonald Campus of McGill University, more specifically the Plant Pest Containment (PPC) Laboratory, which is part of the Eastern Canadian Plant Phenotyping Platform (ECP3). The ideal candidate will bridge the gap between academic research and regulatory science, collaborating closely with government agencies, industry stakeholders, and international plant health organizations.   The candidate should also actively pursue collaborative research with other departments and schools within the Faculty of Agricultural and Environmental Sciences to foster interdisciplinary approaches. Participation in Department, Faculty, and University committees is expected, as are interactions with provincial and federal organizations and professional societies.   Qualifications and Education Requirement s   Only candidates who were internationally based, both working and residing outside Canada, on June 28, 2026, are eligible.   Candidates must be within five years following the start date of their first research-related appointment, minus the length of any eligible delays in research. Recent graduates and postdoctoral fellows may also be eligible.   Ph.D. in Plant Health, Plant Biology, Integrated Pest Management or related field; track record and demonstrated excellence in working in high-containment facilities (Biosafety Level 2 or 3) handling quarantine or regulated pests; knowledge of Canadian plant protection legislation, the Plant Protection Act, and international phytosanitary standards (ISPMs).   McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. The ability to teach and communicate in French and prior experience with the Canadian agricultural and agri-food industry are considered an asset.   Research-related appointments are defined as those where the individual has the autonomy to conduct research independently.   Eligible leaves, including maternity, parental, medical, and bereavement leave, are credited as twice the amount of time taken. Professional leaves, including training, sabbatical, and administrative leave, are not credited.   A PhD is required by McGill University for appointment to a tenure-track position; however, it is not a requirement of the Impact+ Emerging Leaders Program itself.   Candidates should review the eligibility and selection criteria for the Impact+ Program prior to applying.     Canadians living abroad who wish to relocate to Canada are encouraged to apply. Faculty/Department/Unit: Faculty of Agricultural and Environmental Sciences / Department of Plant Science Job Type: Tenure-track Rank: Assistant Professor Salary: Salary will be commensurate with qualifications and experience. Posting Period: Please submit your application by October 30, 2026. Required Documents: The application package should include the documents listed below and must be merged into one single PDF file not exceeding 5 MB.   Cover letter Curriculum vitae  Statement of research describing current and future research, alignment with the strategic priority area, and potential for knowledge translation, mobilization, and application of research results (maximum 2 pages) Research infrastructure needs: laboratory space, equipment (max. 1 page) Teaching statement (max. 1 page) and dossier Contact information for three references    Application Process   Applications must be submitted through Workday, McGill’s HR management system.   Posting Period   Review of applications will start on November 1, 2026. Inquiries from potential candidates are welcome and will be treated in strict confidence. Correspondence can be directed to Prof. Martin Filion ( martin.filion@mcgill.ca ), chair of the search committee. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located.   Commitment to Equity and Diversity   Please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University offers immigration and relocation support for selected candidates . McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing.   McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities.   At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry.   McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record.   McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, Catherine Roy accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca .   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/McConnell-Eng-Bldg/Course-Lecturer-BIEN-267--W27_JR0000081062": {
      "title": "Course Lecturer BIEN 267- W27",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-25\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit : Department of Bioengineering Position : Course Lecturer Course code and name : BIEN 267 - Bioanalytical Methods in Bioengineering Main tasks The course lecturer will be responsible for the teaching, course management, and grading activities in the course. The course is a survey of qualitative and quantitative methods of analysis in Chemistry, Biochemistry and Molecular Biology and their relevance to bioengineering. The course has wet lab tutorial sections. Teaching Qualification Requirements - Ph.D. in a relevant field (such as biophysics, biochemistry...) with preference for a degree in engineering. - Direct experience in molecular biology, including analytical process, chemical equilibrium, basic spectroscopy, chromatography and separation methods, metabolite and protein extraction, protein purification methods and quantitative determination, high-performance liquid chromatography, western blotting.  - Experience in teaching in a higher education setting - Knowledge of spoken and written English Semester : Winter 2027 - January 5, 2027 to April 14, 2027 3 Credit course Location: MDHARG-10 Schedule : T 11.35 am-12.25.pm ​ Salary : $11,824 Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Hiring Unit: Department of Bioengineering Course Title: Bioanalytical Methods in Bioengineering Subject Code: BIEN 267 Location: Schedule: T 11:35 am-12:25 pm Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-11 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/McConnell-Eng-Bldg/Course-Lecturer-BIEN-280-W27_JR0000081069": {
      "title": "Course Lecturer BIEN 280-W27",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-25\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit : Department of Bioengineering Position : Course Lecturer Course Code and Title : BIEN 280 - Thermodynamics in Bioengineering Position and course description: The candidate will be responsible for the teaching, course management, and grading activities in the course. Applicants must be familiar with the course content: Internal Energy, Work, The three Laws of Thermodynamics, Enthalpy, and Entropy. Basic concepts of energy in living organisms: distribution of energy, energy conservation in living organisms, isothermal systems, Gibbs free energy in chemical coupling, reversible processes and redox reactions. Application of Thermodynamics in defining biological processes and components such as hydrolysis, osmosis, ELISA, PCR, DNA, amino acids, proteins. Teaching Qualification Requirements: Ph.D. in Engineering or the Natural Sciences At least 1 year teaching experience Professional Engineer (P.Eng.) license required (or in progress). Expertise in fundamentals of thermodynamics, physical and chemical equilibrium. Knowledge of biological systems and/or biochemistry desired. Demonstrated knowledge of spoken and written English. Strong problem‐solving skills and flexible approach to teaching. Understanding of cross‐cultural practices Semester : Winter 2027- January 5, 2027 to April 14, 2027 Location: ENGMD 279  - M 04:35 pm-06:25 pm Schedule : TR 10.05 am-11.25.pm ​ Salary : $11,824 Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Hiring Unit: Department of Bioengineering Course Title: Thermodynamics in Bioengineering Subject Code: BIEN 280 Location: ENGMD 279 Schedule: TR 10:05 am-11:25 am Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-16 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/McConnell-Eng-Bldg/Course-Lecturer-BIEN-505-W27_JR0000081012": {
      "title": "Course Lecturer BIEN 505-W27",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-25\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit : Department of Bioengineering Position : Course Lecturer Course code and name : BIEN 505 - Medical Technology Innovation and Development . Main tasks The course lecturer will be responsible for the teaching, course management, and grading activities in the course. The course allows for the introduction of new topics in the field of Bioengineering Teaching Qualification Requirements ·         Ph.D. in Engineering or the Natural Sciences ·         1 year teaching experience ·         Professional Engineer (P.Eng.) license required (or in progress) ·         Knowledge of spoken and written English Semester : Winter 2027 - January 5, 2027 to April 14, 2027 3 Credit course Location: WONG 1030 Schedule : M 11.35 am-02.25.pm ​ Salary : $11,824 Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Hiring Unit: Department of Bioengineering Course Title: Medical Technology Innovation and Development Subject Code: BIEN 505 1,2 Location: Wong 1030 Schedule: M 11:35 am-02:25 pm Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-11 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
