    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Dawson-Hall/Academic-Affairs-Administrator_JR0000080580": {
      "title": "Academic Affairs Administrator",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-25\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Position Summary: Under the supervision of the Academic Affairs Officer in the Dean's Office, the Academic Affairs Administrator provides guidance and support to academic staff, academic leaders, and administrative staff across the Faculty to facilitate the recruitment and ongoing support of academic staff throughout all stages of their careers. The incumbent also collaborates with partners across the University to identify and implement best practices and standardize services across all ASCs, ensuring consistency, equity, compliance, and excellence in service delivery. Primary Responsibilities: Guide and provide support to faculty members and administrative staff with respect to the interpretation and application of university regulations, academic policies and procedures and collective agreements governing the entire academic lifecycle.  Review, evaluate and troubleshoot complex requests, and provide guidance on confidential matters including; academic recruitment process, academic appointments, compensation, leaves, employment changes, termination, retirement and other issues related to academic positions.  Foster and maintain excellent working relationships with stakeholders. Communicate and collaborate with the ASCs and the Academic Personnel Office (APO) to ensure that standards are met, and that information and record-keeping is complete, consistent, and transparent to all.  Monitor and initiate academic appointments, reappointments, and costing allocations for personal support awards, salaries, and stipends, according to policies and procedures.  Assist with the annual academic salary policy exercise, the review of sabbatical requests and applications for promotions to full professor.  Provide information to administrators and faculty members on eligibility requirements and application process, and review submissions for completeness and compliance with applicable policies and procedures. Contribute to the seamless operation of the Dean’s Office by providing administrative support, coordinating logistics, maintaining records, and serving as backup coverage as required. Perform other related duties as required. This position may be required to move from one area to another from time to time for leave replacements, special projects, and to address increases in workload. Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities: Strong analytical, organizational, and communication skills, with the ability to interpret and apply policies, procedures, collective agreements, and regulatory requirements while exercising sound judgment and ensuring compliance. Demonstrated ability to manage competing priorities, coordinate schedules and meetings, review complex documentation with accuracy, and provide clear guidance to diverse stakeholders in a fast-paced environment. Proficient in Microsoft Office (Word, Excel, and Outlook), bilingual in English and French, and knowledge of McGill systems including Banner (FIS), Minerva, Workday, and MMP an asset. As one of Montreal's Top Employers, here is what we offer: Competitive benefits package (Health, Dental, Life Insurance) (if eligible) Defined contribution pension plan (with employer contribution up to 10%) (if eligible) Group Registered Retirement Savings Plan (RRSP) and Tax Free Savings Account (TFSA) Competitive vacation policy Two (2) personal days Two (2) floating holidays Nine (9) \"Summer Fridays\" - paid days off between the St-Jean Baptiste holiday and Labour Day Paid time off over the December holiday period Tuition waiver for regular employees and their dependents Up to two (2) days of remote work per week where the position permits Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level # (qualifier) on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: DEC III 3 Years Related Experience / Annual Salary: (MPEX Grade 03) $63,350.00 - $74,530.00 - $85,710.00 Job Profile: MPEX-HRM1G - Academic Affairs - Professional 1 (Excluded) Hours per Week: 33.75 (Full time) Supervisor: Academic Affairs Officer Position End Date (If applicable): Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-11 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/McIntyre--Medical/Teaching-Assistant---Drug-Discovery-and-Development-3---PHAR-508---Winter-2027_JR0000081193-1": {
      "title": "Teaching Assistant - Drug Discovery and Development 3 - PHAR 508 - Winter 2027",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-25\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Department of Pharmacology & Therapeutics Course Title: Drug Discovery and Development 3 Course Code: PHAR 508 Estimated Number of Positions: 1 Total Hours of Work per Term: 35 Position Summary: Tutoring and invigilating exam and student presentations. Reviewing assignments with students as needed. Monitoring and evaluating in-class presentations and discussions. Help with management of myCourses website for the course. Qualifications: Graduate student who has successfully passed PHAR 562 and PHAR 563 or equivalent. Must have sufficient command of English to be able to help international students with writing assignments. Very good communication skills and attention to detail. Knowledge of myCourse s is an asset. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Hourly Salary: $38.46 Position Start Date: 2027-01-05 Position End Date: 2027-04-30 Deadline to Apply: 2026-11-01 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/St-Marys-Hospital/Assistant-Professor--Clinical---Department-of-Medicine--Division-of-Respiratory-Medicine--C1-260921-_JR0000081088": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor (Clinical), Department of Medicine, Division of Respiratory Medicine (C1-260921)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-29\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\n​ Please refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Position Description The McGill University Department of Medicine, Division of Respiratory Medicine in the Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences invites applications for a full-time Contract Academic Staff position as a Clinician-Teacher at the rank of Assistant Professor (Clinical) based at St. Mary’s Hospital Centre. This will be a Contract Academic Staff position and does not confer eligibility for tenure. This position is conditional on the Institution successfully obtaining a PEM (i.e. physician permit) for this position. The Department of Medicine is a leader in academic medicine. Our educational and research missions support excellent and innovative clinical programs across our teaching hospitals. The St. Mary’s Hospital Centre is one of McGill’s major teaching hospitals affiliated with the Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences at Canada’s internationally renowned McGill University, located in the vibrant and multicultural city of Montreal, in the province of Quebec. The Respiratory Service at St. Mary’s Hospital Centre serves the community through a strong commitment to clinical care and teaching. Department members are supported in clinical research and administrative activities. Medical education is a central component of St. Mary’s academic mission. St. Mary’s Hospital Centre is an acute care community hospital located in the heart of Montréal. Job Duties The successful candidate will be appointed to clinical practice in the Department of Medicine, Division Respiratory Medicine at Saint-Mary’s Hospital Centre. Please see the Department of Medicine (DOM) website for McGill DOM Career path definitions . Our career path definitions specify typical ranges of time allotted to clinical work, teaching (clinical and didactic), research and administration. As a Clinician-Teacher, your major non-clinical focus will be on teaching the next generation of physicians, including both didactic and clinical teaching.    Clinical duties will include regular scheduled outpatient clinics and in‑patient care, including participation in the on‑call rotation as a respiratory consultant and supervision of learners on the Internal Medicine clinical teaching units. Outpatient activities will include participation in a rapid investigation service and the management of sleep disorders. Academic responsibilities will include active participation in the Faculty’s core teaching programs.   The successful candidate will also be expected to be actively involved in all aspects of McGill’s academic mission (i.e., research, teaching, supervision of graduate students and residents, and involvement in academic and administrative committees).   McGill faculty members are expected to contribute to service activities within their units, the University, and the wider scholarly community. A demonstrated commitment to equity, diversity, and inclusion is also expected.   Qualifications and Education Requirements Candidates must: Hold an MD or MDCM degree and be eligible for licensure in the province of Québec. Have completed subspecialty training in Respiratory Medicine (Pulmonology). Be certified by the Royal College of Physicians and Surgeons of Canada (or equivalent). Be certified by the Collège des médecins du Québec. Have completed at least one additional year of advanced clinical and/or research training, including training in endobronchial ultrasound–guided sampling (EBUS). Have completed advanced training and demonstrated competence in invasive pleural procedures. Have completed, or be currently enrolled in, a Master’s degree (or equivalent) in Medical Education, with a demonstrated commitment to pursuing an academic career focused on teaching. A clinical interest in interstitial lung disease will be considered an asset. Demonstrate proficiency in English and French, or a commitment to acquiring the language skills necessary to practice medicine in Québec; candidates must be able to interact effectively in both languages with patients, families, and health‑care professionals. Faculty/Department/Unit: Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences, Department of Medicine, Division of Respiratory Medicine ​ Employee Type: Contract Academic Staff ('Academic Contractual') Rank: Assistant Professor Salary: Remuneration will be according to Quebec health care fee schedule Job Status: Full-time Posting Period: Please submit your application within 30 days of date of posting The following supporting documents are required: a cover letter & curriculum vitae (as 1 pdf document) A statement indicating your academic plans if you are successful in obtaining this position. Indicate your planned career path as a Clinician-Teacher. Describe your preferred research time allocation and detail your teaching plans, research plans and plans for service to the academic community; The names and contact information of three referees. Use a personal email address when creating an account in Workday to submit your application. Do not use @mail.mcgill.ca or @mcgill.ca email accounts to apply.   McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing.   L’Université McGill est une institution de langue anglaise dans laquelle la majorité des activités d’enseignement et de recherche s’effectuent en langue anglaise, rendant essentielle l’utilisation de l’anglais pour la communication orale et écrite. McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Brown-Student-Serv/Case-Manager-Crisis-Response_JR0000081293-1": {
      "title": "Case Manager Crisis Response",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-29\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Position Summary: Reporting directly to the Associate Director, Suicide Prevention and Crisis Management, the Case Manager in the Office of the Dean of Students (ODOS), Crisis Response (CMCR) plays a key role in advancing and implementing McGill’s Student Suicide Prevention Framework. Working closely with the Associate Director, the Crisis Response Team, and campus and community partners, the CMCR supports institutional efforts that strengthen suicide prevention, early identification, coordinated care, crisis intervention, and postvention across the University.   The CMCR position is recognized by the University as a first point of contact for students experiencing academic or non-academic difficulties, crisis situations, and other complex concerns. The incumbent assesses and intervenes in crisis situations to ensure student safety and provides confidential, non-judgmental, short-term support to students throughout their academic journey. The role supports students in navigating available resources, facilitates referrals to appropriate campus and community services, and regularly consults with the Associate Director and the Crisis Response Team to ensure coordinated and effective case management. The incumbent collaborates with stakeholders across and beyond the University to promote student well-being, academic success, and access to support.   In support of the Student Suicide Prevention Framework, the CMCR develops and delivers training for students, faculty, staff, and key stakeholders on suicide prevention, recognizing and responding to student distress, crisis intervention, and referral pathways. The incumbent also provides consultation and support to faculties in the development and implementation of postvention protocols following student deaths or other critical incidents. In collaboration with the Associate Director and the Crisis Response Team, the CMCR contributes to the development, implementation, and ongoing evaluation of procedures, protocols, and best practices related to suicide prevention, and crisis management, helping to foster a coordinated, proactive, and evidence-informed culture of care across the University. Primary Responsibilities: Collaborates with the Associate Director in the implementation and ongoing evaluation of McGill's Student Suicide Prevention Framework. Develops and delivers training for faculty, staff, and key stakeholders on suicide prevention, student distress recognition, crisis response, and referral pathways. Provides consultation and support to faculties in the development and implementation of postvention protocols following student deaths or critical incidents. Supports institutional initiatives aimed at strengthening suicide prevention, early intervention, and coordinated care across the university. Contributes to the development of protocols, procedures, and best practices related to crisis response, and suicide prevention. Assess the situation of a student and can determine if there are any safety risks to self or others. Manages student cases, which can range from complex issues surrounding academics, disruptive behaviors, or crisis, to very high-risk crisis situations. Collaborates with the Case Manager Crisis Response Team regularly regarding students of concern or in need of academic guidance.  Ensures regular follow-up with students in need while respecting the limitations of the Case Manager Crisis Response role. Ensures that student files are properly documented and that notes are always up to date. Maintains confidentiality of student files and all information gathered. Collaborate with University stakeholders to facilitate referrals to appropriate internal support services and takes initiative by collaborating and mobilizing these services to help students in the best way possible, while considering the limitations and realities of the resources available. Consults regularly with the Associate Director. Participates in ODoS weekly team meetings. Actively participates in the weekly Crisis Response Team meetings led by the Associate Director. Shows leadership and confidence when organizing and leading meetings with faculty members regarding students of concern. Takes initiative in working directly with hospitals or other external support resources for students at a high risk for suicide when required. Builds and maintains positive collaborative relationships with various internal stakeholders who are closely connected to Student Life and Learning. As required, participates in the development of inclusive policies, protocols, procedures, workshops or trainings. As required, deliver workshops to faculty and staff members regarding how to detect students of concern, in crisis or suicide danger, and referral pathways. Engages in professional development activities when required. Remain current on best practices related to case management, threat assessment, and crisis intervention and emerging research.   Actively participates in Performance Dialogues 2-3 times a year lead by the Associate Director. Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities As a Case Manager Crisis Response, you will have the ability to: Multi-task in a high-pressured environment Weigh competing demands and exercise discretionary decision-making in crisis and high-risk crisis situations Work with a team Respond to situations creatively and proactively, and take initiative to support students adequately Represent students’ needs Deal with ambiguity and manage priorities and tight timelines The ideal candidate will demonstrate competence in crisis intervention, suicide crisis intervention prevention and postvention, as well as a knowledge of mental health crisis interventions and case management in a university context. As one of Montreal's Top Employers, here is what we offer: Competitive benefits package (Health, Dental, Life Insurance) (if eligible) Defined contribution pension plan (with employer contribution up to 10%) (if eligible) Group Registered Retirement Savings Plan (RRSP) and Tax Free Savings Account (TFSA) Competitive vacation policy Two (2) personal days Two (2) floating holidays Nine (9) \"Summer Fridays\" - paid days off between the St-Jean Baptiste holiday and Labour Day Paid time off over the December holiday period Tuition waiver for regular employees and their dependents Up to two (2) days of remote work per week where the position permits Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level #4 on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: Bachelor's Degree 3 Years Related Experience / Annual Salary: (MPEX Grade 05) $73,470.00 - $91,840.00 - $110,210.00 Job Profile: MPEX-SAF2B - Student Services - Professional 2 Hours per Week: 33.75 (Full time) Supervisor: Associate Director Suicide Prevention and Crisis Management Position End Date (If applicable): 2028-11-30 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-13 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Sherbrooke-680/Instructional-Designer_JR0000081343": {
      "title": "Instructional Designer",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-29\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Position Summary: Reporting to the Associate Director of Digital Learning, the Instructional Designer designs and contributes to the development of online courses and programs and supports the production and delivery of instructor-led e-learning activities. The incumbent works as part of a team that includes user interface specialists, project managers and academic stakeholders (subject matter expert, academic coordinators and faculty). The School of Continuing Studies provides education and professional development opportunities to adult learners, across a variety of learning modes including in-class, blended, and online learning. Primary Responsibilities: ·Provide professional expertise and advice with respect to online and computer supported teaching and learning (e-learning); ·Participate in needs assessment and contribute to the planning of e-learning projects, including the estimation of anticipated resources and timeline; ·Assist Subject Matter Experts (SME) with guidance for the production of content for online and hybrid courses; ·Advise SMEs and instructors on a variety of relevant instructional and assessment strategies; ·Advise SMEs and instructors on available technologies and their integration within the instructional strategy and learning context; ·Structure, review and edit educational material and propose revisions for improvement; ·Plan and conduct quality assurance assessment of final educational products, establish evaluation metrics and standards. Prepare and conduct focus groups; ·Write, create and adapt reference material such as flowcharts, scripts and storyboards templates for the production of educational content; ·Write and create documentation (guide-sheets, videos, etc.) as well as training and orientation modules for use by SMEs, instructors and learners; ·Deliver and facilitate training workshops and seminars; ·Address requests for assistance and further training referred by academic units; ·Contribute to the development of successful online instructional strategies and models; ·Research and recommend various instruction delivery tools and resources; ·Contribute to the development, implementation, and improvement of standard operation procedures; ·Participates in university committees and working groups; Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities: Master’s degree, an asset. Demonstrated ability to work autonomously and as part of a team with excellent interpersonal skills. Formal training and several years of experience in a field related to instructional design and/or educational technologies. Demonstrated experience in instructional design for online credit courses and professional development training, in a college or university environment with diverse student groups. Ability to apply and integrate learning theories and ADDIE model to instructional projects, specifically as they relate to online learning and the realm of adult learners. Management skills (process/scope management and teamwork) and demonstrated ability to respect deadlines and other project constraints. Ability to work in a team. Mastery of common educational software applications, web-conferencing tools and learning management systems (BrightSpace, Blackboard or Moodle). Ability to provide one-on-one coaching, consulting and group training sessions to clients. Demonstrated experience in training academic personnel in a university environment. Knowledge of French and English. McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. As one of Montreal's Top Employers, here is what we offer: Competitive benefits package (Health, Dental, Life Insurance) (if eligible) Defined contribution pension plan (with employer contribution up to 10%) (if eligible) Group Registered Retirement Savings Plan (RRSP) and Tax Free Savings Account (TFSA) Competitive vacation policy Two (2) personal days Two (2) floating holidays Nine (9) \"Summer Fridays\" - paid days off between the St-Jean Baptiste holiday and Labour Day Paid time off over the December holiday period Tuition waiver for regular employees and their dependents Up to three (3) days of remote work per week where the position permits Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level # (qualifier) on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: Bachelor's Degree 3 Years Related Experience / Annual Salary: (MPEX Grade 05) $73,470.00 - $91,840.00 - $110,210.00 Job Profile: MPEX-SAF2F - Educational Technology - Professional 2 Hours per Week: 33.75 (Full time) Supervisor: Associate Director Digital Learning Position End Date (If applicable): 2027-11-06 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-16 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Macdonald-Eng-Bldg/Course-Lecturer---FACC-501---Winter-2027_JR0000081490": {
      "title": "Course Lecturer - FACC 501 - Winter 2027",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-29\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Course Lecturer - FACC 501 - Winter 2027 Positions announced here are governed by the McGill Course Lecturers and Instructors Union (MCLIU) collective agreement which may be found on McGill's Human Resources website . Candidates are advised to review the agreement prior to applying. Education: Minimum requirement of i) a Masters or  Ph.D. in Engineering,  or ii) a Master’s degree or Ph.D. in Management or Business Administration, and a Bachelor’s degree in Science or Engineering. Experience : Experience as an entrepreneur, founding and/or launching start-up ventures. Other qualification requirement(s) : Membership in Ordre des ingénieurs du Québec Course Credits: 3 Hiring Unit: Faculty of Engineering, Dean’s Office Course Title: Technology Business Plan Project Term : Winter 2027 Location : TBA Schedule: TBA Deadline to apply: October 15, 2026 To apply: please refer to University’s HR website ( https://www.mcgill.ca/hr/careers ). Include CV and MCLIU form ( https://mcliu.ca/course-application-form/?lang=en ). All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. Hiring Unit: Faculty of Engineering, Dean’s Office Course Title: Technology Business Plan Project Subject Code: FACC 501 001 Location: TBA Schedule: TBA Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-15 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/JGH---Jewish-General-Hosp/Assistant-or-Associate-Professor--Clinical---Department-of-Obstetrics-and-Gynecology--Jewish-General-Hospital--C1-260925-_JR0000081402": {
      "title": "Assistant or Associate Professor (Clinical), Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology, Jewish General Hospital (C1-260925)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-29\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\n​ Please refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Position Description The McGill University Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology in the Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences invites applications for a full-time Contract Academic Staff position at the rank of Assistant or Associate Professor (Clinical) based at the Jewish General Hospital (JGH). The professorial rank will be determined according to the successful candidate’s qualifications and experience. This will be a three-year, renewable appointment and does not confer eligibility for tenure.   The OBGYN Department at the Jewish General Hospital comprises specialists and subspecialists who provide the full complement of Obstetrical, Gynecologic and Oncologic services to the population. Our department is made up of 6 general OBGYN’s, 7 MFMs, 4 MIS and 5 Gyn Oncologist subspecialists. The JGH is the busiest Obstetrical centre on the island of Montreal, and our group ensures 24/7 in-house coverage of our high-volume labour and delivery unit, doing approximately 3600 deliveries per year. We collaborate with and support our Family Medicine Obstetrical Team and Midwifery team. We are a Tertiary level Centre offering specialized MFM care for complex pregnancy related conditions, and we receive many high-risk transfers from across the province annually. Our division of MIS ensures care of several thousand patients with complex gynecological cases and performs over 1000 surgical procedures per year using multiple approaches. Our Gyne-Oncology division provides state-of-the-art care to thousands of patients with gynecological malignancies and performed almost 300 robotic surgical cases last year.       Job Duties The successful candidate will be a member of the Department of OBGYN at the Jewish General Hospital. They are expected to perform clinical duties and attend the majority of department meetings and rounds, as well as provide excellent clinical teaching and supervision to undergraduate medical students, OBGYN residents, Family medicine residents and Fellows. The successful candidate is expected to take a normal allotment of Obstetrical call and bring an average volume of obstetrical patients to the institution, approximately 150 or more per year. The successful candidate is expected to take some MIS call as well.   The incumbent will also be expected to be actively involved in relevant aspects of McGill’s academic mission (e.g., research, teaching, supervision of students and residents, and involvement in academic and administrative committees).   McGill faculty members are expected to contribute to service activities within their units, the University and the wider scholarly community. A demonstrated commitment to equity, diversity, and inclusion is also expected.   Qualifications and Education Requirements   Candidates must:   Hold an MD degree (or equivalent), and be eligible for, licensure in the province of Quebec; Have completed a minimum 1-year fellowship specialty training in Obstetrics and Gynecology and be eligible for competency-based certification by the Collège des médecins du Québec or the Royal College of Physicians and Surgeons of Canada (or equivalent); Hold a Master’s degree or other postgraduate training (preferred, not required); Bilingual language skills (French and English) are preferred, or a commitment to acquire the necessary language skills at the level needed to practice medicine in Quebec. Candidates should be able to interact in English and in French with patients, families, and health care professionals.   Faculty/Department/Unit: Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences, Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology, Jewish General  Hospital Employee Type: Contract Academic Staff ('Academic Contractual') Rank: Assistant/Associate Professor Salary: Remuneration will be according to Quebec health care fee schedule Job Status: Full-time Posting Period: 30 days from date of posting The following supporting documents are required:   ·       Cover letter and curriculum vitae; ·       Statement of Research ·       Names and contact information of three referees.   Use a personal email address when creating an account in Workday to submit your application. Do not use @mail.mcgill.ca or @mcgill.ca email accounts to apply.   Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. L’Université McGill est une institution de langue anglaise dans laquelle la majorité des activités d’enseignement et de recherche s’effectuent en langue anglaise, rendant essentielle l’utilisation de l’anglais pour la communication orale et écrite.   McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Various/Student-Research-Assistant_JR0000081390": {
      "title": "Student Research Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-29\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: School of Physical and Occupational Therapy Position Summary: Carry out program evaluation activities for OT mentoring extension program, such as: create a survey for mentees and mentors run quantitative and qualitative analyses on survey data carry out a focus group (develop focus group questions) and conduct a thematic analysis on this data Assist in administrative duties such as editing, formatting documents, helping out at events as needed. Qualifications:   Minimum Bachelor’s degree High level of English proficiency – written and oral Experience in quantitative and/or qualitative research – preferred Strong communication and team work skills Knowledge of French and English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level #4 (qualifier) on a scale of 0-4. Hourly Salary: $19.02 Hours per Week: 10 (Part time) Location: Various Supervisor: Associate Professor (Professional) Position Start Date: 2026-09-29 Position End Date: 2027-03-31 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-07 This position is covered by the Association of McGill University Research Employees (AMURE) collective agreement. McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/McConnell-Eng-Bldg/Course-Lecturer---FACC-300---Winter-2027----sec-001---005_JR0000081393": {
      "title": "Course Lecturer - FACC 300 - Winter 2027 -  sec 001 & 005",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-29\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Course Lecturer - FACC 300 - Winter 2027 - sec 001 & 005 Hiring Unit: Faculty of Engineering Dean’s Office Course Subject Code and Title: FACC 300 Engineering Economy Term: Winter 2027 Number of credits: 3 Salary: $11 824 Location: Faculty of Engineering, McGill University Teaching Qualification Requirements: Education: Minimum requirement of a Ph.D. in the fields of Engineering, Accounting or Finance, or a master’s degree with a specialization or graduate diploma in the fields of Accounting or Finance. Experience: None Other qualification requirement(s): Member of Professional Order (Engineering or Architecture) Positions announced here are governed by the McGill Course Lecturers and Instructors Union (MCLIU) collective agreement which may be found on McGill's Human Resources website . Candidates are advised to review the agreement prior to applying. Deadline to apply: October 15, 2026 To apply: please refer to University’s HR website. ( https://www.mcgill.ca/hr/careers ) including CV and MCLIU form ( https://mcliu.ca/course-application-form/?lang=en ). McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. Hiring Unit: Dean's Office Course Title: Engineering Economy Subject Code: FACC 300 001 & 005 Location: Faculty of Engineering Schedule: Lectures: Tuesdays and Thursdays, 11:35 am-12:55 pm Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-14 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Davis-House/Administrative-Student-Affairs-Coordinator_JR0000078635": {
      "title": "Administrative Student Affairs Coordinator",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-29\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Under the direction of the immediate supervisor, provides support for administrative and student affairs activities. Participates in ensuring the smooth functioning of the unit’s operations. Responsible for documents and files of unit. Acts as resource person for policies and procedures. Advises students and resolves problems in relation to their files. Major Duties and Responsibilities: Acts as a resource and contact person for the Clinical placements in both Physical Therapy and Occupational Therapy. Follows up on problems in relation with the student’s files. Records expenses and provides account statements on request, i.e. for special events like the Spring clinical. Ensures regulations and deadlines are respected. Facilitates communication between the Clinical community and Clinical Education Faculty Coordinates meetings with the Clinical Community/Teaching partners, take minutes, distribute final copy, follow-up on action items Maintains Clinical Education website, revising text and information in both French and English Facilitates communication between the Clinical community and Clinical Education Faculty Enter data for clinical course teaching offers and coordinates of placement cycle (electronic sending of course confirmations, site payment letters, thank you certificates) Prepares statistics reports and maintains  the Clinical Education Portal database of over 2000 clinicians Acts as main contact for Clinicians regarding all non-academic clinical course issues and portal queries Lead coordination of site payments, tracking and follow-up, accounting administrative tasks, preparation of student expense reports Responds to student inquiries and attends to emails/calls ​ ​ The list of duties and responsibilities outlined above is representative and not a complete and detailed list of tasks which may be performed by an employee whose position has been matched to this generic job description. Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities: Organizational, priority-setting, problem-solving and values team work. Demonstrated sound judgement and decision making abilities. Ability to analyze, and synthesize complex information and to handle multiple projects on deadlines. Client-focused with a high level of tact, confidentiality, diplomacy, professionalism. Demonstrated ability to build appropriate rapport and effective relationships. Demonstrated ability to work in a PC environment using databases, spreadsheets, presentation software and web applications. Experience with databases and other digital solutions to support task efficiency Understanding of the Quebec healthcare system an asset Fluently bilingual in English and French (oral and written). Testing may be administered Minimum Education and Experience: DEP - Office Systems 4 Years Related Experience / DEP - Secretarial Studies Hourly Salary: (MUNACA Level G) $31.19 - $38.65 Hours per Week: 33.75 (Full time) Supervisor: Student Affairs Officer Position End Date (If applicable): 2027-07-31 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-09 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Sherbrooke-680/Administrative-Student-Affairs-Coordinator_JR0000081217": {
      "title": "Administrative Student Affairs Coordinator",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-29\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Position Summary: Under the direction of the immediate supervisor, provides administrative and secretarial support for administrative and student affairs activities. Participates in ensuring the smooth functioning of the unit’s operations. Responsible for documents and files of unit. Acts as resource person for policies and procedures. Coordinates activities related to admission, examinations, registration and graduation. Advises students and resolves problems in relation to their files. Edits documents for grammar and accuracy. Administers unit accounts. Major Duties and Responsibilities: Maintains agenda for the Associate Dean UGME, Assistant Dean UGME, and UGME Professionalism Advisor, which includes coordinating availabilities for many committee commitments and confidential meetings with a variety of stakeholders including students. Processes payments, expenses and travel advance requests for ADUGME and other academic leads. Ensures that the Associate Dean, Assistant Dean, or Professionalism Advisor has all relevant documentation in advance of meetings and events. Prior to meetings with students, prepares student files, which usually contain sensitive and confidential information, for review by the academic lead. Is responsible to gather student's academic data for review by the Associate Dean. Liaises with curriculum administrators for any outstanding information. Assists the FMHS nurse in following up with incoming students whose immunization status does not meet the immunization requirements prior to Orientation. Reports non-compliant students to the ADUGME. Also assists in following up with students if other vaccinations are required during the course of their studies. Tracks student progress throughout the years and liaises with the FMHS Nurse re. outstanding cases. Assists the secretary of the UGME Student Promotions Committee in booking meetings and ensuring quorum. Prepares student files for presentation to Student Promotions Committee – including student academic record, professionalism records, and disciplinary information as necessary. Acts as Minute Taker at Undergraduate Operations Committee and the MDCM Program Committee Upon request, provides assistance for a variety of UGME events such as orientation, graduation, White Coat Ceremony, etc. Provides information on a specific program or guidelines to callers and visitors. Ensures that critical calls and meeting requests are handled with discretion, tact, and urgency. Ensures the timely assessment of students in accordance with UGME policies and procedures in collaboration with Academics to meet CaCMS accreditation requirements. Ensure data in relevant systems and databases is maintained and up to date. Input data into systems as required. Perform regular audits of system information to ensure accuracy of student records and relevant databases. Oversee assessment activities and ensure accurate recording of grades. Collaborate with assessment team, curriculum delivery team, student affairs team, and systems team to ensure assessments are uploaded to correct platform. Coordinate entry and release of grades, failure notifications, and follow up of remedial and supplemental activities. Tracking and follow up of late or incomplete narrative assessments. Contribute to processes related to preparation and submission of documentation for student’s residency applications, including the Medical Student Performance Record (MSPR). Collaborate with various internal stakeholders including the Associate Dean as well as external bodies including CaRMS. Ensure multiple critical deadlines are met throughout the year. Provide support to alumni of the UGME program including producing credentialing documents and MSPR copies as well as degree verification. Input student grades into Banner and ensure course completion for all UGME students. Process, communicate, and track all student accommodations, professionalism concerns, and medium- and long-term leaves of absence. Coordinate student transcripts including entry of grades and leaves of absence. Prepare student documentation based on student record data (eg Letters of Good Standing) as needed. Certify documents for students as needed. Monitors registration status of students. Collaborates on projects to improve services provided to stakeholders. Contributes to program CQI and process improvement as needed. Represent the Student Records team and activities at governance meetings as required.  Assist secretary of the Student Promotions Committee with preparing documents and logistics for meetings. Ensure all processes and procedures related to the role are documented and that SOPs are updated on a regular basis.  Provide back-up and support to teammates and colleagues as needed.   Additional administrative support for other UGME teams as required and assigned to ensure smooth delivery of student affairs activities. Additional related duties as assigned.   The list of duties and responsibilities outlined above is representative and not a complete and detailed list of tasks which may be performed by an employee whose position has been matched to this generic job description. Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities: Proven ability to establish productive relationships with all levels of stakeholders. Proven ability to work autonomously and as member of a team. English and French, spoken and written. Demonstrated ability to provide support for various administrative activities, including those related to student, academic, and other activities. Demonstrated ability to take initiative, problem-solve and organize work. Strong ability to multi-task, prioritize and meet multiple deadlines in a dynamic environment. Must be customer focused and service oriented, assisting all clients with tact and diplomacy while maintaining a professional manner at all times. Proven ability to draft correspondence, must be accurate and possess superior attention to detail. Proven discretion in dealing with confidential information. As one of Montreal's Top Employers, here is what we offer: Competitive benefits package (Health, Dental, Life Insurance) (if eligible) Defined contribution pension plan (with employer contribution up to 10%) (if eligible) Group Registered Retirement Savings Plan (RRSP) and Tax Free Savings Account (TFSA) Competitive vacation policy Two (2) personal days Two (2) floating holidays Nine (9) \"Summer Fridays\" - paid days off between the St-Jean Baptiste holiday and Labour Day Paid time off over the December holiday period Tuition waiver for regular employees and their dependents Up to two (2) days of remote work per week where the position permits Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level # 4 on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: DEP - Office Systems 4 Years Related Experience / DEP - Secretarial Studies Hourly Salary: (MUNACA Level G) $31.19 - $38.65 Hours per Week: 33.75 (Full time) Supervisor: Student Records Officer Position End Date (If applicable): 2027-10-31 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-08 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Bishop-Mountain-Hall/Counter-Person_JR0000081337": {
      "title": "Counter Person",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-29\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Under the direction of the Dining Hall Supervisor or Head Counterperson, sees to the cleanliness and efficient operations of the cafeteria. Hiring Unit: Student Housing & Hospitality Services Qualifications Required   - High School Leaving Diploma.  - Minimum six (6) months experience.  - Ability to read and write English and French.  - Good communication skills. Knowledge of French and English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. Additional Information: Seasonal Position.  Work schedule:  Monday to Friday; 14h15-22h00; 38.75 hours/week + Evening Premiums. Hourly Salary: $31.91 Hours per Week: 38.75 (Full time) Location: Bishop Mountain Hall Supervisor: Dining Hall Supervisor (ER) Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-14 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/McConnell-Eng-Bldg/Course-Lecturer---FACC-300---Winter-2027---sec-002---006_JR0000081445": {
      "title": "Course Lecturer - FACC 300 - Winter 2027 - sec 002 & 006",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-29\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Course Lecturer - FACC 300 - Winter 2027 - sec 002 & 006 Hiring Unit: Faculty of Engineering Dean’s Office Course Subject Code and Title: FACC 300 Engineering Economy Term: Winter 2027 Number of credits: 3 Salary: $11 824 Location: Faculty of Engineering, McGill University Teaching Qualification Requirements: Education: Minimum requirement of a Ph.D. in the fields of Engineering, Accounting or Finance, or a master’s degree with a specialization or graduate diploma in the fields of Accounting or Finance. Experience: None Other qualification requirement(s): Member of Professional Order (Engineering or Architecture) Positions announced here are governed by the McGill Course Lecturers and Instructors Union (MCLIU) collective agreement which may be found on McGill's Human Resources website . Candidates are advised to review the agreement prior to applying. Deadline to apply: October 15, 2026 To apply: please refer to University’s HR website. ( https://www.mcgill.ca/hr/careers ) including CV and MCLIU form ( https://mcliu.ca/course-application-form/?lang=en ). McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. Hiring Unit: Dean's Office Course Title: Engineering Economy Subject Code: FACC 300 002 % 006 Location: Faculty of Engineering Schedule: Lectures: Tuesdays and Thursdays, 16:05 am-17:25 pm Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-14 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Burnside-Hall/Winter-2027-Teaching-Assistant-Postings---Mathematics---Statistics_JR0000081449-1": {
      "title": "Winter 2027 Teaching Assistant Postings - Mathematics & Statistics",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-29\nPosted: Posted Today\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Mathematics and Statistics Estimated Number of Positions: 61 Course Number and Course Title: Please note that the total hours could vary between 45 to 150 hours. Head Teaching Assistant - MATH 140-141 MATH 123 - Linear Algebra and Probability MATH 133 - Linear Algebra and Geometry MATH 140 - Calculus 1 MATH 141 - Calculus 2  MATH 203 - Principles of Statistics 1 MATH 204 - Principles of Statistics 2 MATH 222 - Calculus 3 MATH 223 - Linear Algebra MATH 236 - Algebra 2 MATH 240 - Discrete Structures MATH 243 - Analysis 2 MATH 247 - Honours Applied Linear Algebra MATH 249 - Honours Complex Variables MATH 251 - Honours Algebra 2 MATH 255 - Honours Analysis 2 MATH 262 - Intermediate Calculus MATH 263 - Ordinary Differential Equations for Engineers MATH 264 - Advanced Calculus for Engineers MATH 266 - Theory & Numerics of Ordinary & Partial Differential Equations MATH 308 - Fundamentals of Statistical Learning MATH 314 - Advanced Calculus MATH 315 - Ordinary Differential Equations MATH 323 - Probability MATH 324 - Statistics  MATH 325 - Honours Ordinary Differential Equations MATH 340 - Discrete Mathematics MATH 447 - Introduction to Stochastic Processes MATH 523 - Generalized Linear Models MATH Help Desk (75 hours) STATS Help Desk (45 hours) Position Summary: *NOTE: Please apply through the Careers website as an \"External Candidate\". Use your legal name, a Canadian home address, and a personal email address when creating your external applicant profile (do not use your McGill email). International candidates must have a Canadian bank account, valid SIN and study permit.* Each Teaching Assistant will be responsible for a tutorial group, unless assigned a Help Desk position. These positions will include some or all of the following: Giving tutorials. These involve the formal presentation of carefully prepared illustrative examples worked at the blackboard. Students should be given ample chance to ask questions. Some time may be spent on more informal classroom activities - helping individual students as they work individually or in groups. At least two regularly scheduled office hours per week - the times to be announced at the first tutorial. Marking of assignments of the tutorial group. Invigilation of the midterm at the scheduled time. Team marking of the midterm and final examination under the close supervision of the Faculty teaching the course (who will also set the examinations). Final examinations must NEVER be handed back to students. Maintenance of the complete mark record for the tutorial group. A teaching assistant may be assigned a coordinating or supervisory role in the marking process. Teaching assistants may also be asked to cooperate in preparing solution sheets to assignments. They may be asked to set and mark occasional short \"surprise tests\" which can be used to encourage regular attendance. Maintaining the online platform: read posts from students and write responses. Help Desks: Maintaining the online platform: read posts from students and write responses Meeting regularly with the faculty supervisor Answering questions concerning statistical computing Answering questions concerning gaps of knowledge in mathematics and/or statistics that the student is supposed to have on entering the course (i.e. prerequisite knowledge) Qualifications: Must be a McGill graduate student Honours Degree in Mathematics, Statistics or Applied Mathematics, with high standing. McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Technical-Services-Building/Lab-Helper-B_JR0000081421": {
      "title": "Lab Helper B",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-29\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Dr. V. Adamchuk, Bioresource Engineering Position Summary: Operate proximal sensing platforms pertaining to the new CFI funding. Help integrate various sensors and test their performance. Assist with field data stewardship. Help with organization of laboratory materials and instruments. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level # (qualifier) on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: High School Diploma 1 Year Related Experience Hourly Salary: (AMUSE B) $17.00 Estimated Number of Work Hours per Week: 10 (Part time) Position Start Date: 2026-09-29 Position End Date: 2027-04-30 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-05 This position is covered by the AMUSE collective agreement. McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/McConnell-Eng-Bldg/Research-Assistant-1_JR0000081371": {
      "title": "Research Assistant 1",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-29\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). This part-time, Research Assistant 1 position is in Prof. Thomas Szkopek’s research team, in the Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering Position Summary: Assist Prof. Szkopek’s research group in graphene ion sensitive field effect transistor-based sensor research, with the following activities: design, fabrication, test and iterative improvement of physical test fixtures including electrical contacts, mechanical assembly, and liquid flow cell access for functionalization and test of graphene ion sensitive field effect transistor-based sensors, document the design, fabrication and test of all test fixtures, including device yield results assist with graphene field effect transistor fabrication and test contingent on available time and research team needs, assist with the design and implementation of research instrumentation for other projects Qualifications: The candidate must hold a bachelor’s degree in a relevant engineering discipline, such as but not limited to mechanical engineering, electrical engineering, applied science, or physics. The candidate must have experience in the design and implementation of mechanical and electro-mechanical systems. Familiarity with electronic instrumentation, microfluidics, additive manufacturing methods and system integration is beneficial. The candidate must be capable of working in a multi-disciplinary research environment Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Hourly Salary: $30.54 Hours per Week: 16 (Part time) Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday Location: McConnell Eng. Bldg. Supervisor: Professor Position Start Date: 2026-09-28 Position End Date: 2026-12-31 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-05 This position is covered by the Association of McGill University Research Employees (AMURE) collective agreement. McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Stewart-Biology-Bldg/Research-Associate_JR0000080924-1": {
      "title": "Research Associate",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-29\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Department: Department of Psychology Position Summary: Develop and conduct research projects, plan specific experiments to test hypotheses about the role of the anterior vs. posterior nucleus accumbens in valence during an appetitive/aversive paradigm in mice . Read and interpret new literature to integrate the latest findings in the field Run mouse behavioral experiments with optogenetic stimulation to probe valence in an appetitive/aversive paradigm Perform wet lab work such as histology/RNA-scope to validate viral targeting and probe gene expression, as well as imaging of the data Perform data analysis on behavioral and imaging data Supervise and train a graduate student assisting with the planned experiments Prepare and write manuscripts in collaboration with the primary supervisor. Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities: Expertise in R and Python for data analysis Expertise in bioinformatic analyses Advanced statistical modelling approaches, including multilevel and multivariate methods, dimensionality reduction, and latent variable modelling Good organization skills Expertise with statistical analysis of data Attention to detail Able to work independently and in collaboration with others Experience and Education: Experience with animal behavior and animal handling Experience working with RNAscope, assay development for single-cell and single-nucleus RNA sequencing Experience in computational modelling of complex biological data Extensive studies in the field of neuroscience Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Hourly Salary: $35.37 Hours per Week: 40 (Full time) Location: Stewart Biology Bldg Supervisor: Associate Professor Position Start Date: 2026-09-22 Position End Date: 2027-10-10 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-05 This position is covered by the Association of McGill University Research Employees (AMURE) collective agreement. McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Macdonald-Eng-Bldg/Course-Lecturer--Winter-2027--MECH-642---Advanced-Dynamics_JR0000080947": {
      "title": "Course Lecturer (Winter 2027) MECH 642 - Advanced Dynamics",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-29\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Department of Mechanical Engineering Faculty of Engineering McGill University Winter 2027 Course Lecturer Position Deadline to Apply October 14, 2026 The Department of Mechanical Engineering is inviting applications for a course lecturer position to teach in Winter 2027. Please include your cover letter and Curriculum Vitae. Course Lecturer MECH 642 - Advanced Dynamics (4 credits) Instructor must cover all material as written in the Course Outline on the McGill Course Catalogue page, as follows: Variational methods. Hamilton's principle and equations of motion of engineering systems. Lagrangian formulations for discrete systems. Methods of discretizing continuous systems. Rigid body dynamics. Dynamic behaviour of linear and nonlinear systems. Response of engineering systems to deterministic inputs by classical methods. Stability of linear and nonlinear systems. Teaching Qualification Requirements: Ph.D. in Mechanical Engineering. Minimum of three years of teaching experience or in industry Good knowledge of dynamic mechanisms Demonstrated knowledge of spoken and written English. Understanding cross-cultural practices. Strong problem-solving skills and flexible approach to teaching. Date: January 5, 2027- April 30, 2027 Salary: $15, 765 per course Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-14 For any questions, please contact apptadmin.mecheng@mcgill.ca Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Hiring Unit: Department of Mechanical Engineering Course Title: Advanced Dynamics Subject Code: MECH 642 Location: ENGMD 276 Schedule: Tuesday 5:35 pm – 8:25 pm Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-14 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Parasitology-Bldg/Course-Lecturer---BTEC-619_JR0000081433": {
      "title": "Course Lecturer - BTEC 619",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-29\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Institute of Parasitology Faculty of Agricultural and Environmental Sciences Winter 2027 Course Lecturer Position Deadline to Apply: October 15, 2026 at 11:59 PM   BTEC 619 - Biotechnology Laboratory 2   Location : Laboratory: Macdonald Stewart B-027 McGill Macdonald Campus   Schedule : Course lecturer is expected to be available for 3 hours per week total during labs. Labs are prepared and supervised by separate Course Technician. Labs: Wednesday, 9:35 a.m. - 5:25 p.m. Thursday, 9:35 a.m. - 5:25 p.m. Teaching Qualification Requirements: Applicants should have a graduate degree, or Ph.D. candidate with at least 3 years of progress in their program. A scientific background in science and/or biochemistry is required. This position is located in Ste-Anne-de-Bellevue, Quebec and you are expected to perform the work for this position in Quebec. The McGill exam sessions take place over two weeks after the end of classes, and Course Lecturers are expected to be available on the day of a scheduled final exam and are required to grade final exams as part of their contract . Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. ​ For any questions, please contact Arina de Leeuw, Academic Affairs Administrator, Institute of Parasitology. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Hiring Unit: Institute of Parasitology Course Title: Biotechnology Laboratory 2 Subject Code: BTEC 619 001 & 002 Location: Macdonald Stewart B-027 Schedule: Wed/Thurs 9:35 a.m. - 5:25 p.m Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-15 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/McIntyre--Medical/Teaching-Assistant---Clinical-Mentorship---PHAR-524---Winter-2027_JR0000081442": {
      "title": "Teaching Assistant - Clinical Mentorship - PHAR 524 - Winter 2027",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-29\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Department of Pharmacology & Therapeutics Course Title: Clinical Mentorship Course Code: PHAR 524 Estimated Number of Positions: 1 Total Hours of Work per Term: 35 Position Summary: Attendance at class and student presentations as requested by coordinators. Providing individualized assistance to students having difficulty with the course material, including small group tutorial sessions. Support the Course coordinator in the organization of students’ presentations, the distribution of course material, relevant information and the timely collection of essays.         Qualifications: Must have sufficient command of English to be able to help international students.  Very good communication skills and attention to detail.  Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Hourly Salary: $38.46 Position Start Date: 2027-01-05 Position End Date: 2027-04-30 Deadline to Apply: 2026-11-01 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/McIntyre--Medical/Teaching-Assistant---PHAR-602--Principles-of-Pharmacology----Winter-2027_JR0000081434": {
      "title": "Teaching Assistant - PHAR 602: Principles of Pharmacology  - Winter 2027",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-29\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Department of Pharmacology & Theraeputics Course Title: Principles of Pharmacology Course Code: PHAR 602 Estimated Number of Positions: 1 Total Hours of Work per Term: 40 Position Summary: Tutoring students and grading exams, oral presentations, and written reports. Qualifications: Good attention to detail and good communication skills. Must have sufficient command of English to be able to help international students. Must have retained a good knowledge of course material, which consists of most basic principles of pharmacology. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Hourly Salary: $38.46 Position Start Date: 2027-01-05 Position End Date: 2027-04-30 Deadline to Apply: 2026-11-01 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Mtl-Childrens-Hosp/Assistant-or-Associate-Professor--Clinical---Department-of-Pediatrics--Division-of-Pediatric-Respirology--C1-260911-_JR0000080989": {
      "title": "Assistant or Associate Professor (Clinical), Department of Pediatrics, Division of Pediatric Respirology (C1-260911)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-29\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\n​ Please refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). The McGill University Department of Pediatrics in the Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences is inviting applications for a full-time Contract Academic Staff position as an academic clinician at the rank of Assistant or Associate Professor (Clinical). The successful candidate will be based at the Montreal Children’s Hospital (MCH) of the McGill University Health Centre (MUHC) and will practice predominantly within the Sleep and Ventilation program. The professorial rank will be determined according to the successful candidate’s qualifications and experience. This is a Contract Academic Staff position and does not confer eligibility for tenure. The Department of Pediatrics is a leader in academic child health. Our educational and research missions support excellent and innovative clinical programs. The Montreal Children’s Hospital is one of McGill’s major teaching hospitals affiliated with the Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences at Canada’s internationally renowned McGill University, located in the vibrant and multicultural city of Montreal. All major pediatric medical and surgical subspecialties are represented at the MCH. The Division of Pediatric Respiratory Medicine provides a comprehensive range of inpatient and outpatient services and consists of eight full-time McGill faculty members and several associate staff physicians, delivering multidisciplinary care across the full spectrum of respiratory and sleep disorders in infants, children, and adolescents. Our Sleep and Ventilation program includes two full-time and several part-time subspecialist physicians, a nurse practitioner, three dedicated nurses, and numerous respiratory therapists within the Montreal Children’s Hospital Sleep Laboratory and Clinic practice.  This team participates in new consultations, follow-up clinical activities, and sleep test interpretations. This advertised position is for a third, full-time pediatric respirologist with training and/or expertise in sleep medicine and/or long-term home ventilation to further supplement the group and this growing field. The Respiratory Division also operates a Pulmonary Function Testing Laboratory, including advanced physiologic evaluation such as cardiopulmonary exercise testing (CPET). In addition, the Division maintains a robust diagnostic and interventional bronchoscopy program, including airway evaluation, bronchoalveolar lavage, and cryotherapy. The Division works closely with ENT, Complex Care Pediatrics, Neurology, Gastroenterology, Cardiology, Genetics, Neonatology, Intensive Care, and allied health teams to deliver advanced, integrated pediatric respiratory and sleep medicine care. The successful candidate will focus on clinical activities in Pediatric Respiratory Medicine and with a significant portion of time in Sleep Medicine, provide outpatient and inpatient care, supervise trainees, and participate in academic rounds and scholarly contributions to the field. Compensation is based on a hybrid model derived in part from fee-for-service billings. This hybrid model provides annual compensation which is significantly above the median pediatric respirology income across Canada. Job Duties Clinical Duties Participation in general pediatric sleep clinics and multidisciplinary clinics, including: Complex Sleep–Disordered Breathing Clinic; Technology-Dependent / Ventilation Clinics; Joint clinics with ENT, Complex Care Pediatrics, and Neurology; Interpretation of pediatric polysomnography and other sleep diagnostics; Longitudinal management of children requiring long-term non-invasive ventilation, oxygen therapy, or high-flow nasal cannula; Coverage of inpatient respiratory consult service (approximately 6–8 weeks per year); Participation in weekend respiratory medicine service coverage; Optional participation in general respiratory clinics; Optional participation in the pediatric flexible bronchoscopy service. Teaching Duties Candidates will supervise medical students, residents, and fellows during clinical care and contribute to the Pediatric Respiratory Medicine and Sleep Medicine training programs. Faculty in the Division also plays a key role in interdisciplinary education for nurses, respiratory therapists, and other health professionals. In addition, all faculty in the Department of Pediatrics are responsible for teaching activities for residents and undergraduate medical students. Academic Duties The successful candidate will be expected to contribute to academic rounds and participate in scholarly activities such as clinical research, quality improvement, program innovation, or education scholarship. The incumbent will also be expected to be actively involved in relevant aspects of McGill’s academic mission (e.g., research, teaching, supervision of students and residents and involvement in academic and administrative committees). All McGill faculty members are expected to contribute to service activities within their units, the University, and the wider scholarly community. A demonstrated commitment to equity, diversity, and inclusion is also expected. Qualifications and Education Requirements Candidates should: Hold an MD degree or equivalent; Hold or be eligible for licensure from the Collège des médecins du Québec; Have completed specialty training in Pediatrics and Pediatric Respirology, with certification by the Collège des Médecins du Québec or the Royal College of Physicians and Surgeons of Canada (or equivalent); Have formal training in Pediatric Sleep Medicine; Bilingual language skills (French and English) are preferred, or a commitment to acquire the necessary language skills to practice medicine in Quebec. Candidates should be able to interact in English and in French with patients, families and health care professionals.   Faculty/Department/Unit: Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences, Department of Pediatrics, Division of Pediatric Respirology Employee Type: Contract Academic Staff ('Academic Contractual') Rank: Assistant or Associate Professor Salary: Remuneration will be according to Quebec health care fee schedule Job Status: Full-time Posting Period: 90 days from posting   The following supporting documents are required: - Cover letter and curriculum vitae; -   Names and contact information of three referees.   McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. L’Université McGill est une institution de langue anglaise dans laquelle la majorité des activités d’enseignement et de recherche s’effectuent en langue anglaise, rendant essentielle l’utilisation de l’anglais pour la communication orale et écrite. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Trottier-Building/Course-Lecturer---SEAD-530_JR0000081385": {
      "title": "Course Lecturer - SEAD 530",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-29\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). The Trottier Institute for Sustainability in Engineering and Design (TISED) is inviting applications for a Course Lecturer position for the Winter 2027. Course Title and Course Number: Economics for Sustainability in Engineering and Design - SEAD 530 (3 credits)   Deadline to Apply: 10/15/2026 @ 11:59 p.m. (EDT) (the posting will expire at midnight) Teaching Qualification Requirements Education : Master’s Degree or Ph.D. in Engineering, or a closely related field Experience : Teaching experience in university environment in English and/or French Other qualification requirement(s): Demonstrated knowledge (e.g., through publications, theses, work experience) of micro and macroeconomics of sustainability, market structures, principles of substitution, market failures and externalities, monetization and pricing of externalities. Demonstrated knowledge of policy instruments, permits and licenses, mandates, incentives, penalties, taxation and eco-social principles, mechanism design, the principles of life cycle analyses and the circular economy. Demonstrated knowledge of impact of engineering on ecological and economic sustainability. Past experience in economic analysis for sustainability /sustainable development through graduate education and/or teaching. You must apply through WORKDAY . For detailed instructions, click on the appropriate link below. For external candidates see the instructions  HERE / Français For internal candidates ( you must be connected to the VPN ), see the instructions HERE / Français Hiring Unit: TISED Course Title: Economics for Sustainability in Engineering and Design Subject Code: SEAD 530 001 Location: ENGTR 1100 Schedule: Thursdays, 02:35 pm-05:25 pm Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-15 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Leacock-Building/Assistant-Professor--Philosophy-and-Artificial-Intelligence_JR0000081267-1": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Philosophy and Artificial Intelligence",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-29\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\n​ Please refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). McGill University Department of Philosophy Faculty of Arts Assistant Professor, Philosophy and Artificial Intelligence Full-time tenure-track position in Philosophy at the rank of Assistant Professor Subfield: Philosophy and Artificial Intelligence The Department of Philosophy at McGill University invites applications for a tenure-track position in Philosophy and Artificial Intelligence, with a strong preference for candidates working in metaphysics, philosophy of mind, philosophy of language or epistemology.  The Department of Philosophy, in the Faculty of Arts, is a pluralist, research-intensive unit that includes strengths in ethics, political philosophy, logic, and feminist philosophy. The Department emphasizes rigor in argument, clarity in expression, and intellectual generosity, preparing their community of students and researchers not only for academic success but also for vital interdisciplinary work across the humanities, sciences, and professional fields.   The appointment is expected to be at the rank of Assistant Professor. The position start date is August 1, 2027.   For more information about the Department of Philosophy, please visit our website at: https://www.mcgill.ca/philosophy/ .  Inquiries may be directed to Professor Michael Blome-Tillmann, Chair of the Department of Philosophy, at michael.blome@mcgill.ca . Job Duties: The successful candidate will be actively involved in the academic activities of the department, including research, teaching, supervision of students, and service on academic and administrative committees. McGill faculty members are expected to contribute to service activities within their units, the University, and the wider scholarly community. A demonstrated commitment to equity, diversity, and inclusion is also expected. Qualifications and Education Requirements: Candidates should have completed their PhD or be near completion. An applicant’s record of performance must provide evidence of outstanding research potential and the ability/potential to teach effectively at the undergraduate and graduate level, as well as evidence (or intentions) of undergraduate and graduate supervision and of mentoring students with diverse lived experiences. Faculty/Department/Unit: Department of Philosophy Faculty of Arts Job Type: Tenure-track Rank: Assistant Professor Salary: Salary will be commensurate with qualifications and experience. Application Deadline: Applications should be received by October 30, 2026 , to guarantee full consideration, although applications will continue to be reviewed until the position is filled. Required Documents: The following supporting documents are required: Cover letter and curriculum vitae; Statement of research; Writing sample (article or chapter length); Teaching statement, as well as course evaluations and syllabi if available; Three confidential letters of reference submitted directly by the referees (You will be sent an email with instructions on how to provide referee contact information shortly after application).   Depending on your nationality, it may be necessary for you to have a valid Canadian Visa to travel should you be invited to a campus visit. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/McIntyre--Medical/Teaching-Assistant---Scientific-Communication-for-Pharmacologists---PHAR-610---Winter-2027_JR0000081354": {
      "title": "Teaching Assistant - Scientific Communication for Pharmacologists - PHAR 610 - Winter 2027",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-29\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Department of Pharmacology & Therapeutics Course Title: Scientific Communication for Pharmacologists Course Code: PHAR 610 Estimated Number of Positions: 2 Total Hours of Work per Term: 40 Position Summary: Potentially attend one or more (2-hour) classes to lead activities and/or to provide feedback. If needed, offer one-on-one sessions to help students in need of extra assistance. Invigilate and potentially to mark exams. Manage myCourses website, including uploading material. Qualifications: Graduate student who demonstrated excellent performance in PHAR 610. Must have excellent command of written and oral English as it relates to biomedical research. Requires considerable attention to detail for marking assignments.   Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Hourly Salary: $38.46 Position Start Date: 2027-01-05 Position End Date: 2027-04-30 Deadline to Apply: 2026-11-01 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
