    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Sherbrooke-680/Administrative-Coordinator_JR0000081364": {
      "title": "Administrative Coordinator",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-28\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Unit / Faculty: School of Continuing Studies / French Language Testing and Non-Credit programs Part-time position (20.25 hours / 3 days per week) for 6 months period Position Summary: Under the direct supervision of the Director of French Language Testing and Non-Credit Program, the Administrative Coordinator supports the day-to-day delivery of TEF (Test d’évaluation de français) and TEFAQ (Test d’évaluation du français adapté pour le Québec) exams, language placement tests, and related activities within the Global and Strategic Communication (GSC) domain. This includes scheduling exams, registering candidates, coordinating invigilators, preparing materials, and providing on-site support to ensure testing sessions run smoothly and according to established procedures. The role also serves as a key point of contact for students, responding to inquiries, maintaining accurate records, supporting course administration, and helping to ensure a high standard of service. Major Duties and Responsibilities: 1. Acts as resource person regarding the unit’s policies and procedures. Resolves problems within area of responsibility. Consults relevant documentation and liaises with appropriate resource persons to obtain and provide information on diverse and complex issues. 2. Participates in ensuring the smooth functioning of the unit. Recommends procedural changes to enable unit to function more effectively. Collaborates with supervisor and others in establishing work priorities. Follows up on and ensures appropriate implementation of decisions made by supervisor. 3. Provides information to callers and visitors. Determines reason for call or visit and prepares necessary documents. Directs callers and visitors to appropriate resource persons. Assesses need for intervention of other resource persons and arranges appointments as necessary. 4. Schedules meetings, contacts participants and books meeting rooms. Assists with preparation of agenda. Attends meetings, takes minutes, and follows up on decisions within area of responsibility. 5. Uses a variety of software. Maintains computerized information systems and participates in their development to improve efficiency within the unit. Acts as a resource person concerning the use of software. Explains and demonstrates the use of software, resolves problems within area of expertise, and liaises with computer resource persons as required. Maintains and updates administrative files. Maintains filing systems and arranges for archiving. The list of duties and responsibilities outlined above is representative and not a complete and detailed list of tasks which may be performed by an employee whose position has been matched to this generic job description. Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities: Excellent interpersonal skills to establish and maintain effective working relationships with stakeholders at all levels. Ability to multi-task in a fast-paced environment and to carry on simultaneous projects with minimal supervision. Ability to respect competing deadlines and to adapt to changing circumstances. Strong problem-solving, priority-setting, and organizational skills. Proven attention to detail and analytical skills to understand, interpret, and apply complex academic policies and procedures. Fluency in French (spoken and written)  is required, as communication with candidates and external partners is primarily conducted in French.    The schedule is variable based on operational needs , including required availability on Saturdays to support testing activities.  The role is fully in-person , as it involves the direct administration of examinations and on-site support.  As one of Montreal's Top Employers, here is what we offer: Competitive benefits package (Health, Dental, Life Insurance) (if eligible) Defined contribution pension plan (with employer contribution up to 10%) (if eligible) Group Registered Retirement Savings Plan (RRSP) and Tax Free Savings Account (TFSA) Competitive vacation policy Two (2) personal days Two (2) floating holidays Nine (9) \"Summer Fridays\" - paid days off between the St-Jean Baptiste holiday and Labour Day Paid time off over the December holiday period Tuition waiver for regular employees and their dependents Up to two (2) days of remote work per week where the position permits Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level #3 (qualifier) on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: DEP - Secretarial Studies 4 Years Related Experience / DEP - Office Systems Hourly Salary: (MUNACA Level F) $29.42 - $36.46 Hours per Week: 20.25 (Part time) Monday, Wednesday, Friday Supervisor: Faculty Lecturer Position End Date (If applicable): 2027-04-30 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-13 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Leacock-Building/Course-Lecturer---POLI-381-001---Winter-2027_JR0000081242": {
      "title": "Course Lecturer – POLI 381-001 – Winter 2027",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-28\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Department of Political Science Faculty of Arts McGill University Winter 2027 Course Lecturer Position Deadline to Apply: October 15, 2026 The Department of Political Science is inviting applications for a course lecturer position to teach in Winter 2027. Please include your Cover Letter and Curriculum Vitae and if applicable a copy of your Study/Work Permit. A brief course outline is optional. POLI 381-001 Politics in Japan and South Korea (3 Credits - CAP 80) An introduction to key issues of contemporary politics in Japan and South Korea, covering the politics and economic development of Post-WWII Japan and Post-Korean War South Korea. Themes include: How were the contemporary political systems established in Japan and South Korea? How have these systems changed over time? What are the impacts of political institutions on the political and economic development in the two countries? How do social actors and political and economic institutions interact with each other? What are the foreign policymaking strategies in the two countries? Teaching Qualification Requirements: A completed PhD degree or enrolled in a doctoral program in Political Science or a discipline related to the course; Provide evidence of effective teaching ability as a result of having successfully taught a similar course; The candidate's area of specialization must be as close as possible to that of the course being offered; Experience as a teaching assistant for a course is an asset. Monday and Wednesday 04:05 pm-05:25 pm, January 5, 2027 – April 14, 2027. Location: ENGMD 280. Salary: $11,824 per course. For any questions, please contact Judy Dear , Area Manager, Human Resources & Operations. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Hiring Unit: Department of Political Science Course Title: Politics in Japan and South Korea Subject Code: POLI 381 001 Location: ENGMD 280 Schedule: Monday and Wednesday 04:05 pm-05:25 pm Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-15 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Sherbrooke-680/Administrator_JR0000081078": {
      "title": "Administrator",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-28\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Position Summary Reporting to the Associate Director, Clinical Student Affairs, the Administrator provides administrative support to residency and fellowship programs within Postgraduate Medical Education (PGME). The position is responsible for ensuring business continuity by maintaining high-quality administrative operations while and supporting sustainable operational practices. Working collaboratively with Program Directors and Assistant Program Directors, Student Affairs Managers, administrative staff, Residents and Fellows, faculty members, PGME committees, PGME education, Admissions, Record, other administrative teams and institutional partners. The incumbent independently supports complex administrative activities and provides information on PGME policies, accreditation requirements, Faculty standards, and institutional procedures.   Primary Responsibilities Operational Coverage and Business Continuity Provides temporary administrative coverage to residency and fellowship programs, Recruitment and onboarding periods, transitions, staffing vacancies, periods of increased workload, Accreditation preparation or other operational needs. Assumes responsibility for day-to-day program administration. Ensures uninterrupted service to: Program Directors and Assistant Program Directors RPC, CC, CaRMS, Master Schedule, Accreditation, Academic Sessions, Residents. Operational Assessment and Standardization Reviews administrative operations within assigned programs to identify opportunities for improvement within area of responsibility. Evaluates: Workflows, Administrative practices, Documentation, Filing systems, Communication practices, Tracking tools, Scheduling processes, Implements standardized PGME procedures and best practices. Prepares or updates: Standard Operating Procedures (SOPs), Administrative manuals, Checklists, Templates, Shared documentation, Ensures consistency across all residency programs. Leaves programs in a sustainable operational state prior to transition. Administrative Acts as a resource person for PGME administrative processes. Monitors outstanding administrative items and follows up with residents, fellows, faculty or program staff as required. Organizes and prioritizes work across multiple programs according to established deadlines and priorities. Maintains program calendars, schedules, trackers, shared files and administrative documentation. Provides information regarding: Resident lifecycle administration, Admissions, Leaves, Academic sessions, Competency Based Medical Education (CBME), ONE45, Accreditation standards Identifies operational risks and escalates issues appropriately. Supports implementation of new institutional initiatives. Supports Competence Committee(CC) and Residency Program Committee (RPC) administrative activities, including preparation of materials, meeting coordination, documentation and follow-up. Maintains confidentiality and handles resident, fellow and program information in accordance with University policies and established procedures. Onboarding and Training Applies established PGME SOPs, templates, checklists and administrative procedures across assigned programs. Supports the onboarding of new Administrative Student Affairs Coordinators by sharing established processes, documentation and practical information related to assigned program activities. Provides: Hands-on training, Workflow coaching, Documentation review, Process orientation, Shadowing opportunities, Assists managers in transition planning. Promotes adoption of standardized practices. Continuous Improvement and Projects Supports projects including: Administrative standardization, Accreditation readiness, Technology implementation, Business process redesign, Documentation modernization, Centralization initiatives. Collaborates with managers to improve service delivery. Recommends administrative solutions. Reviews program documentation and administrative practices as part of routine work and identifies opportunities to improve organization, consistency or efficiency. Assists with the development or updating of administrative documentation, including checklists, templates, process guides and program-specific reference materials Quality Assurance Reviews administrative files for completeness and accuracy. Ensures compliance with: Royal College requirements, McGill University policies, Accreditation standards. Identifies gaps and implements corrective actions. Communicates recurring administrative issues and recommends practical solutions Exercises considerable judgment in prioritizing work across multiple programs. Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities Minimum of 3-5 years of relevant administrative experience. Strong ability to manage multiple priorities, stakeholders, and deadlines in a fast-paced environment. Experience coordinating complex administrative operations, schedules, meetings, and committee activities. Knowledge of postgraduate medical education or accreditation standards is an asset. Demonstrated ability to adapt quickly to workflows, systems, and operational environments. Demonstrated ability to learn and apply policies, procedures, guidelines, and established processes. Demonstrated ability to assess administrative workflows, identify gaps or inconsistencies, and implement practical improvements using established processes. Excellent organizational, problem-solving, and project coordination skills. Strong communication and interpersonal skills with the ability to collaborate effectively across diverse groups. Demonstrated attention to detail, sound judgment, and ability to handle confidential information. Experience with onboarding, knowledge transfer, coaching, or mentoring administrative staff is an asset. Proficiency in Microsoft Office Suite and administrative databases/systems. Adaptable, customer-service oriented, and committed to continuous improvement and operational excellence.   As one of Montreal's Top Employers, here is what we offer: Competitive benefits package (Health, Dental, Life Insurance) (if eligible) Defined contribution pension plan (with employer contribution up to 10%) (if eligible) Group Registered Retirement Savings Plan (RRSP) and Tax Free Savings Account (TFSA) Competitive vacation policy Two (2) personal days Two (2) floating holidays Nine (9) \"Summer Fridays\" - paid days off between the St-Jean Baptiste holiday and Labour Day Paid time off over the December holiday period Tuition waiver for regular employees and their dependents Up to two (2) days of remote work per week where the position permits Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level # 4 on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: DEC III 3 Years Related Experience / Hourly Salary: (MUNACA Level H) $33.05 - $40.97 Hours per Week: 33.75 (Full time) Supervisor: Associate Director, Clinical Student Affairs Position End Date (If applicable): 2027-09-23 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-07 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Bishop-Mountain-Hall/Counter-Person_JR0000081328": {
      "title": "Counter Person",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-28\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Under the direction of the Dining Hall Supervisor or Head Counterperson, sees to the cleanliness and efficient operations of the cafeteria. Hiring Unit: Student Housing & Hospitality Services Qualifications Required   - High School Leaving Diploma.  - Minimum six (6) months experience.  - Ability to read and write English and French.  - Good communication skills. Knowledge of French and English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. Additional Information: Seasonal Position.  Work schedule:  Monday to Friday; 7h15-15h00; 38.75 hours/week Hourly Salary: $31.91 Hours per Week: 38.75 (Full time) Location: Bishop Mountain Hall Supervisor: Dining Hall Supervisor (ER) Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-13 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
