    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Stewart-Biology-Bldg/Research-Assistant-2_JR0000080584": {
      "title": "Research Assistant 2",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-28\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Department of Biology ( Professor Andrew Gonzalez ) Position Summary: Maintains clear documentation of the BON in a Box platform and the available biodiversity analysis pipelines.   As required, debugs or enhances the biodiversity analysis pipelines supporting the use cases.   Assists in identifying grants, tenders and partnerships to support research operationalization, and contributes to writing application text s .   Assists in relationship -building with key policy actors such as parties to the Co nvention on Biological Diversity and the r egional Technical Support Centers (TS C ) , to bring scientific research into policy .   Presents the BON in a Box platform and e xplains scientific concepts such as essential biodiversity variables and indicators to non-technical audiences in online and in-person events .   Under the PI’s supervision , r eviews the communication strategy of BON in a Box - for example through social media and websites - to build awareness in our target user group and create a supporting scientific community.   Qualifications: Adaptability and a quick learning ability to understand diverse subjects in ecology, biodiversity, and data science.   Experience with biodiversity data analysis. Experience in R and python coding. Julia an asset.   Strong communication and public speaking skills with the ability to convey complex concepts clearly and effectively.   Demonstrated ability to build productive relationships with partners.   Maturity, professionalism, and a commitment to advancing biodiversity conservation efforts.   Education/Experience: Master's degree in biology or equivalent.   Experience in R and Python coding, Julia an asset.   Location:   This position is located in Montreal, Quebec and the work for this position is expected to be performed in Quebec. Knowledge of French and English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing.  The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level # (4) qualifier on a scale of 0-4.     Hourly Salary: $32.96 Hours per Week: 35 (Full time) Location: Stewart Biology Bldg Supervisor: Professor Position Start Date: 2026-09-14 Position End Date: 2026-12-31 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-02 This position is covered by the Association of McGill University Research Employees (AMURE) collective agreement. McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/McLennan-Library/Open-House-Student-Ambassador_JR0000081266": {
      "title": "Open House Student Ambassador",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-28\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Position Summary: Enrolment Services is currently seeking enrolled undergraduate students of McGill University to join our team for Open House. Y ou will play a pivotal role in creating a warm and informative atmosphere for prospective students and their families during McGill University's Open House events. Your responsibilities will encompass a range of activities aimed at ensuring a positive and engaging experience for all attendees. You will be given a description of duties for the day. Assignments may be inside, outside or both and may include the following: Welcoming prospective students and their families to McGill. Providing directions and information about event activities at Open House (Volunteer Info provided). Providing information about McGill and student life as needed. Assisting with seating guests at information sessions, assisting presenters and distributing material Assisting with preparation of material and event logistics (includes set-up/take-down). Maintenance of volunteer break rooms and distribution of lunch. The list of functions outlined above is representative and not a complete and detailed list of tasks which may be performed by an incumbent. Minimum Education and Experience: High School Diploma Minimal or No Related Experience Hourly Salary: (AMUSE A) $16.70 Estimated Number of Work Hours per Week: 5 (Part time) Position Start Date: 2026-10-05 Position End Date: 2027-04-30 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-05 This position is covered by the AMUSE collective agreement. McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Strathcona-Music/Audio-Visual-Assistant-C--Digital-Composition-Studios-_JR0000081224": {
      "title": "Audio Visual Assistant C (Digital Composition Studios)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-28\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Schulich School of Music - Digital Composition Studios Position Summary: Under the direction of the Digital Composition Studios (DCS) Directors, the DCS Assistant supports the daily operation of the Digital Composition Studios by maintaining studio facilities and equipment, assisting users, providing technical support, and helping with the setup and delivery of classes, workshops, projects, and events. The role also supports equipment management, studio bookings, and basic audio/video production activities. Key Responsibilities: Help keep the five Digital Composition Studios ready for use by checking rooms, equipment, and work areas, and reporting any issues. Assist with studio bookings, room access, and equipment loans in accordance with established DCS procedures. Prepare rooms and equipment for classes, workshops, rehearsals, and student projects. Set up and provide first-line troubleshooting for microphones, audio interfaces, speakers, cameras, projection systems, and other studio or event equipment. Assist students, instructors, and teaching assistants with the use of DCS software, recording equipment, and established studio workflows. Prepare and deliver introductory workshops, software demonstrations, tutorials, and user instructions. Assist with concert setup and technical support, including sound checks, equipment operation, and equipment teardown following events. Support the audio and video documentation of concerts, workshops, and other selected DCS activities, and organize the resulting files. Maintain the production equipment inventory by preparing, transporting, signing out, checking in, and storing equipment, and reporting faults and repair needs. Coordinate with the DCS Directors regarding daily studio operations. Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities Demonstrated familiarity and experience with advanced Western art music (including notation) and contemporary music practice and composition that includes technology.  Advanced knowledge of audio/video or graphic software is required. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level # 4 (qualifier) on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: High School Diploma 2 Years Related Experience Hourly Salary: (AMUSE C) $20.00 Estimated Number of Work Hours per Week: 20 (Part time) Position Start Date: 2026-10-01 Position End Date: 2026-12-23 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-05 This position is covered by the AMUSE collective agreement. McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Bronfman-Building/Faculty-Position-in-Information-Systems---Ranked-Contract-Academic-Staff--CAS---Desautels-Faculty-of-Management--McGill-University_JR0000080822": {
      "title": "Faculty Position in Information Systems - Ranked Contract Academic Staff (CAS), Desautels Faculty of Management, McGill University",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-28\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nFull time\n\n​ Please refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Faculty Position in Information Systems Ranked Contract Academic Staff (CAS) Desautels Faculty of Management McGill University Position Summary McGill’s Desautels Faculty of Management invites applications for one Ranked Contractual Academic Staff (CAS) position in the Information Systems (IS) Area. Note that this is not a tenure-track position. The start date for the appointment will be January 1 2027. With the exception of Ranked CAS who are appointed at the rank of Professor, a Ranked CAS is appointed to an initial term of one, two or three years; the contract is renewable. The position is open to candidates with a PhD in Information Systems or a related field, preferably with 5 years of teaching experience. We have a particular interest in candidates whose teaching connects to the Faculty priority area of Analytics, AI & Advanced Digital Technologies. Successful candidates should have the ability to teach courses in the areas of Analytics, AI, and advanced digital technologies, and contribute to the administration of the Areas and Faculty. The largest share of the teaching load will be in the Master of Management in Analytics program, with the remaining sections allocated across other programs, such as Master of Business Administration and Bachelor of Commerce, depending on teaching needs in a given academic year. This position requires teaching up to 8 course sections per academic year. We have a preference for candidates with a teaching portfolio and an established record in teaching AI and machine learning related courses at Master’s level. McGill University is an English-language university where teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing; but knowledge of French or a willingness to learn French is desirable. McGill University McGill is a world-renowned institution of higher learning with a strong emphasis on scholarly activities and groundbreaking research across various disciplines and is consistently ranked as one of the top universities, both nationally and internationally. Located in downtown Montreal, McGill’s Desautels Faculty of Management brings together a diverse community of scholars whose expertise contributes to a dynamic and collaborative academic environment. The Faculty offers a personalized and experiential approach to education, emphasizing responsible management by applying interdisciplinary, integrated and globally minded perspectives to teaching, research and partnerships. Faculty members across  Areas  conduct research using a wide range of theoretical and methodological approaches and enjoy a high degree of independence in their research and teaching. The Faculty places strong emphasis on research quality, innovation, and impact, with opportunities to engage in various  research centres and institutes .  Montreal is a vibrant, multilingual city that blends rich history with modern innovation, making it an ideal place to live and work. With its safe neighborhoods, affordable housing, excellent public transit and year-round activities, it’s a welcoming environment for individuals and families. From world-class culture and cuisine to thriving industries and diverse communities, Montreal offers a dynamic lifestyle that’s both enriching and accessible. Discover Montreal       Best cities to study in the world      Desautels Faculty of Management Compensation levels are competitive and commensurate with experience. Application process and deadline These materials must be submitted through Workday (all in the same section as you submit your CV) : Cover letter Curriculum vitae Evidence of teaching effectiveness We are not requesting reference letters at this point; reference check is a secondary step which may follow.   Any questions about the position or receipt of materials should be addressed to the chair of the hiring committee, Professor Warut Khern-am-nuai, at warut.khern-am-nuai@mcgill.ca . All application materials should be submitted by October 31, 2026 ; applications after the deadline may be considered. The hiring process including the review of application materials, interviews and campus visits with short-listed candidates will be ongoing until the position is filled or the search is terminated. Short-listed candidates invited to meet with members of the Faculty should expect to interview, if health and safety standards allow it, in-person in Montreal. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based. The cover letter in the application may be addressed to: Professor Animesh Desautels Faculty of Management McGill University 1001 Sherbrooke Street West Montreal, Quebec, Canada H3A 1G5 McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/James-Admin-Bldg/Assistant-Vice-President--Research-Strategy-and-Services_JR0000081050": {
      "title": "Assistant Vice-President, Research Strategy and Services",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-28\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nFull time\n\n​ Please refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). POSITION SUMMARY The Assistant Vice-President, Research Strategy and Services, reports to the Vice-President (Research & Innovation) and is a member of the senior leadership team responsible for advancing the University's research enterprise. The incumbent provides strategic and operational leadership for the University’s research development and sponsored research services and oversees a complex portfolio supporting researchers, research teams, faculties, affiliated hospitals, and research institutes throughout the research funding lifecycle. The incumbent develops a strong culture of service, collaboration, and accountability. He/ she will establish expectations, service standards, and effective ways of working to ensure that researchers and academic units receive timely, clear, coordinated, and solutions-oriented support and that institutional requirements are translated into effective and workable processes. Working closely with the Vice-President (Research & Innovation), Deputy Vice-President, Associate Vice-Presidents, Deans, Directors, and other senior leaders, the Assistant Vice-President provides strategic advice on institutional research priorities, governance, policy, infrastructure, performance metrics, and emerging funding opportunities. The incumbent serves as a catalyst for institutional collaboration, fostering interdisciplinary partnerships and driving continuous improvement in the University's capacity to secure and successfully manage major research investments. The Assistant Vice-President represents the University and the Research & Innovation portfolio on internal and external committees, and works collaboratively with governments, funding agencies, partner institutions, foundations, industry, and other stakeholders to advance the University's research mission and strategic objectives.   PRIMARY RESPONSIBILITIES Strategic Research Development and Major Initiatives Provide strategic leadership for the identification, development, prioritization, and execution of major institutional research initiatives and funding competitions, including CFI, CERC, CFREF, NFRF and other strategic funding opportunities. Lead the development of high-value institutional funding proposals, partnership agreements, and implementation plans that position the University for success in competitive national and international funding programs. Identify opportunities to strengthen interdisciplinary research and foster collaboration across faculties, research institutes, affiliated hospitals, and external partners. Research Services Lead the University’s sponsored research services and oversee effective delivery across the full funding lifecycle, including funding opportunity identification, application review and submission, sponsored research agreements, amendments and ongoing award administration. Oversee service delivery for research software, cloud-, and advanced research computing services to researchers. Ensure responsive, consistent, and high-quality services to individual researchers, research teams, Faculties, affiliated hospitals, and research institutes. Establish clear service standards, accountabilities, decision pathways, and escalation mechanisms, and use service data and feedback to improve the researcher experience. Lead and develop high-performing teams and strengthen organizational capacity, project management, coordination, and continuous improvement across the portfolio.   Institutional Research Strategy and Planning Lead the development and implementation of institutional policies, programs, procedures, and best practices that support research excellence and strengthen the University's research environment. Lead institution-wide strategic projects related to research planning, -infrastructure, and capacity-building, including the University's Strategic Research Plan and the implementation and monitoring of related action plans. Advise the Vice-President (Research & Innovation) on institutional research priorities, emerging opportunities, policy issues, infrastructure, and other complex matters affecting the research enterprise. Prepare related briefings, reports and recommendations. Monitor developments in the provincial, national, and international research funding landscape and assess their implications for the University. External Relations and Institutional Representation Build and maintain productive relationships with federal and provincial governments, funding agencies, foundations, industry partners, research organizations, and peer institutions to advance institutional research priorities. Represent the University and the Research & Innovation portfolio on internal governance committees, external advisory bodies, and sector-wide initiatives.    The ideal candidate will possess a graduate degree and a demonstrated record of progressive senior leadership in research administration, research strategy, or research development within a large, complex organization, preferably in a university, research-intensive, or public-sector environment. They will bring deep knowledge of the Canadian research funding landscape, experience leading institutional research initiatives and partnerships, and a proven ability to navigate complexity while driving organizational effectiveness and service excellence. Success in this role will require strategic leadership, exceptional relationship-building skills, sound judgment, and the ability to lead high-performing teams while aligning people, processes, and resources in support of the University’s ambitious research priorities. Bilingualism in English and French is required.   McGill University has partnered with the leadership advisory firm Odgers for this recruitment. Applications are encouraged immediately to Michael Williams, Andrea Patrick and Krutika Hotwani at  https://careers.odgers.com/en-ca/job/32099 . This is an existing vacancy. The new AVP will assume the position in early 2027. Inquiries or accommodation requests can be directed to  mcgillavp@odgers.com . McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority."
    },
