    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/McGill-College-2001/Postdoctoral-Researcher_JR0000081624": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researcher",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-30\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). POSITION SUMMARY The Pandemic and Emergency Readiness Lab (PERL) within the Department of Global and Public Health invites applications for a Postdoctoral Researcher in Pandemic Prevention and Global Health Security to support a CIHR STEPS Team Grant focused on strengthening Canada's public health emergency response systems. This fellowship offers a unique opportunity to conduct policy-relevant research that informs national preparedness strategies and contributes to the development of innovative civilian surge capacity models. The successful candidate will be supervised by Professor Prativa Baral and will work closely with interdisciplinary researchers, policymakers, public health leaders, and community partners while contributing to high-impact research and knowledge mobilization activities. The incumbent will hold a dual status as both a student and a McGill employee, and is a member of the AMURE (Postdocs) bargaining unit. RESPONSIBILITIES The incumbent will provide technical leadership and cross-workstream integration across a CIHR STEPS Team Grant. The fellow will function as an investigator, lead key research activities, and translate findings into policy-ready outputs. They will be encouraged to apply for independent salary and research funding. Lead integration of findings across the project’s four workstreams: policy mapping, economic evaluation, participatory co-design, and qualitative research  Conduct and coordinate key research activities including stakeholder interviews (n≈50), focus groups, and contribute to national survey design and analysis  Contribute to economic evaluation workflows, including cost-minimization, cost-effectiveness, budget impact, and ROI modelling, in collaboration with health economics expert. Facilitate co-design workshops with the pan-Canadian advisory network and support knowledge mobilization with government partners  First-author peer-reviewed publications and draft policy briefs, toolkits, and implementation reports for federal and provincial audiences    The project focuses on three public health system building blocks:  Governance : Mapping and comparing civilian reserve models across Canadian provinces/territories (Québec RIUSC, Ontario Corps, Nova Scotia Guard) and international cases (Nordic countries, Global South), with focus on activation triggers, command structures, and integration with public health systems  Finance : Economic evaluation of civilian versus military surge (CAF deployments vs. NGO/civilian alternatives), including cost-minimization, cost-effectiveness, budget impact analysis, and 10–20-year ROI modelling under climate and pandemic scenarios  Workforce : Participatory co-design with a pan-Canadian advisory network and two national workshops to prototype governance, financing, and tiered workforce models, with explicit SGBA+ and equity lenses throughout  Qualitative Research & National Survey : Semi-structured interviews, regional focus groups, and a national stakeholder survey examining feasibility, acceptability, equity implications, and barriers/enablers to civilian reserve participation  Fellows will be integrated into the broader McGill global health community and encouraged to develop their own scholarly identity and independent funding applications aligned with PERL’s mission.     QUALIFICATIONS We’re seeking a rigorous, applied researcher with experience in health systems, emergency management, or public health policy — someone who can lead mixed-methods research and translate findings into real policy change.    Applicants must have: Possess a PhD (within the last 1.5-2 years) in public health, health policy, political science, emergency management, epidemiology, health economics, or a closely related field  Demonstrated experience with qualitative and/or mixed-methods research (e.g., interviews, thematic analysis, focus groups, policy analysis)  Proven ability to translate research into policy-relevant outputs, briefs, toolkits, stakeholder reports, or public engagement  Strong writing and analytical skills, with a peer-reviewed publication record in a relevant area  Experience in emergency preparedness, disaster research, civil protection, or health systems strengthening (highly desirable)  Bilingualism (English and French), strongly preferred given Québec-based co-leadership and national stakeholder engagement  Familiarity with federal-provincial governance and emergency management landscape (an asset, either in Canada or elsewhere with a similar model). The postdoctoral researcher must be resident in Québec to perform work for this position.    Hiring Unit:  Department of Global and Public Health    Number of Positions:  1     Supervisor:  Professor Prativa Baral    Total Hours Per Week:  35   Salary:  $75,000/year    Planned Start and End Date:  November 1, 2026  -  October 31, 2027   Location:  2001 McGill College Application Procedure: Interested candidates should submit (upload in Workday): A cover letter (max 2 pages) describing your research background and your specific interest in this project, as well as how your experience aligns with the civilian reserve research program  Curriculum Vitae  One writing sample (published article or manuscript in progress)  Names and contact information for two academic references    Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis until the position is filled. We thank all applicants; only those selected for an interview will be contacted. * Candidates’ eligibility to hold a Postdoctoral Research Fellowship expires 5 years after the date on which their PhD was awarded, regardless of when they are hired.  Be sure to refer to the following link for further information on eligibility criteria: http://www.mcgill.ca/gps/postdocs/fellows/registration/eligibility .   ** Any offer to non-Canadian citizens/non-permanent residents is contingent on the candidate obtaining a work permit after an offer has been made and a letter of invitation sent, see: http://www.mcgill.ca/gps/postdocs/international/workpermit   « Connaissance du français et de l'anglais: L'Université McGill est une université de langue anglaise où les tâches quotidiennes peuvent nécessiter une communication verbale et écrite en anglais.  Le niveau d’anglais requis pour ce poste a été déterminé comme étant de niveau #4 (qualificatif) sur une échelle de 0-4.»   “Knowledge of French and English:   McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level #4 on a scale of 0-4.”   In accordance with Canadian and Quebec law, the present offer is conditional upon your obtaining the appropriate work authorization from any or all of the following as applicable: Citizenship & Immigration Canada, Immigration Quebec and Human Resources & Skills Development Canada (HRSDC).  Annual Salary: $75,000.00 Hours per Week: 35 (Full time) Location: McGill College 2001 Supervisor: Assistant Professor (Research) Position Start Date: 2026-09-30 Position End Date: 2027-10-31 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-30 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Wong-Building/Course-Lecturer--CHEE384--Materials-Engineering-_JR0000081618": {
      "title": "Course Lecturer: CHEE384 (Materials Engineering)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-30\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). The Faculty is inviting applications to teach a course in Winter 2027.  Please complete the online application form and upload required documents. Applications will be ranked according to the collective agreement between McGill University and the MCLIU. Preference will be given to the qualified applicant with the most priority points. Hiring Unit: Chemical Engineering Course Title: Industrial Water pollution Control Subject Code: CHEE384 (3 credits) Term: Winter 2027 Location: TBD Schedule: TBD Teaching Qualification Requirements (TQRs): Education MSc in Chemical Engineering with a focus on Materials Engineering. Experience: Experience with traditional metal, polymer, ceramic processes (casting, molding, forming, cutting, joining techniques) as well as expertise with novel processing technologies (3D printing, laser machining, etc.), also speciality techniques as they apply to composite materials; Knowledge of structure-property relationships as they result from material processing history and their effect on chemical safety considerations; Understanding and experience with heat treatment and the resulting structure -property changes; Expertise in sustainability considerations with respect to the material engineering life cycle (i.e. capable of discussing social and environmental considerations concerning mining, processing, use phase, to end of life fate (recycling, reusing, upcyclability, effect of processing aids in recyclability) Other qualification requirement(s): Excellent communication skills (written, spoken). Hiring Unit: Chemical Engineering Course Title: Materials Engineering Subject Code: CHEE 384 Location: TBD Schedule: TBD Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-18 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/McIntyre--Medical/Teaching-Assistant--Winter-2027----Advanced-Strategies-in-Genetics---Genomics---BIOC-600_JR0000081586": {
      "title": "Teaching Assistant (Winter 2027) - Advanced Strategies in Genetics & Genomics - BIOC 600",
