    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/McIntyre--Medical/Assistant-Professor--Tenure-Track--Impact--Chairs-Emerging-Leaders-RNA-Therapeutics-Engineering---Departments-of-Biochemistry-and-Biomedical-Engineering-L000161_JR0000081675": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor (Tenure Track) Impact+ Chairs_Emerging Leaders_RNA Therapeutics Engineering - Departments of Biochemistry and Biomedical Engineering L000161",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-05\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\n​ Please refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Canada Impact+ Emerging Leaders Assistant Professor (Tenure Track) in RNA Therapeutics and Engineering Program Description The Canada Impact+ Emerging Leaders program is a one-time initiative designed to support institutions in recruiting internationally based Early Career Researchers to Canada. Emerging Leaders will receive research funding and institutional support to advance transformative research in Canada’s strategic priority areas , build partnerships across sectors and borders, and translate their research into applications and impacts that benefit Canadians and the world. The Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences (FMHS) and School of Biomedical Sciences at McGill University invite applications for a full-time tenure-track Assistant Professor position in RNA Therapeutics and Engineering in the Departments of Biochemistry and Biomedical Engineering (BME). The successful candidate will be expected to propose an independent research program demonstrating strong alignment with the program’s strategic priority of ‘Health, including biotechnology’. The Emerging Leaders Program award is valued at $100,000 per year over six years to support the Early Career Researchers’ Research Program, with the possibility of extension for a further six years. The successful candidate will be expected to begin a full-time tenure-track appointment at the rank of Assistant Professor no later than June 9, 2027 . Position Description This recruitment takes place at an exceptional moment of growth in RNA science and therapeutics at McGill. The position will leverage complementary strengths in Biochemistry, including RNA biology, gene regulation, RNA guided genome editing, genomics and molecular mechanisms, and in Biomedical Engineering, including biomaterials, RNA delivery, microfluidics, imaging and living systems engineering. It is closely aligned with DNA-to-RNA (D2R), McGill’s $165-million CFREF initiative in RNA discovery and therapeutics and will build on the momentum of McGill’s recent recruitment in RNA biology. The successful candidate will also have access to the McGill mRNA Therapeutics Platform, which supports the design, production, optimization and characterization of mRNA therapeutics, together with extensive opportunities for collaboration across McGill’s research institutes, affiliated hospitals and broader ecosystem in cancer, genomic medicine, cell-based therapeutics and precision medicine. We seek outstanding candidates with expertise and research interests in Biochemistry and Biomedical Engineering, defined broadly, who will develop a creative and high-impact research program in the fundamental and/or translational aspects of RNA therapeutics and engineering. The search is aimed toward the rapidly developing continuum connecting RNA design and therapeutics, mRNA vaccines, RNA delivery, genome engineering and next-generation cell-based therapeutics. The successful candidate will pursue an independent research program that combines molecular, biochemical, genomic, quantitative and/or engineering approaches to advance fundamental understanding and create new therapeutic technologies. Areas of interest include, but are not limited to: RNA design and engineering , including mRNA vaccines, cancer vaccines, protein-replacement therapeutics, regulatory RNA elements and programmable RNA technologies; RNA delivery and targeting , including lipid nanoparticles (LNPs), biomaterials, precision nanomedicine and other viral or non-viral delivery technologies; RNA-guided genome engineering , including CRISPR-based approaches, base editing, prime editing and related technologies; Cell-based therapeutics and immunoengineering , including engineered immune cells, stem or progenitor cells, in vivo cellular engineering and next-generation CAR-T and related therapeutic strategies; Functional genomic, single-cell or quantitative approaches used to optimize RNA therapeutics, delivery strategies, genome-engineering systems or engineered cell therapies. Synthetic biology approaches connecting RNA regulation, genome engineering and programmable cellular function; RNA bioprocessing, manufacturing, microfluidics and enabling technologies for the translation of RNA medicines. We seek investigators with a distinctive research vision who can lead a program within the continuum ranging from fundamental discovery towards RNA therapeutic innovation and whose program will provide an intellectual bridge between the complementary strengths of Biochemistry and Biomedical Engineering. Job Duties The successful candidate will be expected to: develop an innovative, independent and externally funded research program of the highest international standards in RNA therapeutics and engineering; integrate molecular, biochemical, genomic, quantitative and/or engineering approaches to address important questions in RNA biology and therapeutic innovation; establish interdisciplinary collaborations across Biochemistry, Biomedical Engineering, SBMS, D2R and relevant McGill research institutes and affiliated hospitals; contribute to and leverage McGill's expanding capabilities in RNA design, engineering, delivery, bioprocessing and therapeutic translation; successfully compete for external research funding; supervise and mentor undergraduate, graduate and postdoctoral trainees; teach undergraduate and graduate courses and contribute to curriculum development in their areas of specialization; contribute to the intellectual and academic life of the Departments, School and University; disseminate research findings through high-impact scholarly publications and other knowledge translation and exchange activities; where appropriate, pursue innovation, commercialization and partnerships with biotechnology, industry and other sectors. Qualifications and Education Requirements Only candidates who were internationally based, both working and residing outside Canada, on June 9, 2026 are eligible. Candidates must be within five years following the start date of their first research-related appointment, minus the length of any eligible delays in research. Recent graduates and postdoctoral fellows may also be eligible.   Research-related appointments are defined as positions in which the individual has autonomy to conduct research independently. Eligible leaves, including maternity, parental, medical and bereavement leave, are credited as twice the amount of time taken. Professional leaves, including training, sabbatical and administrative leave, are not credited. A PhD is required by McGill University for appointment to a tenure-track position; however, it is not a requirement of the Impact+ Emerging Leaders Program itself. Applicants must hold a PhD or equivalent doctoral degree in biochemistry, molecular or cellular biology, biomedical engineering, bioengineering, chemical biology, genomics, biophysics or another relevant discipline. Candidates should demonstrate: an outstanding record of research and scholarly achievement appropriate to career stage; a compelling and original research vision in RNA therapeutics and engineering; strong potential to establish an internationally leading and competitively funded research program; demonstrated capacity or potential for interdisciplinary collaboration; a commitment to and evidence, appropriate to career stage, of excellence in teaching, inclusive pedagogy, mentorship and supervision; interest in innovation, knowledge translation and/or partnerships with biotechnology and industry, where appropriate. Candidates should review the eligibility and selection criteria for the Impact+ Program prior to applying.     Canadians living abroad who wish to relocate to Canada are encouraged to apply.   Application Process Applications must be submitted through Workday, McGill's HR management system, using the link below. Use a personal email address when creating an account in Workday. Refer to the How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates) job aid for instructions on how to apply. Required Documents The application package should include the documents listed below and must be merged into one single PDF file not exceeding 5 MB: 1-page executive summary Cover letter (maximum 2 pages) Curriculum vitae Statement of research describing current and future research, alignment with the strategic priority area, and potential for knowledge translation, mobilization, and application of research results (maximum 2 pages) Research infrastructure needs: laboratory space, equipment (maximum 1 page) Teaching statement and dossier (maximum 1 page) Contact information for three references  Job Details Job Type: Tenure-track Rank: Assistant Professor Salary: Salary will be commensurate with qualifications and experience. Posting Period Posting date: October 5th, 2026 Closing date: November 21st, 2026 Inquiries from potential candidates are welcome and will be treated in strict confidence. Correspondence can be directed to: Sonia Nardini, sonia.nardini@mcgill.ca . Commitment to Equity and Diversity Please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University offers immigration and relocation support for selected candidates . McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing.   McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities.   At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry.   McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record.   McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, Catherine Roy accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca .   McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/MacDonald-Harrington/Workshop-Facilitator_JR0000080740-1": {
      "title": "Workshop Facilitator",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-05\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). The Peter Guo-hua Fu School of Architecture is seeking to recruit a Workshop Facilitator to support the operation of the Laser Cutting Room during peak academic periods. The incumbent will assist students with equipment use, monitor adherence to safety procedures, and help maintain a safe and organized workshop environment. Responsibilities: Open and close the Laser Cutting Room during peak demand periods, including midterm and final review periods, typically Monday to Friday from 5:00 p.m. to 9:00 p.m. Assist students with the after-hours booking system and room access procedures. Provide guidance to students on the safe and proper operation of laser cutting equipment. Monitor equipment use to ensure all safety protocols and operating procedures are followed. Troubleshoot student files, machine settings, and basic equipment issues to support successful project completion. Report technical problems, equipment malfunctions, and safety concerns to the appropriate staff. Assist with the maintenance, organization, and cleanliness of the Laser Cutting Room and its equipment. Support a safe, professional, and productive workspace for all users. Qualifications: Current undergraduate or graduate student, preferably in Architecture, Design, Engineering, or a related field. Strong interpersonal and customer service skills with the ability to assist students in a professional and approachable manner. Ability to follow and enforce workshop safety procedures and operational guidelines. Basic technical aptitude and willingness to learn the operation of workshop equipment and tools. Strong organizational skills and attention to detail. Ability to work independently and responsibly during evening shifts. Reliable, punctual, and capable of managing multiple requests in a busy environment. Ability to maintain a clean, safe, and organized workspace. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level # (qualifier) on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: High School Diploma 2 Years Related Experience Hourly Salary: (AMUSE C) $21.00 Estimated Number of Work Hours per Week: 3.39 (Part time) Position Start Date: 2026-10-15 Position End Date: 2026-12-22 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-09 This position is covered by the AMUSE collective agreement. McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/MacDonald-Harrington/Teaching-Assistant---ARCH-551-W27_JR0000081619": {
      "title": "Teaching Assistant - ARCH 551 W27",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-05\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: The Peter Guo-hua Fu School of Architecture Course Title: Urban Design and Planning Course Code: ARCH 551 Estimated Number of Positions: 1 Total Hours of Work per Term: 100 Hiring Unit: Peter Guo-hua Fu School of Architecture Course Title and Course Number: Urban Design and Planning / ARCH 551 Estimated Number of Positions: One (1) Total Hours of Work per Term: 100 Position Summary: Fundamentals of city-building in Canada relative to municipal, regional, and provincial actions used to guide urban growth and development. Contemporary urban design in major metropolitan centres as shaped by legal, political, and cultural realities. Current preoccupations in city-building: reurbanisation and adaptive reuse of infrastructure, collaborative multi-stakeholder projects, strategic initiatives, changing relationships between professional experts and grassroots actors. Introduction to specific aspects of practice: public participation and community engagement; land development and real estate; project feasibility and implementation; policy monitoring and evaluation; emergent city-building regimes.  Qualifications: The ideal T.A. is a graduate student enrolled in a post-professional or doctoral program in Urban Planning, Architecture or a related field, who has professional experience, who can demonstrate superior writing skills, and who has a good rapport with students. The bulk of the T.A.'s time will be devoted to answering students' questions and grading their work (papers and examination); he or she may also give one or more lectures.   Hourly Salary: $38.46 Position Start Date: 2027-01-04 Position End Date: 2027-04-30 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-09 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/MacDonald-Harrington/Office-Assistant-B_JR0000080734": {
      "title": "Office Assistant B",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-05\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). The Peter Guo-hua Fu School of Architecture is seeking to recruit an Office Assistant to provide portfolio tutoring and guidance to Architecture students, supporting them in showcasing their work effectively and developing strong portfolios for graduate studies, internships, and professional opportunities. Responsibilities: Provide a welcoming and supportive environment for prospective students, answering questions related to portfolio preparation, admissions processes, and program requirements. Offer one-on-one and group guidance on developing, organizing, and presenting strong portfolios. Schedule portfolio review appointments, workshops, and information sessions. Maintain records of student consultations, attendance, and portfolio review activities. Prepare and distribute workshop materials, presentations, guides, and communications. Assist with the coordination of portfolio review events, open houses, recruitment activities, and admissions-related sessions. Respond to inquiries by email, phone, and in person, directing students to appropriate resources and services as needed. Support the collection and organization of portfolio submissions and related documentation. Help track educational materials, equipment, and resources used during tutoring sessions and events. Maintain an organized, professional, and student-focused workspace. Perform other related duties as required in support of portfolio tutoring and student recruitment activities. Qualifications: Current graduate student or recent graduate in Architecture or a closely related field. Strong portfolio demonstrating excellence in architectural design and presentation. Knowledge of graduate school application processes and internship opportunities in Architecture. Experience mentoring, tutoring, teaching, or advising students is an asset. Excellent communication, interpersonal, and organizational skills. Proficiency with industry-standard design software, including Adobe Creative Suite (InDesign, Photoshop, Illustrator) and other relevant digital tools. Ability to work independently and collaboratively in a student-focused environment. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level # (qualifier) on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: High School Diploma 1 Year Related Experience Hourly Salary: (AMUSE B) $21.00 Estimated Number of Work Hours per Week: 4.25 (Part time) Monday Position Start Date: 2026-10-15 Position End Date: 2026-12-22 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-09 This position is covered by the AMUSE collective agreement. McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Sherbrooke-1010/Senior-Campus-Planner_JR0000078308": {
      "title": "Senior Campus Planner",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-05\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Position Summary: The Senior Campus Planner reports to the Associate Director, Campus Planning and Development. The incumbent will lead project planning initiatives and develop recommendations relating to feasibility, options and conceptual design development.  The incumbent will liaise with external and internal stakeholders and multiple complex groups in order to communicate, resolve issues, and negotiate consensus.  The incumbent is responsible for leading planning efforts and representing programmatic needs as well as providing planning consultation to a broad diversity of stakeholders both internal and external to the University.  The incumbent acts as a critical resource for executive-level decision-making about campus master planning, site planning and project planning initiatives.  The incumbent operates as a system-wide consultant for multiple units and University entities seeking campus planning and project development for a diverse and complex set of initiatives and programs.  The incumbent provides leadership to peer professional and academic staff. Primary Responsibilities: Provides senior professional expertise on University real estate matters, to include property acquisition and property disposal. Collaborate in the development of long-range facility plans for all academic and administrative departments.  This includes developing and overseeing feasibility studies, master plans, reports, and presentations to assist in the strategic long-range planning and facility development required. Plans and controls initiatives by identifying needs including space programming. Analyzes and prioritizes in support to the strategic plan to the University and the University Master Plan. Develops a program of requirements for specific initiatives and assist in the project initiation from a planning perspective. Responsible for achieving tasks by respecting detailed calendars and budgets. Communicates and makes presentations to Senior Management in terms of space allocation and report findings as well as negotiates with University departments on matters of space allocation, exchange or relocation. Negotiates with clients, being faculties and departments and other administrative units as well as external agencies on confirming feasibility parameters of projects and outlines program’s needs. Liaises with external clients particularly with the provincial and municipal Governments to ensure projects comply with regulations. Prepares all reports detailing the processes and recommendations for the projects. Develops and presents business cases and feasibility studies including budget for approval from Senior Management. Prepares and negotiates submissions to external agencies. Establishes and develops partnership with other institutions and external agencies and represents the University in official meetings. Consults with internal and external stakeholders and reviews proposed changes associated with landscape, circulation, land use, wayfinding and infrastructure. Leads development of site plans and business cases, including facility sitting, orientation, height and massing and circulation studies. Leads project planning initiatives to improve quality of the campus environment. Evaluates development proposals for consistency with the University Master Plan. Leads site selection and land use analysis for Master Planning efforts for all campuses. Develops and presents planning documents, reports, presentations and studies. Collaborates with external parties or jurisdictions when plans are intertwined, coordinates review and interaction with these entities for issues that have wide significance. Performs research and collects data from multiple sources to address planning needs. Gather various sources of information, analyzes, and consistently reports in different formats (verbal, written) to variety of audiences and levels (internal and external to the University) that are involved in decision-making processes. Ensures quality graphic production capacity, including overseeing others’ work, using software (Adobe Design Suite, Illustrator, Photoshop and InDesign) to achieve and maintain consistent graphic standards in all planning material. Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities : Demonstrated experience in all phases of development project planning and proven ability to manage architects and engineers in the planning phase.  Experience managing initiatives in higher education setting is an asset.  Academic planning and scenario development experience. Extensive knowledge of and experience in macro project financial analysis and interpretation.  Professional certification as architect and/or urbanist is required.  Strong analytical background and project development skills.  Ability to manage multiple development initiatives and prioritize appropriately.  Ability to work in a PC environment and advanced level of proficiency in Microsoft Word, Excel and PowerPoint.  Exceptional communication skills with internal stakeholders at all levels of the organization, as well with external stakeholders and authorities. Experience in higher education in a related field, an asset. Strong writing and presentation skills and oral fluency in English and French essential mandatory. Knowledge of French and English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level 4 on a scale of 0-4. Minimum Education and Experience: Bachelor's Degree 5 Years Related Experience / Annual Salary: (MPEX Grade 06) $82,280.00 - $102,850.00 - $123,420.00 Job Profile: MPEX-LOG3C - Planning & Design - Senior Professional/Manager Hours per Week: 33.75 (Full time) Supervisor: Associate Director Planning Position End Date (If applicable): 2028-08-04 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-16 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Bronfman-Building/Teaching-Assistant---MGCR-222_JR0000077854": {
      "title": "Teaching Assistant - MGCR 222",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-05\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Desautels Course Title: Introduction to Organizational Behaviour Course Code: MGCR 222 Estimated Number of Positions: 1 Total Hours of Work per Term: 0 Hiring Unit: Desautels Faculty of Management Course Title and Course Number: Introduction to Organizational Behaviour MGCR 222 Estimated Number of Positions: 1 Total Hours of Work per Term: 123 Position Summary: Responsibilities   include supervision of grading of homework assignments, quizzes, and midterm exam, as well as holding weekly office hours to answer questions from students about the course contents and assignments. Qualifications: Must be McGill graduate student with appropriate course background. Please note that given the planned resumption of most in-person teaching activities in the Fall, Teaching Assistants will be required on campus to perform their tasks in person.  Should government restrictions prohibit the resumption of in-person teaching activities, courses will revert to remote teaching. In the event of such a change, Teaching Assistants will be advised as soon as possible.  Teaching Assistants may be required to perform tasks suited to a remote/online teaching environment using a virtual learning platform [e.g., Zoom, WebEx] and the McGill myCourses learning management system. Hourly Salary: $37.34 Position Start Date: 2026-08-30 Position End Date: 2026-12-12 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-07 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Adams-Building/Teaching-Assistants-EPSC-for-Winter-2027_JR0000081571-1": {
      "title": "Teaching Assistants EPSC for Winter 2027",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-05\nPosted: Posted Today\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Teaching Assistants EPSC for Winter 2027   Hiring Unit: Earth and Planetary Sciences   Course Title and Course Number: EPSC 181 Environmental Geology EPSC 186 Astrobiology   EPSC 201 Understanding Planet Earth     EPSC 212 Introductory Petrology  EPSC 225 Properties of Minerals EPSC 303 Structural Geology EPSC 355 Sedimentary Geology EPSC 547 Modelling Geochemical Processes EPSC 549 Hydrogeology  ESYS 104 The Earth System  ESYS 200 Earth-System Interactions    Estimated Number of Positions: 35   Total Hours of Work per Term: 80   Hourly Salary: According to the collective agreement   Position Start Date: 2026-01-04   Position End Date: 2026-04-16   Application Deadline: 2026-10-19   Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University You must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Must be registered in a graduate program at McGill University. Priority will be given to students enrolled in a graduate program in Geography.   McGill University is committed to equity in employment. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing.   Use a personal email when creating an account to submit your application. Do not use @mail.mcgill.ca or @mcgill.ca email accounts when you apply.       EPSC 181 Environmental Geology Key Responsibilities Under the general supervision of the course instructor: Performs grading and maintains grading records. Assists with tutoring. Performs related duties. Must attend lectures in person as indicated. Qualifications B.Sc. degree in Earth Sciences or closely related field. Enrollment in the Earth & Planetary Sciences M.Sc. or Ph.D. graduate program. Knowledge of the course subject and material. Must be organized and approachable. Ability to work effectively with a diverse range of people. English, spoken and written. Ability to read French is desired.   EPSC 186 Astrobiology Key Responsibilities Under the general supervision of the course instructor: Prepares course material (including laboratory preparation, if applicable). Performs grading and maintains grading records. Assists with tutoring. Performs related duties. Must be available to attend all lectures in person. Qualifications B.Sc. degree. Enrollment in the Earth & Planetary Sciences M.Sc. or Ph.D. graduate program. Knowledge of the course subject and material. Must be organized and approachable. Ability to work effectively with a diverse range of people. English, spoken and written. Ability to read French is desired.   EPSC 201 Understanding Planet Earth Key Responsibilities Under the general supervision of the course instructor: Prepares course material (including laboratory preparation, if applicable). Performs grading and maintains grading records. Assists with tutoring. Performs related duties. Qualifications B.Sc. degree. Enrollment in the Earth & Planetary Sciences M.Sc. or Ph.D. graduate program. Knowledge of the course subject and material. Must be organized and approachable. Ability to work effectively with a diverse range of people. English, spoken and written. Ability to read French is desired.  EPSC 212 Introductory Petrology Key Responsibilities Under the general supervision of the course instructor: Prepares course material (including laboratory preparation, if applicable). Is present during the lab to aid students. Performs grading. Assists with tutoring. Performs related duties Qualifications B.Sc. degree. Enrollment in the Earth & Planetary Sciences M.Sc. or Ph.D. graduate program. Have taken an introductory petrology course or demonstrate equivalent knowledge. Must be familiar with metamorphic and/or igneous petrology and be able to recognize the common rock-forming minerals in hand specimens and thin sections. Must be able to work with a petrographic microscope. Must be organized and approachable. Ability to work effectively with a diverse range of people. English, spoken and written. Ability to read French is desired. EPSC 225 Properties of Minerals Key Responsibilities Under the general supervision of the course instructor: Prepares course material (including laboratory preparation, if applicable). Performs grading and maintains grading records. Assists with tutoring. Performs related duties. undefined Qualifications B.Sc. degree. Enrollment in the Earth & Planetary Sciences M.Sc. or Ph.D. graduate program. Knowledge of the course subject and material. Familiarity with the properties of hand specimens of the main ore and rock-forming minerals is an asset. Must be organized and approachable. Ability to work effectively with a diverse range of people. English, spoken and written. Ability to read French is desired.   EPSC 303 Structural Geology Key Responsibilities Lead lab demonstrations. Grade and maintain grading records. Hold weekly office hours. Respond to student inquiries. Work under the general supervision of the course instructor and help with course material and laboratory preparation. Perform related duties as assigned. Participate in short field trips within the Montreal area, if applicable. Qualifications B.Sc. degree. Enrollment in the Earth & Planetary Sciences M.Sc. or Ph.D. graduate program. Knowledge of the course subject and material. Must be organized and approachable Ability to work effectively with a diverse range of people. Proficiency in English, both spoken and written Ability to read French is desired.   EPSC 355 Sedimentary Geology Key Responsibilities Under the general supervision of the course instructor: Prepares and assists with laboratory classes, including organizing specimens. Grades laboratory reports and exams. Hold office hours and/or tutorials.  Qualifications B.Sc. degree. Enrollment in the Earth & Planetary Sciences M.Sc. or Ph.D. graduate program. Knowledge of the course subject and material. Must be organized and approachable. Ability to work effectively with a diverse range of people. English, spoken and written. Ability to read French is desired. EPSC 547 Modelling Geochemical Processes Key Responsibilities Under the general supervision of the course instructor: Prepares course material (including laboratory preparation, if applicable). Performs grading and maintains grading records. Assists with tutoring. Performs related duties. Qualifications B.Sc. degree. Enrollment in the Earth & Planetary Sciences M.Sc. or Ph.D. graduate program. Knowledge of the course subject and material. Must be proficient in Matlab programming. Must be organized and approachable. Ability to work effectively with a diverse range of people. English, spoken and written. Ability to read French is desired. EPSC 549 Hydrogeology Key Responsibilities Under the general supervision of the course instructor: Prepares course material (including laboratory preparation, if applicable). Performs grading and maintains grading records. Assists with tutoring. Performs related duties Qualifications B.Sc. degree. Enrollment in the Earth & Planetary Sciences M.Sc. or Ph.D. graduate program. Have taken a hydrogeology or groundwater engineering course or demonstrate equivalent knowledge. Hydrogeology research experience preferred. Must be organized and approachable. Ability to work effectively with a diverse range of people. English, spoken and written. Ability to read French is desired. ESYS 104 The Earth System Key Responsibilities Under the general supervision of the course instructor: Prepares course material (including laboratory preparation if applicable). Performs grading and maintains grading records Assists with tutoring. Performs related duties. undefined Qualifications B.Sc. degree. Enrollment in Earth & Planetary Sciences, Atmospheric and Oceanic Sciences, or Geography M.Sc. or Ph.D. graduate program. Knowledge of the course subject and material. Must be organized and approachable. Ability to work effectively with a diverse range of people. English, spoken and written. Ability to read French is desired. ESYS 200 Earth-System Interactions Key Responsibilities Under the general supervision of the course instructor: Prepares course material (including laboratory preparation, if applicable). Performs grading and maintains grading records. Assists with tutoring. Performs related duties. Qualifications B.Sc. degree. Enrollment in the Earth & Planetary Sciences M.Sc. or Ph.D. graduate program. Knowledge of the course subject and material. Must be organized and approachable. Ability to work effectively with a diverse range of people. English, spoken and written. Ability to read French is desired. McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/MacDonald-Harrington/IT-Assistant-B_JR0000080731": {
      "title": "IT Assistant B",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-05\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). The Peter Guo-hua Fu School of Architecture is seeking a highly organized IT Assistant to help the School media center technician and the students in the printing of course deliverables. Responsibilities: Opening and closing the printing room after hours in the busy periods during midterms and finals, (workdays after 5 PM and Weekends on demand for 2-4 weeks depending on the student deadlines). Assisting students with the afterhours requests through a schedule. Aiding students with the use of printers, verifying that the printers are operating correctly, that the students are following the proper procedure, and are not overutilizing the materials. Troubleshooting student files and printer errors. Helping with the maintenance and cleanliness of the printing room. Qualifications: Current student at The Peter Guo-hua Fu School of Architecture at McGill University Experience providing customer service and technical support in a student, academic, or office environment. Familiarity with Adobe Acrobat and basic file preparation for printing and scanning (PDF, Adobe Creative Suite, Microsoft Office, etc.). Ability to troubleshoot printer, software, and file-related issues with minimal supervision. Ability to work independently during evenings and weekends. Reliable, organized, and detail-oriented. Ability to follow procedures, enforce printing room policies, and monitor material usage. Ability to lift and move boxes of paper and printing supplies when required. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level # (qualifier) on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: High School Diploma 1 Year Related Experience Hourly Salary: (AMUSE B) $21.00 Estimated Number of Work Hours per Week: 3.82 (Part time) Monday Position Start Date: 2026-10-15 Position End Date: 2026-12-22 Deadline to Apply: This position is covered by the AMUSE collective agreement. McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
